\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republicans seize on auto workers strike as opportunity to recapture the White House\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Tait in Washington \par \uc2 1,144 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2023\par 13:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans believe they stand to gain from the dispute but there is a divide between sympathy and disdain for the workers\par \par \uc2 A strike pitting a resurgent trade union against the US\u8217\'20\'19s three biggest carmakers has exposed key differences in labour relations among Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 even while animating their assault on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s self-styled \u8220\'20\'1CBidenomics\u8221\'20\'1D policies.\par \par \uc2 Led by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the former president and 2024 party frontrunner, Republican hopefuls have seized on the stoppage by 13,000 \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW) \uc2 members at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  production facilities in Missouri, Michigan and Ohio to highlight rumbling economic discontent as a catalyst to recapturing the White House. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is demanding a 40% pay raise, shorter hours and better pensions for its members \u8211\'20\'13 and is threatening to spread the strike to other plants if its terms are not met.\par \par \uc2 Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 who have attacked unions for decades \u8211\'20\'13 believe they stand to gain from a dispute that could seriously test Biden\u8217\'20\'19s claim to be the most pro-labour president in US history. Yet underlying their conviction is a divide between those professing sympathy for the strikers\u8217\'20\'19 grievances and others who have invoked \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  to suggest that they deserve to be sacked.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Biden has clearly sided with the union\u8217\'20\'19s demands and urged management to share more of their companies\u8217\'20\'19 record profits with the workforce.\par \par \uc2 Bullishly heading the Republican charge is Trump, who has made clear his intention to woo \uc2 UAW\uc2  members by scheduling a keynote speech in Detroit next week \u8211\'20\'13 the symbolic heartland of the US motor industry and near the site of the strike-hit Ford plant in Dearborn.\par \par \uc2 He will address 500 workers and union members from a range of industries \u8211\'20\'13 including carworkers \u8211\'20\'13 in a bid to reclaim the level of working-class support that enabled him to carry Michigan in his 2016 presidential victory over \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , before losing the state in his 2020 defeat to Biden.\par \par \uc2 Next Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s event will be timed to coincide with the second Republican primary debate in California, which he is deliberately skipping to shield his presumed status as the party\u8217\'20\'19s anointed nominee-in-waiting.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s something in this strike for Trump, and maybe one or two of the other Republicans,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Larry Sabato\uc2 , the director of the centre for politics at the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a political opportunity for Trump and he recognises it. Whatever you think of Trump, his political instincts aren\u8217\'20\'19t bad.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe needs something in the neighbourhood of 37% to 40% of union voters to win back Michigan, so he will come in and give a speech promising that he is their best, best friend and will be to their dying day \u8211\'20\'13 even though they will live longer than he will.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe did pretty well in 2016 in Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin, partly because a lot of union members didn\u8217\'20\'19t like \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  but also because Trump\u8217\'20\'19s swagger really seemed to appeal to many union members \u8211\'20\'13 they were attracted to that style of leadership.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain, the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s president, rebuffed Trump\u8217\'20\'19s ostensibly union-friendly posture, saying in an issued statement: \u8220\'20\'1CEvery fibre of our union is being poured into fighting the billionaire class and an economy that enriches people like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the expense of workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Crucially, however, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has yet to endorse Biden\u8217\'20\'19s re-election bid, despite the president\u8217\'20\'19s labour-friendly posture and his vocal support for the union\u8217\'20\'19s demands \u8211\'20\'13 giving Republicans leeway to make inroads with its members.\par \par \uc2 Trump is not alone among the GOP field in displaying sympathy for the \uc2 UAW\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 or at least its grassroots membership.\par \par \uc2 Describing himself as \u8220\'20\'1Camong the most pro-labour Republicans in the US senate\u8221\'20\'1D, JD Vance, the senator from Ohio \u8211\'20\'13 a traditionally union-friendly state \u8211\'20\'13 said American carworkers had \u8220\'20\'1Cgotten the short end of the stick\u8221\'20\'1D and declared: \u8220\'20\'1CI support the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s demand for higher wages.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But he qualified his support with an attack on the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cpremature transition to electric vehicles\u8221\'20\'1D, calling it a \u8220\'20\'1C6,000lb elephant in the room\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , the rightwing populist presidential candidate and biotechnology entrepreneur, avoided choosing a side and said the union should direct its anger at the White House. \u8220\'20\'1CI empathise with workers who have seen wages not go up nearly at the same rate as prices have gone up,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe people they should be really protesting against is the current administration that has given us the economic policies of inflation without wage growth to go along with it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Contrasting with efforts to curry favour with the strikers are the hardline attitudes expressed by Tim Scott and Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 Scott, a senator for South Carolina, cited \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s firing of striking air traffic controllers in 1981 as a model.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  gave us a great example when federal employees decided they were going to strike,\u8221\'20\'1D Scott said at a campaign event in Iowa. \u8220\'20\'1CHe said, \u8216\'20\'18You strike, you\u8217\'20\'19re fired.\u8217\'20\'19 Simple concept to me, to the extent that we can use that once again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In separate remarks, he accused the \uc2 UAW\uc2  of fighting for \u8220\'20\'1Cmore pay and fewer days on the job. It\u8217\'20\'19s a disconnect from work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Analysts said Scott\u8217\'20\'19s comments were motivated by a track record of known anti-union sentiment \u8211\'20\'13 illustrated by his sponsorship of a recent bill in the senate that critics say would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scott.senate.gov/media-center/press-releases/senator-scott-congressman-allen-colleagues-introduce-landmark-legislation-to-protect-workers-and-support-small-business-owners"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 curtail workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights}}}\uc2  and a desire to attract business funds to finance his presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 Haley, the US ambassador to the United Nations during Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidency who has repeatedly invoked \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  on the presidential campaign trail, appeared to identify with the former British prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s famously uncompromising approach to striking trade unionists, boasting of being \u8220\'20\'1Ca union buster\u8221\'20\'1D while governor of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you have the most pro-union president and he touts that he is emboldening the unions, this is what you get,\u8221\'20\'1D she told Fox News. \u8220\'20\'1CThe union is asking for a 40% raise, the companies have come back with a 20% raise \u8211\'20\'13 I think any of the taxpayers would love to have a 20% raise and think that\u8217\'20\'19s great.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Samantha Sanders, the director of government affairs and advocacy at the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , a Washington-based thinktank, suggested Republican interventions on the strike were driven by opportunism.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know what is the decision-making on their campaigns,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAll I can say is, what is your track record? What have you done for workers? Have we reason to believe they would follow on these messages of support some of them express? I have not seen anything backed up by action while they were in office.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230922ej9m001pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
