\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US autoworkers on strike over wages: \u8216\'20\'18You give your life but aren\u8217\'20\'19t taken care of\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,319 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2023\par 17:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The next generation of autoworkers faces a future with no healthcare or retirement \u8211\'20\'13 unless the union prevails\par \par \uc2 When Ryder Littlejohn started working at Ford in 1994 his starting wage was $12.45 an hour with a path to go full rate after three years at $25 an hour.\par \par \uc2 A third-generation autoworker, Littlejohn noted that adjusted for inflation, his starting rate was over $20 an hour in today\u8217\'20\'19s dollars and a full rate of $45 an hour, with pension, retiree healthcare and cost-of-living adjustment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re asking for is what we already had,\u8221\'20\'1D said Littlejohn, a \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  member and one of the 150,000 \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers to agree to strike against the US\u8217\'20\'19s three largest car companies last week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what we\u8217\'20\'19re asking for today, what I was hired at in 1994. They have the money to return to us what we\u8217\'20\'19re owed and what we were promised would be returned to us,\u8221\'20\'1D said Littlejohn, a skilled trades maintenance leader at the Buffalo, New York, Ford stamping plant.\par \par \uc2 As the strike at the big three US automakers \u8211\'20\'13 Ford, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 continues, Littlejohn and others argue that their struggle highlights a bigger fight: to restore and sustain the prosperity of the American middle class whose wealth has dissipated in recent decades amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inequality.org/great-divide/americas-auto-workers-on-strike-against-inequality-again/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 broadening wealth inequality}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re all fed up, the temps are fed up, the legacy employees such as myself, we\u8217\'20\'19re tired of watching because those temps are our children, our neighbors, our friends. You get that job like Dad or Mom, you saw Dad or Mom working hard all those years, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not quite what you expected because your paycheck is a lot smaller than your Dad or your Mom with the same bills, if not more,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 There was a time when a job in the auto industry was a pathway to a secure, middle-class future. Going back to Henry Ford, the industry helped create the industrial middle class and consumer society.\par \par \uc2 Facing increased competition and growing demand in 1914, Ford {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thehenryford.org/explore/blog/fords-five-dollar-day/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doubled}}}\uc2  pay for autoworkers to $5 a day for eight hours of work, albeit with conditions: about $20 an hour in today\u8217\'20\'19s dollars when adjusted for inflation, higher than the current autoworkers\u8217\'20\'19 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kcra.com/article/united-audo-workers-union-strike-detroit/45137558#"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 starting wage of $18 an hour }}}\uc2  or less for temporary workers.\par \par \uc2 The move helped stabilize a workforce suffering from high turnover, pushed competitors to raise wages to compete with Ford, and put more money in the pockets of workers who could, in turn, afford to buy vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The relationship between car workers and their bosses has often been fractious. In subsequent decades, autoworkers held sit-down strikes and protests to organize and formed the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  in the 1930s. The pay, benefits and collective bargaining rights fought for and won by autoworkers \u8220\'20\'1Ccreated the middle class in America\u8221\'20\'1D, Olen Ham, who participated in the 1936-37 sit-down strikes at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  in Flint, Michigan, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-uaw-middleclass-analysis/as-uaw-fades-so-does-a-path-to-u-s-prosperity-idUSTRE54U1BG20090531"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told }}}\uc2  Reuters in 2009.\par \par \uc2 As unions came under fire \u8211\'20\'13 an attack led by Ronald Reagan but also supported by lackluster commitment from successive Democrat administrations \u8211\'20\'13 that middle class was squeezed. \uc2 UAW\uc2  membership, which peaked near 1.5 million in 1979, fell to about 431,000 in 2008 and is now less than half that.\par \par \uc2 The financial crisis further undermined autoworkers\u8217\'20\'19 pay. With large parts of the industry in bankruptcy, \uc2 UAW\uc2  agreed to a series of concessions to help keep the motors running. At \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , for example, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20090206032904/http://www.gm.com/corporate/investor_information/docs/fin_data/gm07ar/download/gm07ar_full.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2007 contract agreement}}}\uc2  dropped wages and benefits for new hires to $15 an hour. The union also agreed to sacrifice job security protections, healthcare for retirees and a tiered wage regime that severely affects new hires.\par \par \uc2 Shana Shaw started working at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  in Wentzville, Missouri, in 2008 and was among the first group of second-tiered workers to be paid less and given fewer benefits than workers hired before 2007.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf things don\u8217\'20\'19t change, when I hit 30 years, and I go to retire, I will not have healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Shaw.\par \par \uc2 She recounted the numerous injuries she has suffered from working in the \uc2 GM\uc2  plant, including one that led to spinal surgery, a fractured left foot and an injured right hand. She has 16 years left before retiring, but worries about how she will cope with only a 401(k) savings plan instead of a pension to rely on.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you work for a company for 30 years, you give your life to them in a way,\u8221\'20\'1D said Shaw. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re paid for your time and your time is your most important asset and when you give it to a corporation you figure they would actually care about you enough when you\u8217\'20\'19re older and your body is broken down because you made their product, a quality product because you care about who is in those vehicles, you want them to be safe and get home to their families because it could be your mother, brother, sister, neighbor, you want to make sure it\u8217\'20\'19s the highest quality, and you aren\u8217\'20\'19t taken care of in return. It breaks my heart.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 You give your life to them in a way, and you aren\u8217\'20\'19t taken care of in return. It breaks my heart\par \par \uc2 Shana Shaw\par \par \uc2 Shaw said she worried about her children\u8217\'20\'19s futures. Most of the people she knows live paycheck to paycheck, and the high cost of living makes it hard to own a home or start a family.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEverything is so expensive, and people are working harder and harder and harder, and they\u8217\'20\'19re working longer hours. But when is it enough?\u8221\'20\'1D Shaw said. \u8220\'20\'1CI wish more people could understand this fight is not just for us, but it\u8217\'20\'19s for everybody.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shawn Fain won election as president of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  in March 2023 on a reform slate aimed at clawing back the concessions autoworkers made in the 2007-2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 As Fain has repeatedly pointed out, the US auto companies have returned to record profits \u8211\'20\'13 profits they have shared with shareholders and their CEOs.\par \par \uc2 Profits at Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  almost doubled between 2013 and 2022, totaling $250bn, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/uaw-automakers-negotiations/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Economic Policy Institute. }}}\uc2  CEO pay jumped by 40% during the same period and the companies paid out nearly $66bn in shareholder dividend payments and stock buybacks.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Ford have all characterized the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s proposals as unsustainable. \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2 , the Ford CEO, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2023/09/18/uaw-strike-union-president-downplays-biden-role-in-talks.html#:~:text=The%20automakers%20have%20said%20the,under%20the%20UAW's%20current%20demands."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  the demands would cause the company to go bankrupt in an interview with CNBC. \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 , the \uc2 General Motors\uc2  CEO, has said she is \u8220\'20\'1Cfrustrated\u8221\'20\'1D with the strike, telling CNN in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/09/15/business/mary-barra-gm-ceo-uaw-strike-interview/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent interview}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1COur team is ready to be at the table \u8230\'20\'26 and we need \uc2 UAW\uc2  leadership to get back to the table so we can get these issues resolved and get people back to work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But for now, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and the car companies remain at loggerheads. Fain has given the companies until Friday to make \u8220\'20\'1Csubstantial progress\u8221\'20\'1D on their offer or face a new wave of targeted strikes.\par \par \uc2 You should be able to make a living wage working 40 hours a week, just like our grandparents did\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou should be able to make a living wage working 40 hours a week, just like our grandparents did. We\u8217\'20\'19re not asking to get something that isn\u8217\'20\'19t normal in this country. That type of life has been squandered,\u8221\'20\'1D added Littlejohn. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to say, enough is enough. Not only us, but the entire working class of this country, has to say at some point enough is enough. They\u8217\'20\'19re not going to bestow this upon us because they\u8217\'20\'19re good-natured or they care about us. They don\u8217\'20\'19t. They only care about profit.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230920ej9k0020e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
