\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Race against time to avert biggest US autoworker strike in generations\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 745 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 September 2023\par 15:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  push for resolution with \uc2 UAW union\uc2  to prevent industry\u8217\'20\'19s biggest strike in generations\par \par \uc2 US autoworkers and the big three US vehicle makers were in a race against time on Thursday to stop the industry\u8217\'20\'19s biggest strike in generations.\par \par \uc2 With the White House pushing for a resolution, the \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW) \uc2 and the car companies still appeared {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox2detroit.com/news/uaw-big-three-continue-negotiations-ahead-of-thursday-strike-deadline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 far apart}}}\uc2  as a midnight deadline to agree to a new union contract approached.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and the car companies, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , fail to reach agreements for 150,000 workers, thousands could walk out on Friday in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://uaw.org/standup/?fbclid=IwAR195tZTFjEdQtxK2QeeyV0-qn4tqqtbI04LV4Q6oOCaKiCXTxfQPG8bej0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 planned \u8220\'20\'1Cstand up\u8221\'20\'1D strikes}}}\uc2  aimed at individual auto plants. It would be the first time workers at all three auto companies have gone on strike.\par \par \uc2 Under the planned strike strategy, autoworkers would strike suddenly at targeted, individual plants, with additional locations following to pressure the automakers to come to an agreement at the bargaining table. A rally is planned with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and Senator Bernie Sanders in Detroit on Friday to kick off the looming strikes.\par \par \uc2 A recent \uc2 Morning Consult{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pro.morningconsult.com/analysis/uaw-strike-public-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  found US adults would support the autoworkers\u8217\'20\'19 strike by a two to one margin.\par \par \uc2 The White House weighed in on the talks on Wednesday. The White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein said Joe Biden had \u8220\'20\'1Cencouraged the parties to stay at the table and to work 24/7 to get a win-win agreement that keeps \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers at the heart of our auto future\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether Biden would bring in negotiators or be more actively involved, Bernstein said \u8220\'20\'1Cthe president\u8217\'20\'19s been very much engaged\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations have been strained. At the start of talks, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, Shawn Fain, refused to participate in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/aug/21/uaw-big-three-automakers-union-contract-negotiations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 handshaking ceremony}}}\uc2  with the car company CEOs that has been a ceremonial part of negotiations for decades.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey could double our wages, not raise car prices and still make billions of dollars in profit,\u8221\'20\'1D said Fain during a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/uaw.union/videos/1019290362830555/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 live stream}}}\uc2  on Wednesday evening, in which he detailed the distance between the union\u8217\'20\'19s demands and most recent proposals from the automakers. \u8220\'20\'1CThe big three can afford to immediately give us our fair share. If they choose not to, they are choosing to strike themselves and we are not afraid to take action.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and Ford have said in statements they continue to negotiate with the union.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Ford said in an email, \u8220\'20\'1COur focus is on reaching a deal that rewards our employees, allows for the continuation of Ford\u8217\'20\'19s unique position as the most American automaker and enables Ford to invest and grow. We are developing responsible contingency plans in the case of a work stoppage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://stellantisnegotiations2023.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stellantis}}}\uc2  has also cited contingency plans in preparation for a possible strike.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve gotten to this point because workers have given up so much in the past 15 years since the bailouts and recession,\u8221\'20\'1D said Marcie Pedraza, an electrician at a Ford plant in Chicago, Illinois, for 7 years. \u8220\'20\'1CEvery contract it seemed little by little the companies just wanted to take more away and we gave more and more. We as workers are giving our labor, our blood and sweat and tears and so on, the companies just keep taking from us and not giving back what workers gave in concessions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since the 2008 economic recession, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and Ford have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/uaw-automakers-negotiations/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  tens of billions in profits, including $21bn in profit in the first six months of 2023.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  is asking for a 46% wage increase over a four-year contract, in line with recent salary increases for the CEOs of the automakers and a focus on ensuring the transition to electric vehicles includes good, union jobs. The union has noted wages for workers have stagnated, with average wages having {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/sep/12/bernie-sanders-support-united-auto-workers-strike#:~:text=The%20average%20starting%20wage%20at,30%25%20after%20adjusting%20for%20inflation."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decreased 30% }}}\uc2  over the past 20 years with inflation taken into account.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey have the money, they just don\u8217\'20\'19t want to pay their fair share or what we deserve,\u8221\'20\'1D Pedraza added. \u8220\'20\'1CNobody wants to go on strike, right, because it\u8217\'20\'19s going to hurt us too, it\u8217\'20\'19s going to impact our lives and our livelihood. Even though we do get strike pay, it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t compare to what we make when we\u8217\'20\'19re working full time. But we know, as workers, that all we have to bargain with is our labor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230914ej9e001gz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
