\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Strikes aren\u8217\'20\'19t bad for the US economy. They\u8217\'20\'19re the best thing that could happen\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 1,134 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 September 2023\par 17:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Auto workers, writers, actors, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers, Amazon workers, \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers, flight attendants \u8211\'20\'13 labor isn\u8217\'20\'19t a \u8216\'20\'18special interest\u8217\'20\'19. It\u8217\'20\'19s all of us\par \par \uc2 America is in the midst of the biggest surge in labor activity in a quarter-century.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW)\uc2 , the Writers Guild of America, the actors\u8217\'20\'19 union known as Sag-Aftra, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers, Amazon workers, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and \uc2 UPS\uc2 , flight attendants. The list goes on.\par \par \uc2 More than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nl.nytimes.com/f/a/kfJJExcff7dedv-aja0lQQ~~/AAAAAQA~-WE2CGX9Gt0UUhNycmVpY2hAYmVya2VsZXkuZWR1WAQAAAAD"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 4.1m workdays were lost}}}\uc2  to stoppages last month, according to the labor department. That\u8217\'20\'19s the most since 2000. And this was before the \uc2 UAW\uc2  struck the big three.\par \par \uc2 Some worry about the effect of all this labor activism on the US economy, and view organized labor as a \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial interest\u8221\'20\'1D demanding more than it deserves.\par \par \uc2 Rubbish. Labor activism is good for the economy in the long run. And organized labor isn\u8217\'20\'19t a special interest. It\u8217\'20\'19s the leading edge of the American workforce.\par \par \uc2 What accounts for this extraordinary moment of labor activity?\par \par \uc2 Not that workers enjoy striking. Even where unions have funds to help striking workers offset lost wages, they rarely make up even half of what\u8217\'20\'19s forgone. Large corporations whose operations are hobbled by strikes often lay off other workers, as the big three and their suppliers are now threatening to do.\par \par \uc2 The reason workers go on strike is their expectation that the longer-term gains will be worth the sacrifices.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s labor market continues to be tight, despite efforts by the Fed to slow the economy and make it harder for workers to get raises. So employers (like \uc2 UPS\uc2 ) are more inclined to give ground to avoid a prolonged strike.\par \par \uc2 But something far more basic is going on here. As I travel around the country, I hear from average working people an anger and bitterness I haven\u8217\'20\'19t heard for decades. It centers on several things.\par \par \uc2 The first is that wages have barely increased while corporate profits are in the stratosphere.\par \par \uc2 Average weekly non-supervisory wages, a measure of blue-collar earnings, were higher in 1969 (adjusted for inflation) than they are now.\par \par \uc2 The American dream of upward mobility has turned into a nightmare of falling behind. Whereas 90% of American adults born in the early 1940s were earning more than their parents by the time they reached their prime earning years, this has steadily declined. Only half of adults born in the mid-1980s are now earning more than their parents by their prime earning years.\par \par \uc2 Nearly one out of every five American workers is in a part-time job. Two-thirds are living paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, executive compensation has gone through the roof. In 1965, CEOs of America\u8217\'20\'19s largest corporations were paid, on average, 20 times the pay of average workers. Today, the ratio is over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.statista.com/statistics/261463/ceo-to-worker-compensation-ratio-of-top-firms-in-the-us/#:~:text=In%202021%2C%20it%20was%20estimated,key%20industry%20of%20their%20firm."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 398 to 1}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Not only has CEO pay exploded. So has the pay of top executives just below them. The share of corporate income devoted to compensating the five highest-paid executives of large corporations ballooned from an average of 5% in 1993 to more than 15% today.\par \par \uc2 Corporate apologists claim CEOs and other top executives are worth these staggering sums because their corporations have performed so well. They compare star CEOs to star baseball players or movie stars.\par \par \uc2 But most CEOs have simply ridden the stock market wave. Even if a company\u8217\'20\'19s CEO had done nothing but play online solitaire, the company\u8217\'20\'19s stock price would have soared.\par \par \uc2 Stock buybacks have also soared \u8211\'20\'13 a huge subsidy to investors that further tips the scales against working people. The richest 1% of Americans owns about half the value of all shares of stock. The richest 10%, over 90%.\par \par \uc2 Why don\u8217\'20\'19t corporations devote more of their income to research and development, or to higher wages and benefits for average workers? In a word, greed.\par \par \uc2 Small wonder that unions are more popular than they\u8217\'20\'19ve been in a generation. A Gallup poll published in August found that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nl.nytimes.com/f/a/wIVNQzMdVnyhHG46mu6jzg~~/AAAAAQA~-WE2CGX9Gt0UUhNycmVpY2hAYmVya2VsZXkuZWR1WAQAAAAD"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 67% of Americans approve}}}\uc2  of unions, the fifth straight year such support has exceeded the long-term polling average of 62%.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden has pitched himself as the most pro-union president in recent history. More surprisingly, Republican politicians are trying to curry favor with union workers as well. Both parties know that much of the working class is up for grabs in 2024.\par \par \uc2 American workers still have little to no countervailing power relative to large American corporations. Unionized workers now comprise only 6% of private-sector workforce \u8211\'20\'13 down from over a third in the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 Which is why the activism of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , the Writers Guild, Sag-Aftra, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , flight attendants, Amazon warehouse workers and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers is so important.\par \par \uc2 In a very real sense, these workers are representing all American workers. If they win, they\u8217\'20\'19ll energize other workers, even those who are not unionized. They\u8217\'20\'19ll mobilize some to form or join unions.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19ll push non-union employers to raise wages and benefits out of a fear of becoming unionized if they don\u8217\'20\'19t. They\u8217\'20\'19ll galvanize other workers to stage wildcat strikes for better pay and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 For far too long, America\u8217\'20\'19s top executives, Wall Street traders and biggest investors have siphoned off almost all the economic gains. This is unsustainable, economically and politically.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not economically sustainable because the only way businesses can sell the goods and services American workers produce is if workers have enough money to buy them. If most gains continue to go to the top, the economy will become ever more susceptible to downdrafts and crashes.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s mainstream media emphasize the feared negative effects of the current wave of strike on the US economy, forgetting that the wave of strikes in the 1930s, 1940s, and 1950s helped create the largest middle class the world had ever seen \u8211\'20\'13 the key to America\u8217\'20\'19s postwar prosperity.\par \par \uc2 Stagnant wages and widening inequality are politically unsustainable because they foster anger and bitterness that\u8217\'20\'19s easily channeled by demagogic politicians (re: Donald Trump and his enablers in the Republican party) into bigotry, paranoia, xenophobia and authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 The current wave of strikes isn\u8217\'20\'19t bad for America. It\u8217\'20\'19s good for America.\par \par \uc2 Labor is not a \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial interest\u8221\'20\'1D. It is, in a real sense, all of us.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , a former US secretary of labor, is a professor of public policy at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , and the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/saving-capitalism-for-the-many-not-the-few/9780345806222"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Saving Capitalism: For the Many, Not the Few}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-common-good-9780525436379/9780525436379"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Common Good}}}\uc2 . His newest book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-system-who-rigged-it-how-we-fix-it/9780525659044"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The System: Who Rigged It, How We Fix It}}}\uc2 , is out now. He is a Guardian US columnist. His newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230919ej9j00290\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
