\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We build those cars\u8217\'20\'19: US workers on Ford picket line demand a fair share\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Perkins in Wayne, Michigan \par \uc2 873 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2023\par 10:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Horns blare in support of strikers outside the carmakers\u8217\'20\'19 plant in Wayne, Michigan, as union members attack \u8216\'20\'18corporate greed\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Under blue skies in Wayne, Michigan, a half-hour outside Detroit, the mood was festive but defiant as hundreds of autoworkers settled in for the first weekend of picketing at the entrances to Ford\u8217\'20\'19s Michigan Assembly Plant.\par \par \uc2 Ford\u8217\'20\'19s workers were among the first to go out in a series of targeted strikes that marked the beginning of the largest industrial action taken by US car workers in over a decade.\par \par \uc2 A chorus of horns blared in support from Michigan Avenue, a busy highway running through the nation\u8217\'20\'19s automotive heartland. Strikers turned away semi-truck after semi-truck trying to deliver parts to the plant, which produces the Ranger and Bronco. \u8220\'20\'1CHell no, you\u8217\'20\'19re not coming in here, keep it moving,\u8221\'20\'1D a worker yelled.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW) \uc2 president, Shawn Fain, called the strike after failing to reach new union contracts with Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  before a midnight Thursday deadline.\par \par \uc2 The strikers\u8217\'20\'19 message: they\u8217\'20\'19re no longer accepting the automakers\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Ccorporate greed\u8221\'20\'1D. They point to the companies\u8217\'20\'19 record profits in recent years and huge stock buyback programs that are enacted as workers struggle to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 Ford\u8217\'20\'19s CEO, \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2 , briefly stopped by to meet with picketers. Several workers near retirement weren\u8217\'20\'19t particularly impressed by the gesture. He made $29m a year, they noted, while hourly workers were \u8220\'20\'1Cfighting to get money to survive after we leave here\u8221\'20\'1D, said plant worker Stu Jackson. \u8220\'20\'1CHow many years do we even have left to live after we retire? Ten years?\u8221\'20\'1D asked Jackson, who highlighted the toll factory work exacts on workers\u8217\'20\'19 bodies and health.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDid you see Farley in his tailored European suit? Wasn\u8217\'20\'19t he sharp?\u8221\'20\'1D Jackson asked. \u8220\'20\'1CHe looks like the $29m man. Those nice shoes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd look at us,\u8221\'20\'1D Jackson added with indignation, motioning to the small group dressed in jeans, T-shirts and sweatpants. \u8220\'20\'1CThis isn\u8217\'20\'19t fair.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As Fain has pointed out repeatedly, CEO pay has soared as the car companies have recovered from the 2008/2009 financial crisis. Pay for the big three companies\u8217\'20\'19 bosses jumped by 40% between 2013 and 2022. The GM boss, \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 , took home $29m in 2022. Meanwhile, auto manufacturing workers have seen their average real hourly earnings fall 19.3% since 2008, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/uaw-automakers-negotiations/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Economics Policy Institute}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Domonique Hicks, a young mother of three who lives in Detroit, said the $16.67-an-hour wage she received was not feeding her children.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re here to take back what Ford took from us,\u8221\'20\'1D Hicks said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to bargain with us so we\u8217\'20\'19re making a statement \u8211\'20\'13 if you can make millions and billions, then we deserve something. We build those cars.\u8221\'20\'1D The strike will go on for as long as Ford \u8220\'20\'1Cwants to keep their checkbook in their pocket\u8221\'20\'1D, she added.\par \par \uc2 Among other issues, the union is calling for a 40%-plus pay increase, an end to two-tier wage systems in which new hires are paid significantly less for doing the same work and the restoration of benefits cut to help save the car companies after the 2008/2009 recession drove them to bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 Auto executives expressed frustration as the strike entered its first weekend. A Ford spokesman called the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s terms \u8220\'20\'1Cunsustainable\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m extremely frustrated and disappointed. We don\u8217\'20\'19t need to be in a strike right now,\u8221\'20\'1D Barra told CNBC on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The White House is watching developments closely. On Friday Joe Biden said his team was engaged in trying to find a resolution and called on the car companies to \u8220\'20\'1Cgo further\u8221\'20\'1D in their negotiations with striking workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe companies have made some significant offers. But I believe that should go further to ensure record corporate profits mean record contracts,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CRecord corporate profits, which they have, should be shared by record contracts for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden reiterated.\par \par \uc2 Hicks said she had a message for those who oppose the strike, or worry about how it will affect the economy. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are hurting. You\u8217\'20\'19re talking about shutting down the economy? [The auto companies] are shutting down the economy because they aren\u8217\'20\'19t putting money back into it, so we\u8217\'20\'19re here to get it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow am I supposed to feed my kids?\u8221\'20\'1D Hicks asked. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re just trying to live and support our family.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even with a wage of about $24 an hour after starting at $16 nearly four years ago, plant worker Amanda Robinson says she can barely afford the payments on her car and there\u8217\'20\'19s not much left after bills at the end of the month to raise her three-year-old son.\par \par \uc2 Working in the plant is not an \u8220\'20\'1Ceasy walk in the park, sit at a desk\u8221\'20\'1D job, she said. It was grueling and took a physical toll, Robinson added, and they deserved better wages.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re showing them that we\u8217\'20\'19re not playing,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re willing to do whatever it takes. Everybody is standing behind us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230916ej9g0018h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
