\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A fight against corporate greed\u8217\'20\'19: Bernie Sanders rallies with \uc2 UAW\uc2  in Detroit\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 532 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2023\par 08:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Senator kicks off \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 largest strike against US\u8217\'20\'19s big three carmakers amid record company profits\par \par \uc2 US car workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2023/sep/15/uaw-auto-workers-strike-live-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 striking}}}\uc2  against the nation\u8217\'20\'19s three biggest automakers \u8220\'20\'1Care waging \u8230\'20\'26 a fight against the outrageous level of corporate greed\u8221\'20\'1D seen across the country, Bernie Sanders said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The liberal US senator\u8217\'20\'19s remarks came on Friday afternoon during a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c7dRVgX3ebA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rally}}}\uc2  with the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  in Detroit, Michigan, kicking off the first day of the union\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CStand Up\u8221\'20\'1D strikes against \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and Ford.\par \par \uc2 During his speech at the rally, Sanders told the crowd: \u8220\'20\'1CThe fight that you are waging here is not only about decent wages, decent benefits and decent working conditions in the automobile industry. No. The fight you are waging is a fight against the outrageous level of corporate greed and arrogance that we are seeing on the part of CEOs who think they have a right to have it all and could [not] care less about the needs of their workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He continued: \u8220\'20\'1CThe fight you are waging is to rebuild the struggling middle class of our country that was once the envy of the world.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sanders also asserted that the CEOs and stockholders of the US\u8217\'20\'19s biggest carmakers \u8220\'20\'1Cmake out like bandits\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe refuse to live in an oligarchy,\u8221\'20\'1D Sanders said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe refuse to accept a society in which so few have so much and so many have so little.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Among those who watched the Vermont senator\u8217\'20\'19s speech was Chris Sanders, a worker at the Ford plant in Dearborn, Michigan, for 10 years. Sanders, 54, told the Guardian that significant {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/13/business/economy/uaw-strike-economic-impact.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 media attention }}}\uc2  and focus on the economic effects of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/13/uaw-strike-explainer-ford-stellantis-gm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the strike}}}\uc2  on businesses and consumers misses the point.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe question that should be asked is what has 20 years of not paying our fair share cost the economy?\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we learned [anything] from the Covid-19 pandemic, we learned that putting money in the hands of real people is what keeps the economy going, because what creates jobs are not billionaires.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat creates jobs is having money in the hands of real people spending it or saving it, because they are spending it on products that create demand.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Chris Sanders noted the Ford plant had been holding job fairs and had trouble hiring because the starting wages of about $16 an hour no longer compete with other jobs. \u8220\'20\'1CNo one ever has a problem with the executives getting paid $21m to $27m, and hourly labor and benefits is less than 5% of the total cost of a vehicle,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey just always want to put it to the greedy autoworker and I\u8217\'20\'19m so damn tired of it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , it secretary of state, \uc2 Jocelyn Benson\uc2 , and lieutenant governor, Garlin Gilchrist II, gave introductory speeches at the rally before the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, Shawn Fain, brought on Sanders, who is an independent but caucuses with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s time to pick a side,\u8221\'20\'1D Fain said while introducing Sanders. \u8220\'20\'1CEither you\u8217\'20\'19re with the billionaire class or you\u8217\'20\'19re with the working class.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230916ej9f00002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
