\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  may soon strike. Every American should support them\b0\par\par\uc2 Bernie Sanders \par \uc2 1,029 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2023\par 10:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers at the big three carmakers earn less now in real dollars than they did 15 years ago \u8211\'20\'13 as their CEOs make more and more. Their fight is everyone\u8217\'20\'19s fight\par \par \uc2 In the United States today, at a time of unprecedented income and wealth inequality, weekly wages for the average American worker are actually lower than they were 50 years ago after adjusting for inflation. In other words, despite a massive increase in worker productivity, despite CEOs now making nearly 400 times more than what their employees earn, despite record-breaking corporate profits, dividends and stock buybacks, average American workers are worse off than they were 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 That morally grotesque and growing inequality is exactly what has been occurring in the automobile industry for decades. This time, however, under new union leadership, the members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW) \uc2 are fighting back. If the big three automakers (\uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 ) do not provide reasonable contracts to address longstanding inequities in the industry, there will be a strike \u8211\'20\'13 and all of us should support the strikers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  members will be fighting not only for themselves but against a corporate culture of arrogance, cruelty and selfishness causing massive and unnecessary pain for the majority of working families throughout the country. Their fight against corporate greed is our fight. Their victory will resonate all across the economy, impact millions of workers from coast to coast and help create a more just and equitable economy.\par \par \uc2 What are some of the issues that are pushing \uc2 UAW\uc2  members to strike? At the top of the list is the extraordinary level of corporate greed shown by industry leaders.\par \par \uc2 In the first half of 2023 the big three automakers made a combined $21bn in profits \u8211\'20\'13 up 80% from the same time period last year. Over the past decade, these same companies made some $250bn in profits in North America alone.\par \par \uc2 Yet last year, the big three spent $9bn \u8211\'20\'13 not to improve the lives of their workers, not to make their factories safer, but on stock buybacks and dividends to make their wealthy executives and stockholders even richer.\par \par \uc2 Further, while many of their workers are struggling to survive financially, last year the CEO of \uc2 General Motors\uc2  raked in about $29m in total compensation, the CEO of Ford approximately $21m and the CEO of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  over $25m.\par \par \uc2 Incredibly, over the last four years, CEO pay at the big three has increased by more than 40%.\par \par \uc2 While auto industry CEOs and stockholders make out like bandits, the workers who build the vehicles earn totally inadequate wages\par \par \uc2 While auto industry CEOs and stockholders make out like bandits, the workers who build the vehicles earn totally inadequate wages and, over the last several decades, have fallen further and further behind. There was once a time when a union job in the automobile industry was the gold standard for the working class of this country. Those days are long gone.\par \par \uc2 The average starting wage at the big three today is around $17 an hour \u8211\'20\'13 less than a number of non-union auto plants around the country. The top wage is $32.32 an hour. Unbelievably, over the last 20 years, the average wage for American autoworkers has decreased by 30% after adjusting for inflation. The reality is that autoworkers at the big three are earning less today than they did 15 years ago.\par \par \uc2 What the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is fighting for is not radical. It is the totally reasonable demand that autoworkers, who have made enormous financial sacrifices over the past 40 years, finally receive a fair share of the record-breaking profits their labor has generated.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean? It means that if the big three can afford to give a pay raise of more than 40% to their CEOs, they should be able to provide the same type of pay raise for the autoworkers who make their products.\par \par \uc2 And let\u8217\'20\'19s be clear. While decent wages are a key demand for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , there are other important contract changes that the union has proposed.\par \par \uc2 The union, quite appropriately, wants to get rid of the two-tier system under which newer workers earn lower wages and receive less generous benefits than others doing the same exact work. They want to end the use of \u8220\'20\'1Ctemporary workers\u8221\'20\'1D who are ruthlessly exploited and treated like second-class citizens.\par \par \uc2 They want to make sure that all autoworkers receive a decent pension plan and retiree health benefits so that they can retire with the respect and the dignity they deserve.\par \par \uc2 They want to make sure that autoworkers have the right to strike when the big three announce that they will be shutting down a plant. Over the past 20 years, the big three have shut down 65 factories and shipped tens of thousands of jobs overseas where they can pay workers starvation wages with no benefits.\par \par \uc2 The union also wants to make sure, as the industry proposes to build 10 new electric vehicle battery plants, that the workers in these plants become part of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and receive the same wages and benefits as union members.\par \par \uc2 As we transition away from fossil fuels and move toward electric vehicles in the fight to combat the climate crisis, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  wants to make sure that the green jobs of the future are well-paying, union jobs.\par \par \uc2 The CEOs of the big three and their masters on Wall Street must understand they cannot have it all. Decade after decade their greed has decimated the middle class, hollowed out communities throughout our country and caused massive economic suffering for the working class of America. These CEOs have created a destructive race to the bottom in a global quest for cheap labor and lax environmental standards.\par \par \uc2 Enough is enough! Let us stand together to put an end to corporate greed and start rebuilding our struggling middle class. Let us stand in solidarity with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and create an economy that works for all, not just the privileged few.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Bernie Sanders is a US senator and chairman of the health, education, labor and pensions committee\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230912ej9c00136\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
