\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Building Dignity: can a code of conduct stop abuse of US construction workers?\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse in Minneapolis, Minnesota \par \uc2 1,585 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2023\par 11:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Minnesota advocates hope to end exploitation of construction workers by making developers make ethical standards pledge\par \par \uc2 After putting up a new interior wall for a six-story dorm for \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2  students, Ivan Gonzalez confessed to some problems with a job that he, along with dozens of other immigrant workers, were rushing to finish.\par \par \uc2 Inside a second-floor dorm room-to-be, Gonzalez said he typically worked 10-hour days, six days a week doing drywall, and was paid $220 a day, often in cash. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no overtime pay, no benefits,\u8221\'20\'1D Gonzalez said, noting he sometimes worked 13-hour days.\par \par \uc2 Two co-workers were keen to highlight another problem: as they were leaving work one recent evening, they saw six teenagers \u8211\'20\'13 they guessed 13, 14 or 15 years old \u8211\'20\'13 scurrying out of a van and into the building to do clean-up work.\par \par \uc2 Upset by such scenes, worker advocates \u8211\'20\'13 from Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general to immigrant worker centers \u8211\'20\'13 have voiced alarm that many Twin Cities construction workers are toiling under hugely exploitative conditions.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes labor contractors disappear without paying workers the two weeks\u8217\'20\'19 pay owed them.\par \par \uc2 Like Gonzalez, many workers are misclassified as independent contractors, not as employees, and their employers cheat them and the government by not paying overtime or social security, workers\u8217\'20\'19 compensation and unemployment insurance taxes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome contractors are engaging in some of the worst behavior you can imagine,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dan McConnell, president of the Minnesota Building and Trades Council, a union group. \u8220\'20\'1CWe hear about people treated like slaves, kept in some dreary house, not able to talk with anybody. They\u8217\'20\'19re told that if they complain, they\u8217\'20\'19ll have them deported. One labor broker threatened to behead some workers\u8217\'20\'19 family members back in their home country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Eager to curb such abuses, several labor unions and a prominent workers center, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ctul.net/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Centro de Trabajadores Unidos en la Lucha}}}\uc2  (CTUL) came up with an innovative idea: they founded the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static1.squarespace.com/static/5bc77c59b2cf79afd1a8a823/t/62e937a63019962bf60f998e/1659451304793/FINAL+BDR+report+ENGLISH.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Building Dignity and Respect Standards Council}}}\uc2 , a group they want Minneapolis-area building developers to join.\par \par \uc2 By joining, developers would pledge to ensure that their contractors and subcontractors comply with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static1.squarespace.com/static/5bc77c59b2cf79afd1a8a823/t/62e97d65dc97f578714cf35d/1659469158022/BDR+Code+of+Conduct+for+Website.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a code of conduct that calls for}}}\uc2  a $20 minimum wage, paying time-and-a-half for overtime, paying workers\u8217\'20\'19 comp and unemployment insurance taxes, not misclassifying workers and never threatening workers or using child labor.\par \par \uc2 For its champions, Building Dignity is a model they hope will spread to other cities to stop abuses and lift standards for the nation\u8217\'20\'19s nearly 9 million construction workers. \u8220\'20\'1CA big advantage of this is we wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to worry about always responding case by case about wage theft,\u8221\'20\'1D said Douglas Guerra, a drywall worker and CTUL member who helped Building Dignity write an educational handbook for immigrant workers. \u8220\'20\'1CThis program would ensure that developers help prevent wage theft from happening in the future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Guerra has been victimized many times: a subcontractor once skipped out on paying him over $3,000 in wages, and some contractors, eager to misclassify him as a contractor, insisted he buy his own workers\u8217\'20\'19 comp insurance. Speaking in Spanish, Guerra said: \u8220\'20\'1CBuilding Dignity, even if it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t eradicate all abuses, would really transform things and alleviate lots of problems.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One study found that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://midwestepi.files.wordpress.com/2020/10/mepi-ilepi-costs-of-payroll-fraud-in-wi-mn-il-final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over 30,000 Minnesota construction workers (23% of the total workforce) are misclassified}}}\uc2  as independent contractors and paid off the books. The study said misclassified construction workers earn $29,700 less a year (36% less) in wages and benefits than they should. Moreover, the study found that Minnesota loses $136m annually in tax revenues due to construction payroll fraud, including $65m in income taxes and $58m in workers\u8217\'20\'19 compensation premiums.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMisclassification is a very serious problem when it comes to construction,\u8221\'20\'1D said Keith Ellison, Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of these companies are pushing the costs and risks on to individual workers and reaping the financial benefits for themselves.\u8221\'20\'1D By misclassifying workers, employers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/misclassification-the-abc-test-and-employee-status-the-california-experience-and-its-relevance-to-current-policy-debates/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 can cut costs per worker by 30%}}}\uc2  by not paying overtime or social security and Medicare taxes or unemployment insurance and workers\u8217\'20\'19 comp premiums.\par \par \uc2 Union officials say that in the Twin Cities, non-union contractors do half the multi-family residential construction and often pay less than half what unionized contractors pay. Most non-union contractors used to be law-abiding, union officials say, but now most systematically break wage-and-hour laws.\par \par \uc2 Ellison and local prosecutors have been bringing more wage theft and misclassification cases against construction contractors and labor brokers, but worker advocates say the law-breaking continues unabated. One labor broker was recently indicted for fraud, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://finance-commerce.com/2023/07/framing-contractor-charged-with-insurance-fraud/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for underestimating its payroll by $3.1m}}}\uc2  in order to illegally cut its workers\u8217\'20\'19 comp premiums by $340,000, while a second broker was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox9.com/news/construction-company-owner-accused-of-fraud-threatening-workers-criminal-sexual-conduct"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 indicted for workers\u8217\'20\'19 comp fraud and sexually assaulting a worker.}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a game of Whac-A-Mole,\u8221\'20\'1D said Burt Johnson, general counsel for the North Central States Regional Council of Carpenters. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is always another labor broker who is willing to break the law and risk getting punished. It\u8217\'20\'19s the developers and general contractors who can stop the crimes that are committed. They get to decide whether workers will receive middle-class wages, healthcare, pensions and training or whether they will partner will labor brokers to exploit workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 2018, the Hennepin county attorney, the Minneapolis-area prosecutor, brought a case that sparked greater attention to abuses by labor brokers. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://workdaymagazine.org/contractor-ricardo-batres-charged-with-labor-trafficking-revealing-immigrant-worker-abuse/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ricardo Batres was accused of not paying overtime}}}\uc2  even though he sometimes forced people to work 70-hour weeks and was accused of forcing workers to live in overcrowded housing with no hot water. The indictment also accused him of threatening to deport and even kill workers if they complained to public authorities. When a worker severely hurt his back, Batres ordered him to tell hospital officials he wasn\u8217\'20\'19t injured on the job. Batres ultimately pleaded guilty to labor trafficking and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kare11.com/article/news/crime/man-sentenced-to-270-days-5-years-probation-in-labor-trafficking-case/89-197df081-6f5c-4789-a194-b7fd5560717d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was sentenced to 270 days in prison.}}}\par \par \uc2 CTUL talked with immigrant workers to help gather evidence against Batres. That frightful case spurred CTUL, the carpenters and two other unions to create the Building Dignity council in 2020. The council has gathered steam far more slowly than expected partly because of the pandemic, because the city focused on racial justice after George Floyd was murdered and because developers resisted joining Building Dignity. In June 2022, CTUL sponsored a protest march by construction workers and their allies that sought to pressure three developers \u8211\'20\'13 Solhem, Yellow Tree and United Properties \u8211\'20\'13 to join Building Dignity, with CTUL asserting those developers had used law-breaking labor brokers. So far, no developers have joined.\par \par \uc2 Adam Hanson, president of Minnesota Associated Builders and Contractors, voiced developers\u8217\'20\'19 concerns about Building Dignity and the groups that founded it. \u8220\'20\'1CUnfortunately, we have heard about the questionable pressure tactics and campaigns they have used against quality, local construction companies and developers in attempts to get these businesses to join their group.\u8221\'20\'1D Hanson said. He added that Building Dignity \u8220\'20\'1Chas given very little information\u8221\'20\'1D to developers \u8220\'20\'1Cabout the group\u8217\'20\'19s business model and structure\u8221\'20\'1D. He said developers \u8220\'20\'1Cdo not know where the money [Building Dignity] is demanding goes or how it is any different than becoming a union-signatory contractor\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Doug Mork, Building Dignity\u8217\'20\'19s executive director, said the money developers pay would help hire staff and underwrite a monitoring program to ensure contractors comply with the code of conduct. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not a union,\u8221\'20\'1D Mork said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not negotiating for higher wages or anything else the way unions do. We have a code of conduct with standards they have to abide by. Ninety-eight per cent of those standards are already the law.\u8221\'20\'1D He said developers joining the council \u8220\'20\'1Cgive us the ability to educate, monitor and enforce\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Yellow Tree and Solhem did not respond to the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 CTUL is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ctul.net/week-of-action-september-2023/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 planning another protest march}}}\uc2  on 12 September to again press Yellow Tree, Solhem and United Properties to join. \u8220\'20\'1CThis isn\u8217\'20\'19t going to flip overnight,\u8221\'20\'1D said Merle Payne, CTUL\u8217\'20\'19s co-director. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a huge systems change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asked why it has declined to join Building Dignity, United Properties told the Guardian that it \u8220\'20\'1Cis firmly committed to adhering to the highest ethical standards with integrity, honesty and good faith in all areas\u8221\'20\'1D. It added, \u8220\'20\'1CWe expect our [contractors] to uphold these same principles.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bringmethenews.com/minnesota-business/apartments-brewery-proposed-for-site-of-former-minneapolis-lumber-yard"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Solhem has proposed building up to 600 apartments}}}\uc2  at a former lumber yard in Northeast Minneapolis, but some neighborhood groups have vowed to block the project unless Solhem joins Building Dignity. Elliott Payne, the city councilman who represents that area, said Solhem\u8217\'20\'19s owner told him that unions were trying to bully him, that he ran a clean, safe operation and that he would not open his books up to a third party he did not trust.\par \par \uc2 But Payne cautioned Solhem, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t support a project where workers face abuses. I\u8217\'20\'19m hearing stories of workers getting injured on job sites and sexual harassment and assault.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ellison, Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general, said: \u8220\'20\'1CUltimately if you don\u8217\'20\'19t have something like the Building Dignity Council, you end up with a race to the bottom. We need to keep good contractors, and if you make them compete with people who are cutting corners and doing the wrong thing, they will feel pressure to do the same thing. This makes it extremely difficult for everyone, especially for fair, law-abiding contractors.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230909ej9900108\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
