\par\par \uc2 Global development\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s slavery for modern times\u8217\'20\'19: how children of 12 toil in Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s fields\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Helmore in Colorado \par \uc2 1,600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2023\par 20:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some Republican-led states are pushing to relax child labour laws but even in the farms of San Luis valley, where most are Hispanic/Latino, youngsters can work an unlimited number of hours\u8226\'20\'22 All photographs by Daniel Brenner for the \uc2 Guardian\par \par \uc2 High in Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s fertile San Luis valley, the collision between Americans\u8217\'20\'19 demand for cheap food and the reliance on cheap labour \u8211\'20\'13 including children as young as 12 \u8211\'20\'13 comes into stark relief. At nearly 2,500 metres (8,000ft) above sea level, the alluvial plain, known for vegetable production, lies far from the turmoil of Washington DC, where child labour laws have long been a flashpoint.\par \par \uc2 Farmers have grown vegetables here for generations, including for the US potato market, which supports, from cultivation to consumer, about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalpotatocouncil.org/economic-impact-report/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 714,000 jobs and $34bn (\u163\'00\'A327bn) in wages}}}\uc2 . As well as potatoes, there are carrots and lettuce \u8211\'20\'13 produce that requires a high degree of manual labour.\par \par \uc2 At rates of about $16 an hour, that labour may be from local people and continue down through generations. More likely, they are people coming from Mexico, Central America or South America on temporary work visas or as undocumented migrants. During the October harvest, they may be from the Navajo Native American reservation. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.farmworkerjustice.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/NAWS-data-fact-sheet-FINAL.docx-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overwhelming majority (78%) of the agricultural workers}}}\uc2  identify as Hispanic/Latino.\par \par \uc2 The work, especially in the lettuce fields, is arduous, requiring lines of workers to move up and down the rows, hoeing and later picking lettuce heads, often from 5am to 3pm, when the heat on this high-altitude plain between the Sangre de Cristo and San Juan mountains begins to soar.\par \par \uc2 For Jacqueline Aguilar, whose father came from Mexico as a teenager and worked his whole life in the fields until his death last year, the work is, in a sense, traditional. From the age of 12, she worked alongside him.\par \par \uc2 Now 21, she recently returned from an internship in Washington with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://stopchildlabor.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Child Labor Coalition}}}\uc2 , one of several lobbying groups fighting efforts by 10 Republican-led states to roll back a century of protection laws restricting child labour.\par \par \uc2 These include expanding permissible work hours, broadening the types of jobs child workers are permitted to do, and limiting employers\u8217\'20\'19 liability for injuries, illnesses or workplace fatalities. The laws broadly make it easier for children aged 14 to 17 to work longer and later \u8211\'20\'13 and in occupations that were previously off-limits.\par \par \uc2 However, Colorado is not one of these states. First, the Democrats hold the governorship, as well as majorities in the state house and senate. And second, it already allows children to work.\par \par \uc2 Children as young as 12 can work an unlimited number of hours in agriculture \u8211\'20\'13 so long as they do not miss school. In the potato-sorting warehouses, the minimum age is 16 because it is deemed \u8220\'20\'1Chazardous work\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, Aguilar was amazed to find she was the only person of colour in the room. She points out that the US manual labour force is now in effect \u8220\'20\'1CSpanish\u8221\'20\'1D, to use a common umbrella term.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think Republicans are in denial that white people don\u8217\'20\'19t want to work manual labour any more,\u8221\'20\'1D Aguilar says at the petrol station in Center, a farm town in the middle of the valley. \u8220\'20\'1CSo they say, \u8216\'20\'18let\u8217\'20\'19s just send in the kids.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-19-26"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2018 Government Accountability Office report}}}\uc2 , more than half of work-related fatalities for children in the US happen in the agriculture sector, where one child employee dies roughly every three days. A separate study, from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marshfieldresearch.org/nccrahs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Children\u8217\'20\'19s Center for Rural and Agricultural Health and Safety}}}\uc2 , found that 33 children are injured working on farms every day.\par \par \uc2 Outstanding investigations include the deaths of three 16-year-olds in Mississippi, Missouri and Wisconsin, including Duvan Tomas Perez, a Guatemalan boy who was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/19/guatemalan-boy-dies-mississippi-child-labor-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 killed working in a poultry plant}}}\uc2  ; Michael Schuls, from Wisconsin, who was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/michael-schuls-teen-killed-sawmill-accident-organ-donation-family-says/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 killed working at a sawmill}}}\uc2 , and Will Hampton, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kansascity.com/news/local/article276271571.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 killed working at a landfill site.}}}\uc2  But with only about 800 investigators, federal labour authorities are limited in their ability to crack down on abuses and poor conditions.\par \par \uc2 Investigations have also exposed child labour in factories supplying parts to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/investigates/special-report/usa-immigration-hyundai/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hyundai and Kia}}}\uc2  in Alabama; at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/feds-dhs-investigating-human-trafficking-children-slaughterhouses-rcna66081"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slaughterhouses and meat-processing plants}}}\uc2  in Nebraska and Minnesota; and at fast-food chains including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/12/07/1141139724/mcdonalds-child-labor-laws-violations-pittsburgh#:~:text=The%20fast%20food%20franchisee%2C%20Santonastasso,its%20restaurants%2C%20according%20to%20officials."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  in Pittsburgh and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/boston/news/dunkin-stores-child-labor-lawviolations-westford-group/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dunkin\u8217\'20\'19 Donuts}}}\uc2  in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 The investigations have resulted in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.safetynewsalert.com/news/child-labor-task-force-teen-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $6.6m in fines}}}\uc2  for employers, an 87% rise on a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The truck was on me for five minutes. I thought, \u8216\'20\'18by the time my mom gets here, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to be dead\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Mhia Cazares, child labourer\par \par \uc2 Mhia Cazares was 16 when, picking lettuce in the Colorado fields last year, a truck rolled forward and back over her, injuring her spine. She was probably spared a fatal injury because the ground was soft after rain.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf it had been dry, the driver of the lettuce machine would have crushed me,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CThe guy panicked, so it was on me for five minutes. I thought, \u8216\'20\'18by the time my mom gets here, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to be dead.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her injury kept her off school, so she lost friends, and there were rounds of visits to doctors. Then there was the legal fight for compensation. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been a long process. I\u8217\'20\'19m constantly in pain. It\u8217\'20\'19s changed my lifestyle \u8211\'20\'13 and my mental health.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She has sued her employer. This summer, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/HB23-1196"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colorado passed a law}}}\uc2  making it easier for injured minors to sue employers for child labour violations.\par \par \uc2 Mhia\u8217\'20\'19s mother, Vanessa Cazares, who also worked in Center\u8217\'20\'19s lettuce fields as a teenager, says she did not want her daughter to follow in her footsteps, but adds: \u8220\'20\'1CShe wanted the experience.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s work, it\u8217\'20\'19s money, and you\u8217\'20\'19ve got to do what you\u8217\'20\'19ve got to do to provide for your family,\u8221\'20\'1D says Cazares.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut I don\u8217\'20\'19t want them to do what we had to do. There are other opportunities out there, so go to school and get your grades.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 In the baking fields of Colorado, the conflict between child labour protection and economic need is clear. For Aguilar\u8217\'20\'19s family and friends, working in the fields has long been normal in an area with few other opportunities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn my home and in my culture, it\u8217\'20\'19s normalised to work young. We need to help our families,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be making things worse for them but I\u8217\'20\'19d like to see more people of colour advocating for what we\u8217\'20\'19re connected to. There needs to be change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A bill {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/4046"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 before Congress}}}\uc2 , the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/4046/text"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 children\u8217\'20\'19s act for responsible employment and farm safety}}}\uc2  (Care Act), aims to tighten up on loose rules on minimum age, working hours and hazardous conditions for children working on farms.\par \par \uc2 The bill was first introduced in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/111th-congress/house-bill/3564/text"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2009}}}\uc2  by Lucille Roybal-Allard, a Democrat representative for California from 1993 to January this year. It failed, as did similar efforts in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/113/bills/hr2342/BILLS-113hr2342ih.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2013}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/115th-congress/house-bill/2886"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2017}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://publicintegrity.org/inequality-poverty-opportunity/immigration/immigration-employment/child-farmworkers-labor-laws/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2019}}}\uc2 . The bill was subsequently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/31/us-congress-should-protect-child-farmworkers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reintroduced last year}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But these efforts to tighten regulations concern Cazares and others. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of families may not be financially stable. I understand they\u8217\'20\'19re worried about children, but money controls the world,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 The next generation of younger people just aren\u8217\'20\'19t prepared to do those [manual] jobs\par \par \uc2 Jeff McCullough, Spud Seller farm\par \par \uc2 For farm owners, the need for immigrant and child labour is beginning to change due to several factors. Heavy extraction from a shrinking aquifer beneath the valley has pushed up water prices, causing some farmers to switch from lettuce production to potatoes. But potato production is down, too, from about 30,000 hectares (75,000 acres) to nearer 21,000.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe only way to get into sustainability and use less water is to farm less, and that\u8217\'20\'19s hard for the community and the farmers,\u8221\'20\'1D says Jim Ehrlich, director of a Colorado potato growers organisation. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to hurt.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Demand for warehouse labour to sort potatoes is sinking, too, as farmers start using AI-trained robots. The robots try to match each potato on the conveyor belt to 100,000 stored images. Where a match fails, the potato is removed by a robotic arm, reducing the need for hired potato pickers by 25%.\par \par \uc2 As the technology improves, demand for labour will fall further, and the tradition of children working in the fields, even on summer jobs, is declining.\par \par \uc2 Jeff McCullough, of the Spud Seller farm, believes that young people can be retained in farming through the use of AI because \u8220\'20\'1Cthey understand it better\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThe next generation of younger people just aren\u8217\'20\'19t prepared to do those [manual] jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D he adds.\par \par \uc2 The complexity of Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s manual labour supply, the changing nature of agriculture and the impact of the climate crisis may mean that the area\u8217\'20\'19s use of child labour in agriculture will eventually die out. But this does not resolve the immediate dilemma for the child workers of the San Luis valley.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was only working in the fields because I needed to buy myself food and stuff for school,\u8221\'20\'1D Aguilar says. \u8220\'20\'1CThe labour force here is Spanish \u8211\'20\'13 from Mexico, Peru, Venezuela, Guatemala \u8211\'20\'13 like it is in most of the US. Without the Spanish, this world would literally stop.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut people don\u8217\'20\'19t want to accept that we work jobs no one else wants to work. It\u8217\'20\'19s another form of slavery, but one for the modern times.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230906ej96001s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
