\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Biden hails strength of economy as US adds 187,000 jobs in August\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 743 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2023\par 19:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Number of new jobs matches July\u8217\'20\'19s total, reflecting resilience of labor market even with high interest rates\par \par \uc2 The US jobs market is holding steady as interest rates sit at a 22-year high, with US employers adding 187,000 jobs in August, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics}}}\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 The number of new jobs added in August is the same as the number of new jobs in July, showing that the labor market, down to levels seen before the pandemic, is resilient even with high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 President Biden on Friday afternoon {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2023/09/01/heading-into-labor-day-weekend-biden-celebrates-new-job-growth.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 touted}}}\uc2  August\u8217\'20\'19s jobs figures, as they came in above economists\u8217\'20\'19 expectations, which was pegged closer to 170,000 new jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs we head into Labor Day, we ought to take a step back and take note of the fact that America\u8217\'20\'19s now in one of the strongest job-creating periods in our history,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. \u8220\'20\'1CSome experts said to get inflation under control, we needed higher unemployment and lower wages, but I\u8217\'20\'19ve never thought that was the problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is closely monitoring the jobs market and other indicators that point to whether the economy is slowing down and inflation is dropping toward their target level. The Fed in July raised interest rates for the 11th time in under two years, bringing rates up to a range of 5.25% to 5.5%, making mortgage rates and other loans more expensive.\par \par \uc2 Even at the beginning of this year, employers were adding as many as 517,000 jobs a month, as seen in February. But over the last few months, the number of new jobs has started to slow.\par \par \uc2 In August, the unemployment rate was 3.8%, the highest it\u8217\'20\'19s been in over a year. The unemployment rate has been holding relatively steady over the last year, reaching a record low of 3.4% in February and then coming down again during the spring and summer.\par \par \uc2 The healthcare, leisure and hospitality and social assistance industries have continued to see job growth in August, while transportation and warehousing lost a good chunk of jobs, largely because of the bankruptcy of the trucking company Yellow. About 30,000 employees from the company were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/31/yellow-trucking-firm-layoffs-bankruptcy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laid off}}}\uc2  as a result of the company\u8217\'20\'19s closing.\par \par \uc2 Other recent data has pointed to easing in the labor market, including \uc2 BLS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey (Jolts), which showed there were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/jolts.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 8.8m job openings}}}\uc2  in July, 338,000 fewer than in June. There were 1.5 job openings for every unemployed worker, according to the survey, the lowest rate since September 2021.\par \par \uc2 Payroll firm ADP also released {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://adpemploymentreport.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data}}}\uc2  that showed the number of jobs added by private employers dropped 30,000 in August, down to 177,000 jobs. \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2 , an outplacement firm, said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.challengergray.com/blog/job-cut-announcements-jump-in-august-2023-ytd-up-210/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  that US employers announced 75,151 layoffs in August, the highest level since 2020.\par \par \uc2 Fed officials have emphasized that their goal is to achieve a \u8220\'20\'1Csoft landing\u8221\'20\'1D, to bring inflation \u8211\'20\'13 which reached a peak of 9.1% last summer \u8211\'20\'13 down to 2% without dramatically affecting the jobs market. So far, the drop in inflation, which was 3.3% in July, has not had as severe of an impact on the labor market, as some had feared. But officials say there is still some way to go to bring inflation down to 2%, and the full effects of their rate hikes have yet to be felt.\par \par \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , the Fed chair, said in a closely watched {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/aug/25/fed-chair-inflation-jerome-powell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 speech}}}\uc2  on 25 August that the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s fight against inflation would continue, and officials would \u8220\'20\'1Ckeep at it until the job is done\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s job to bring inflation down to our 2% goal, and we will do so,\u8221\'20\'1D Powell said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have tightened policy significantly over the past year. Although inflation has moved down from its peak \u8211\'20\'13 a welcomed development \u8211\'20\'13 it remains too high.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Complicating the picture for the Fed is that even as interest rates remain high, consumer spending has remained robust as people keep spending on things like concerts and vacations. The impact that a new, higher level of spending will have on prices remains unclear, especially as other sectors, like the housing market, are seeing contractions because of interest rates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are navigating by the stars under cloudy skies,\u8221\'20\'1D Powell said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230901ej91001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
