\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Will \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 union-busting stifle a union rebirth in the US?\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 2,918 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2023\par 18:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has brought 100 cases against the coffee chain over anti-union activities \u8211\'20\'13 but it cannot punish the company\par \par \uc2 With {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unionelections.org/data/starbucks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 340 victories}}}\uc2  at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores across the US, the campaign to organize the coffee chain\u8217\'20\'19s workers is one of the most successful union drives in a generation. But \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 fierce union-busting campaign has badly slowed its momentum and exposed deep flaws in US labor law that threaten other promising unionization efforts.\par \par \uc2 Two years on since workers at a Buffalo Starbucks started {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/aug/28/starbucks-workers-buffalo-ny-organizing-union-first-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the first}}}\uc2  successful campaign to form a union at a company-run store, labor experts say the coffee chain\u8217\'20\'19s aggressive union-busting is shining a harsh light on the shortcomings of the National Labor Relations Act (NLRA) and how that 88-year-old law which governs unionization campaigns is proving far too weak to stop a powerful, multibillion corporation from using an arsenal of illegal tactics to stifle a highly promising union drive.\par \par \uc2 Many labor experts say the unionization campaign at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has done more than any other effort to inspire union drives, whether at Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s, \uc2 Apple\uc2  or elsewhere, but if \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  succeeds in quashing its baristas\u8217\'20\'19 organizing efforts and prevents them from ever getting a first contract, that would be a major symbolic and substantive blow to the hopes for a union rebirth in the US.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has figured out an ingenious plan to get around labor law\par \par \uc2 Former barista Jaz Brisack\par \par \uc2 Even strong union supporters admit that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Cunion avoidance\u8221\'20\'1D tactics have severely cut into the union\u8217\'20\'19s momentum and win rate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has figured out an ingenious plan to get around labor law, which is: break so many labor laws so fast that the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  simply can\u8217\'20\'19t keep up in enforcing the law,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jaz Brisack, a fired barista who worked at the first company-run \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the Elmwood Avenue store in Buffalo \u8211\'20\'13 where workers voted in favor of unionizing.\par \par \uc2 The regional offices of the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) have brought 100 separate cases against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 an extraordinarily high number \u8211\'20\'13 which together allege more than 1,000 illegal actions, many of them in retaliation against workers for unionizing: from closing stores because they had unionized to reducing workers\u8217\'20\'19 hours after their stores unionized. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has also filed an unusual nationwide complaint accusing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  of refusing to bargain at 163 unionized stores across 28 states.\par \par \uc2 All told, rulings by various judges and the five-person labor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/news-story/updated-nlrb-region-7-detroit-wins-injunction-requiring-starbucks-to"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 board have ordered reinstatement}}}\uc2  of 28 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers they found to have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/news-story/nlrb-region-15-wins-injunction-requiring-starbucks-to-rehire-seven"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 been illegally fired in retaliation for union activity}}}\uc2 . Dozens more pro-union baristas are awaiting rulings about whether they, too, were fired illegally \u8211\'20\'13 the NLRA prohibits employers from retaliating against workers for backing a union. Their union, Starbucks Workers United, asserts that nearly 200 workers have been fired in retaliation for union activity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had not engaged in this ferocious, unlawful campaign, they would have 3,000 unionized stores by now, not 300,\u8221\'20\'1D said John Logan, a professor of labor studies at \uc2 San Francisco State University\uc2  and an expert on corporations\u8217\'20\'19 anti-union strategies. The number of unionization petitions filed by \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers has plummeted from 71 a month in March 2022 to around a dozen a month today.\par \par \uc2 Logan said the NLRA aims to let workers freely choose whether they want a union to represent them. \u8220\'20\'1CThe problem,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cis companies like \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  have turned it into a choice by the companies, not by the workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 former CEO, \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , testified {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/mar/29/starbucks-howard-schultz-faces-bernie-sanders-at-union-busting-hearing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 before a Senate committee }}}\uc2  in March, he asserted that the company had not broken the law even once in battling against the union. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  continues to maintain that position, asserting that any pro-union worker who was fired was not dismissed for union activity, but for violating company rules, such as arriving late to work.\par \par \uc2 Labor leaders often complain that the NLRA\u8217\'20\'19s weaknesses give a bright green light to anti-union companies to break the law. The NLRA doesn\u8217\'20\'19t allow for any fines, not even one dollar, if a company is found to have, for instance, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wesa.fm/economy-business/2023-07-05/pittsburgh-starbucks-workers-union-rehire"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 illegally fired the four workers leading a union drive}}}\uc2 . Nor can a company be fined for closing a store or operation in retaliation for its workers unionizing. When the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  rules that a company broke the law by refusing to bargain, it can\u8217\'20\'19t order the company to reach a first contract. All it can do is order the company to return to the bargaining table, but when that happens, many companies resume doing everything they can to avoid ever reaching a first contract. Even though the first \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  store unionized 20 months ago, the company hasn\u8217\'20\'19t reached a contract with workers at any of its 340-plus unionized stores.\par \par \uc2 The remedy that\u8217\'20\'19s ordered for a failure to bargain in good faith is an order to bargain more. That just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work\par \par \uc2 Benjamin Sachs of \uc2 Harvard University\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe remedy that\u8217\'20\'19s ordered for a failure to bargain in good faith is an order to bargain more. That just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Benjamin Sachs, a labor law professor at Harvard.\par \par \uc2 In response to the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s questions, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it \u8220\'20\'1Cis committed to progress negotiations towards a first contract\u8221\'20\'1D. The company accused the union of dragging its feet in bargaining, saying the union \u8220\'20\'1Chas only responded to 25% of the more than 465 bargaining sessions that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has proposed for individual stores\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The union responded that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is the one under scrutiny for refusing to bargain. The union added that it hasn\u8217\'20\'19t responded to many of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 requests to bargain because the company has sought to \u8220\'20\'1Cimpose illegal conditions\u8221\'20\'1D intended \u8220\'20\'1Cto prevent us from designating members of our own bargaining teams\u8221\'20\'1D. The union says \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has failed to make even one counterproposal to its many bargaining proposals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is proof that a concerted effort by a corporation to delay and violate the law too easily succeeds under the rules of the game we have today,\u8221\'20\'1D Sachs said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need new rules of the game.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only one to blame,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CThe legal system bears responsibility for enabling corporations to act this way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Criticizing the system\u8217\'20\'19s delays, Sachs noted that after a fired worker asks the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  for reinstatement, it can take up to five years of litigation \u8211\'20\'13 including a decision by an \uc2 NLRB\uc2  administrative law judge, then an appeal to the five-person labor board, then an appeal to a federal circuit court of appeals \u8211\'20\'13 before a worker wins reinstatement, and by then the union drive has often fallen apart because workers were frightened off or discouraged from joining.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou can have all the labor protections in the world, but if you don\u8217\'20\'19t have an effective enforcement and remedies scheme, then it\u8217\'20\'19s virtually worthless,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wilma Liebman, who served as chair of the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  under Barack Obama.\par \par \uc2 Schultz and his company continue to assert that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has not violated the law even though judges have ruled that \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ithaca.com/news/ithaca/labor-officials-say-starbucks-broke-law-by-closing-store-in-ithaca/article_89ab0e2c-5abf-11ed-acfa-db8a7873d2c9.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 illegally closed a store in Ithaca}}}\uc2  in retaliation for unionizing; illegally threatened workers in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/daily-labor-report/starbucks-illegally-threatened-seattle-workers-nlrb-judge-rules"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Seattle}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.law360.com/employment-authority/articles/1677574/starbucks-threatened-to-halt-benefits-nlrb-judge-says"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Los Angeles}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://chicago.eater.com/2023/5/8/23712814/starbucks-illegal-firing-labor-law-violations-hyde-park-edgewater-chicago-nlrb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chicago}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/daily-labor-report/starbucks-illegally-threatened-union-organizers-nlrb-judge-says"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Minneapolis}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/starbucks-violated-worker-rights-union-fight-labor-judge/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Buffalo}}}\uc2  with loss in pay and benefits because of union activity; illegally {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kcur.org/2023-05-31/federal-judge-rules-wichita-starbucks-broke-labor-law-during-union-drive"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reduced the hours of Wichita baristas}}}\uc2  ; illegally spied {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.unionprogress.com/2023/07/04/nlrb-judge-gives-starbucks-workers-a-huge-victory/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  on workers in Pittsburgh}}}\uc2  ; and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-10-12/starbucks-illegally-asked-for-cops-to-disperse-staff-judge-says?in_source=embedded-checkout-banner"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 illegally called police because baristas in Kansas City}}}\uc2  had congregated outside their store.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  will say to the grave that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  hasn\u8217\'20\'19t broken the law, but that\u8217\'20\'19s factually inaccurate,\u8221\'20\'1D San Francisco State\u8217\'20\'19s Logan said, pointing to the many rulings that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has violated the law.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has appealed ruling after ruling that found it has acted unlawfully. Schultz maintained that just because a trial judge had found illegalities doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  did anything wrong \u8211\'20\'13 that finding might be overturned on appeal.\par \par \uc2 Acknowledging that appeals can last years, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe process for reviewing the merits of these allegations is multi-step, includes several layers of review by the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  and the federal court system, and usually takes years to complete. Where claims have been filed against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  that we believe are unfounded, we continue to defend the company.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers see a clear objective behind \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 retaliatory moves: to frighten and even terrorize workers \u8211\'20\'13 to make workers too scared to support or work for unionization. Pro-union workers further assert that what they see as \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 refusal to bargain aims to deter workers at additional stores from unionizing by sending a loud message that if they unionize, there\u8217\'20\'19s no guarantee their store will negotiate a first contract anytime soon to deliver better wages and benefits. Workers at many stores allege that after their stores voted to unionize, management cut back on their weekly hours (and weekly pay) and cut their store\u8217\'20\'19s staffing to make their jobs more stressful and to show that unpleasant things happen if they unionize.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has taken a scorched-earth policy to target union leaders and union stores for retaliation,\u8221\'20\'1D said Richard Bensinger, an adviser to the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 unionization drive. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is starving out union supporters. They\u8217\'20\'19re cutting their hours and starving the stores by cutting staff. They\u8217\'20\'19re starving the unionized workers by not giving them credit card tips. They\u8217\'20\'19re doing everything they can at union stores to be as nasty as they can to undermine the union, to say to non-union workers, \u8216\'20\'18\u8216\'20\'18Look what\u8217\'20\'19s happening there.\u8217\'20\'19 In some cases, they\u8217\'20\'19re even closing unionized stores, like in Ithaca.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wxxinews.org/local-news/2023-05-11/starbucks-will-close-its-last-stores-in-ithaca-amid-ongoing-labor-dispute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 closed all three of its stores in Ithaca, New York}}}\uc2 , the first city in the US where every \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  was unionized. The company said the closings were for business reasons and had nothing to do with the union. But Kolya Vitek, a barista who worked at two of the Ithaca stores, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe closures are very blatantly union-busting. There is no reason they needed to close those stores.\u8221\'20\'1D Stephanie Heslop, another barista in Ithaca, added: \u8220\'20\'1CThey wanted to burn the union to the ground here.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After nearly four years as a barista, Quinn Craig led the effort to unionize a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in San Antonio, Texas. \u8220\'20\'1CAs soon as we filed our petition, I started preparing to get fired. I knew that it was coming,\u8221\'20\'1D said Craig, who often wore a cap saying \u8220\'20\'1CScary Union Organizer\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CI saw that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  was firing lead organizers in stores all across the country. By the time we won our election, we saw 30 or 40 worker-organizers fired across the country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The San Antonio organizing drive was fueled by dismay with constantly changing work schedules and what workers said was systematic understaffing, which made their jobs far more stressful. \u8220\'20\'1CWe also wanted to advocate for a better benefits system,\u8221\'20\'1D Craig said. \u8220\'20\'1CMore than half the people at our store didn\u8217\'20\'19t qualify for all the benefits that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is bragging about.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On 23 June 2022, the San Antonio workers voted 10 to 6 to unionize. Soon after, workers said, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  began reducing their weekly hours and pay \u8211\'20\'13 a move many saw as punishment for unionizing and a stratagem to get them to quit.\par \par \uc2 On the first anniversary of their union victory, the store\u8217\'20\'19s workers walked out, protesting what they said was understaffing. That same day, Craig was fired. \u8220\'20\'1CThey fired me on the one-year anniversary of our store winning a union election,\u8221\'20\'1D Craig said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey fired the lead organizer on the day we were celebrating. That\u8217\'20\'19s villainous. They\u8217\'20\'19re not sneaky about their retaliatory actions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To explain the firing, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said Craig had failed to secure the store\u8217\'20\'19s cash or set the security alarm before the walkout. \u8220\'20\'1CI called the manager to say we were walking out,\u8221\'20\'1D Craig said. \u8220\'20\'1CHer response was \u8216\'20\'18OK\u8217\'20\'19 and [she] hung up\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 without giving any instructions.\par \par \uc2 Alleging unlawful retaliation, Craig has asked the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  for reinstatement. Craig says \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 tactics \u8211\'20\'13 the firings, closings and reduced hours \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Chave really had a chilling effect. I personally saw several stores in my region lose interest in unionizing. Without all the union-busting, we could have had double the number of stores in my region organized.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many baristas say {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prospect.org/labor/2023-05-19-curtailing-starbucks-war-unionized-baristas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one Starbucks strategy in particular has discouraged workers}}}\uc2  from unionizing. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/05/03/1095909869/starbucks-union-ceo-howard-schultz-workers-united-labor-benefits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In May 2022, Schultz announced that Starbucks}}}\uc2  would give certain raises and benefits to workers at its more than 9,000 non-union stores, but not offer those raises and benefits to its unionized workers. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  insists it would be illegal to impose any raises or benefits on its unionized stores without first negotiating about them, but the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/08/25/business/economy/starbucks-union-howard-schultz-nlrb.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NLRB\u8217\'20\'19s general counsel asserts that this policy constitutes unlawful discrimination}}}\uc2  against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 unionized workers. Under this policy, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has given its non-union workers, but not its unionized ones, a more relaxed dress code, increased training, faster sick leave accrual and, most important, credit card tipping. (Workers at the first few \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/12/16/business/starbucks-tipping/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to unionize had asked early on for credit card tipping}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 Baristas say credit card tipping can boost pay by $5 an hour, often meaning a 30% pay increase. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 refusal to give many raises and benefits, including credit card tipping, to workers at its unionized stores has fueled {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://one.starbucks.com/negotiations/what-does-decertification-mean/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decertification efforts at more than a dozen stores}}}\uc2 . Decertification is a process to vote out the union. Pointing to the denial of credit card tipping, San Francisco State\u8217\'20\'19s Logan said: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is offering the workers a $5-an-hour bribe to vote out the union.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Federal law prohibits companies from aiding decertification efforts. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has referred workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MorePerfectUS/status/1692203682450805160"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interested in decertification to the National Right to Work Legal Defense Foundation}}}\uc2 , a group long funded by rightwing billionaires, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prwatch.org/news/2014/06/12498/who-behind-national-right-work-committee-and-its-anti-union-crusade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including the Koch brothers}}}\uc2 . But the coffee company says it hasn\u8217\'20\'19t joined in that foundation\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to assist decertification petitions. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/buffalo/news/2023/05/30/petition-to-decertify-starbucks-workers-united-dismissed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked several of the decertification petitions}}}\uc2  because it says \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had failed to bargain in good faith, preventing workers from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecity.nyc/2023/7/26/23809086/nlrb-starbucks-union#:~:text=National%20Right%20to%20Work's%20lawyers,should%20not%20have%20been%20considered."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 getting a fair shot at reaching a first contract}}}\uc2 . \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://one.starbucks.com/negotiations/what-does-decertification-mean/#:~:text=Importantly%2C%20the%20law%20prohibits%20Starbucks,refrain%20from%20joining%2C%20a%20union."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has criticized the labor board}}}\uc2  for not giving its workers a free choice to decertify the union \u8211\'20\'13 a claim many workers ridicule, saying that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , with its aggressive union-busting, hasn\u8217\'20\'19t given its workers a free choice on whether to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Labor experts have long proposed ways to revamp the NLRA so that it truly discourages illegal actions by anti-union employers. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/pro-act-problem-solution-chart/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Protecting the Right to Organize Act}}}\uc2  (Pro Act), which President Biden backs, but Senate Republicans have blocked, calls for substantial fines against companies that fire pro-union workers or commit other illegal actions.\par \par \uc2 Unless \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is made to pay a real price for its illegal conduct, there will be no reason for it not to violate the law\par \par \uc2 John Logan of \uc2 San Francisco State University\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnless \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is made to pay a real price for its illegal conduct, there will be no reason for it not to violate the law,\u8221\'20\'1D Logan said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would like to see a discussion of having criminal penalties for CEOs whose companies engage in egregious unlawful practices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Many labor leaders say that to prevent years of delay before negotiating a first contract \u8211\'20\'13 that is, if one is ever negotiated \u8211\'20\'13 the NLRA should provide for compulsory arbitration if the two sides fail to reach a first contract within a few months. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://onlabor.org/renewing-interest-in-interest-arbitration-first-contract-arbitration-and-the-pro-act/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pro Act calls for mandatory arbitration}}}\uc2 . Some labor experts look to Alberta, Canada, as a model; there, if the two sides fail to reach a first contract within 90 days after bargaining begins, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.alberta.ca/dispute-resolution-methods"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the dispute goes to a neutral arbitrator}}}\uc2  who determines the contract\u8217\'20\'19s provisions.\par \par \uc2 But every time Democrats have pushed to amend the NLRA to make it easier to unionize, Republicans have used filibusters to block the legislation. That happened under presidents Johnson, Carter, Clinton, Obama and Biden.\par \par \uc2 Short of overhauling the NLRA, union supporters say the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  should obtain a nationwide injunction to order \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to cease and desist from firing pro-union baristas. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s general counsel, \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/daily-labor-report/nlrb-argues-for-nationwide-injunction-in-denver-starbucks-case"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has repeatedly sought such an injunction}}}\uc2 , but judges {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/daily-labor-report/starbucks-escapes-nationwide-ban-on-terminating-union-supporters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have thus far failed to grant it}}}\uc2 , evidently not convinced that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is systematically taking illegal actions.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas applauded a \uc2 NLRB\uc2  decision from last Friday that some labor experts say could go far to discourage companies like \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  from violating the law when battling against unionization. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/news-story/board-issues-decision-announcing-new-framework-for-union-representation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Under the board\u8217\'20\'19s decision}}}\uc2 , if a majority of workers sign cards saying they want to unionize and the employer insists on holding a union vote and then is found by the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  to have broken the law in fighting unionization, the labor board will order the company to grant union recognition based on the signed cards.\par \par \uc2 But labor experts fear that conservative, corporate-friendly federal judges may overturn the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 With labor leaders complaining that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 illegalities continue unabated, many pro-union workers are pushing for more militant action to get \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to stop the firings and negotiate a first contract. Some have called for more strikes or civil disobedience outside \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  cafes or a nationwide consumer boycott \u8211\'20\'13 or a combination of all three strategies.\par \par \uc2 Despite \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 aggressive tactics, many workers remain optimistic. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re doing everything they can to crush our organizing effort. What they\u8217\'20\'19re doing is terrible, closing stories and firings,\u8221\'20\'1D said Casey Moore, a union spokesperson and fired Buffalo barista. \u8220\'20\'1CBut every day we still have stores filing for elections and workers emerging with new energy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230828ej8s00131\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
