\par\par \uc2 Money\par \b \uc2 Transition from \u8216\'20\'18dirty\u8217\'20\'19 to green US jobs rises, leaving older workers behind\b0\par\par\uc2 Nina Lakhani in New York \par \uc2 731 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2023\par 15:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers in their 40s, 50s and 60s and those without college degrees appear least likely to move into green jobs, research finds\par \par \uc2 The rate of transition from \u8220\'20\'1Cdirty\u8221\'20\'1D to green jobs is rapidly rising but older and blue-collar workers are being left behind, new research has found.\par \par \uc2 Overall the transition rate from dirty to clean industries increased tenfold between 2005 and 2021 with a significant uptick in EV-related roles \u8211\'20\'13 even before the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/11/biden-climate-bill-inflation-reduction-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $369bn Inflation Reduction Act boost}}}\uc2  for renewables and electric cars.\par \par \uc2 Yet fewer than 1% of all workers who leave a dirty job appear to get another in a clean or green industry, raising concerns about who will benefit from the green transition, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nber.org/papers/w31539"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study published in the National Bureau of Economic Research.}}}\par \par \uc2 Researchers from the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2  analysed data from 130m online work profiles \u8211\'20\'13 representing approximately 300m job-to-job transitions \u8211\'20\'13 to examine how workers may be affected by the shift from fossil fuels towards less dirty or carbon-intensive energy sources.\par \par \uc2 Dirty industries include coal, oil and gas extraction or production, as well as energy-intensive manufacturing jobs like cement, mining, chemical and paper and pulp.\par \par \uc2 Older workers (in their 40s, 50s and 60s) and those without a college education appear least likely to move into green jobs, which are classified as roles clearly associated with renewable energy like solar, wind or the production of electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 So far, the majority of green jobs have gone to white-collar workers like sales managers, software developers and marketing managers. Just over a quarter (27%) appear to have gone to first-time job seekers, and more than 20,000 jobs went to overseas workers.\par \par \uc2 Overall, about one in five workers moved from one dirty job to another. But in some cities, the share of dirty-to-dirty transitions is as high as 90%, suggesting there are limited attractive options outside carbon-intensive industries.\par \par \uc2 In some states such as Louisiana, Delaware and Texas over half of all transitions out of heavy industry or dirty jobs are into other dirty jobs, while workers in California, Oregon and Arizona workers are most likely to move into EV or clean energy industries. This may be down to a heavier concentration of fossil-fuel intensive jobs and barriers in accessing educational opportunities including reskilling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur data suggest that, if past trends continue, the clean energy transition may have important distributional consequences that could exacerbate underlying trends in labor market inequality,\u8221\'20\'1D the authors said.\par \par \uc2 The IRA, Biden\u8217\'20\'19s landmark climate legislation passed a year ago, targets both supply and demand for EVs and renewable technologies, and is central to the administration\u8217\'20\'19s climate and economic policies.\par \par \uc2 No Republican voted for the IRA but most of the investment that has been triggered by the bill has been funneled into projects in GOP-held congressional districts, with battery and electric vehicle plants popping up in states such as Georgia, Tennessee and Texas.\par \par \uc2 The IRA has been criticised for failing to require worker protections amid fears that the landmark funding boost will entrench existing inequalities \u8211\'20\'13 unless steps are taken to ensure the transition to a green economy is geographically, racially and socially just.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s tough to forecast the IRA\u8217\'20\'19s effects \u8230\'20\'26 but it will likely have a significant positive effect on the growth of green jobs overall,\u8221\'20\'1D said lead author Jisung Park, an environmental and labor economist at Penn\u8217\'20\'19s School of Social Policy & Practice.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe details of how the IRA will be implemented and targeted will likely continue to be in flux, but there certainly appears to be significant interest among policymakers in ensuring that we apply the lessons from previous employment shocks \u8211\'20\'13 including the so-called \u8216\'20\'18China shock\u8217\'20\'19 or from earlier environmental policies like the Clean Air Act \u8211\'20\'13 which had highly unequal impacts on workers across geography, educational background, and income,\u8221\'20\'1D Park added.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, as part of an IRA anniversary tour by the Biden administration, the treasury secretary, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , visits a union facility in Las Vegas, Nevada \u8211\'20\'13 a key battleground state for 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 to meet workers learning skills to work on clean energy projects. She said: \u8220\'20\'1CAccelerating these transitions can mean greater demand for US clean energy technologies produced by American workers. It can also bolster global clean energy supply chains.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230814ej8e0025t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
