\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Already vulnerable\u8217\'20\'19: UK film and TV workers feel the pinch from US strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Nadia Khomami and Jem Bartholomew \par \uc2 915 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 August 2023\par 11:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hollywood action has broad support in UK but is hitting livelihoods decimated by pandemic and arts cuts\par \par \uc2 UK film and TV workers have spoken about the devastating effects of the Hollywood strikes on their jobs and livelihoods, with many saying they are struggling to keep afloat.\par \par \uc2 The industrial action by members of the Writers Guild of America (WGA) and the Sag-Aftra union which began on 14 July have disrupted the film and television industries for the past month, bringing global production to a standstill and delaying the release of a number of high-profile movies. The focus has been on residuals \u8211\'20\'13 the payments that performers receive for repeat showings of films and TV shows \u8211\'20\'13 as well as issues over actors\u8217\'20\'19 likenesses being reproduced by artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 It is the first time in 60 years that Hollywood actors have joined their colleagues from the WGA on the picket line, with Meryl Streep, George Clooney, Jennifer Lawrence, Joaquin Phoenix and Jamie Lee Curtis among the famous names backing the action. Many Equity members in Britain also belong to Sag-Aftra and a lot of British films are co-productions, meaning UK workers have also been affected by the strike.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to the Guardian, Ian Heard, a 39-year-old location scout based in Sussex, said he expected to barely earn anything for the rest of the year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCovid blew all my savings, just keeping afloat,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19ve got to pay your tax bill, pay your mortgage, and then you\u8217\'20\'19ve got higher food prices, you\u8217\'20\'19ve got higher energy bills, everything is higher and there\u8217\'20\'19s not really anywhere you can cut \u8230\'20\'26 Everyone\u8217\'20\'19s going to suffer.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Heard added that crew workers were not represented in the strikes by actors or writers, which made it hard for some of them to support the action. \u8220\'20\'1CCrew know that when everything is back to normal, their conditions won\u8217\'20\'19t improve,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Eleanor Ventress, a 28-year-old costume designer based in London, said better pay and protection against AI was something that was \u8220\'20\'1Cworth fighting for\u8221\'20\'1D, but added: \u8220\'20\'1CObviously it does have knock-on effects for us.\u8221\'20\'1D Ventress said she had a project postponed at short notice and did not know when she would work again.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s definitely watching the pennies now,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s all about trying to find side hustles at the moment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For instance, Ventress has taken on work two days a week outside the film and TV industries, and is considering selling items on the online craft marketplace \uc2 Etsy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Bickerdike is a 53-year-old physical and mechanical special effects technician based in West Sussex. \u8220\'20\'1CMy income has completely switched off, but my overheads are the same,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m just living off my cushion at the moment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bickerdike, who has a seven-year-old son, said he worried about the uncertainty of having no income for potentially months. \u8220\'20\'1CThese people going on strike impacts an enormous range of people,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers (\uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ), an industry trade association, continues to be unwilling to discuss overhauling residuals, maintaining a bullish line that writers and actors will run out of money before its members do. Last month, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2023/07/writers-strike-hollywood-studios-deal-fight-wga-actors-1235434335/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an unnamed studio executive}}}\uc2  reportedly said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe endgame is to allow things to drag on until union members start losing their apartments and losing their houses.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anthony Boys, a 44-year-old editor for TV series such as Veep and The Great who works with a lot of US clients, said he \u8220\'20\'1Ccouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be more in favour\u8221\'20\'1D of the fight to secure better payment residuals and protections against AI.\par \par \uc2 Boys said he was lucky to have built up some income, as he did not expect to work until 2024, but added that not everyone in the industry would be in his position.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are other symptoms going on that are affecting the UK industry,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, such as Brexit, the fallout from the pandemic and \u8220\'20\'1Cthe current government\u8217\'20\'19s unwillingness to invest in culture. It\u8217\'20\'19s creating a bit of a perfect storm.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Helena, a 31-year-old costume professional in London, said it was incredibly tough for film and TV workers lower down the food chain. She had a week\u8217\'20\'19s notice of a job ending \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cmy income\u8217\'20\'19s gone to zero\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 which was a big financial hit after Covid, during which she did not get furlough money.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLots of people have been playing catchup after that,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you\u8217\'20\'19re already vulnerable, then it\u8217\'20\'19s these moments that can be quite hard.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, it was announced that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2023/aug/10/emmy-awards-emmys-2023-postponed-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the 2023 Emmy awards would be pushed back four months}}}\uc2  to 15 January 2024, reflecting the mood in Hollywood that a deal between Sag-Aftra and the WGA with the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  is unlikely to be imminent.\par \par \uc2 In July, a host of British actors including Brian Cox, Imelda Staunton, Naomie Harris and Simon Pegg {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/jul/21/uk-star-london-rally-support-striking-us-actor-brian-cox-equity-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joined a demonstration in London}}}\uc2  in solidarity with the Hollywood strike.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to the Guardian at the time, Spencer MacDonald, the national secretary of the \uc2 Bectu\uc2  trade union, said thousands of crew members in the UK had been suspended from productions. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re still under contract but they\u8217\'20\'19re not being paid and can\u8217\'20\'19t work for another employer,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey need to be released from their contracts or compensated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230813ej8d000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
