\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US economy adds 187,000 July jobs in sign labor market is cooling\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2023\par 14:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Figure is less than expected in first jobs report since Fed raised interest rate to 22-year high\par \par \uc2 US employers added 187,000 jobs in July, less than expected and a sign that the labor market is cooling after a series of interest rate hikes by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  have driven rates to their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/26/fed-raises-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest level }}}\uc2  in 22 years.\par \par \uc2 The jobs market has continued to add at least 200,000 new jobs each month this year, according to the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ). But that compares to an average monthly gain of 400,000 in 2022.\par \par \uc2 July\u8217\'20\'19s gains were just 2,000 more than the jobs added in June. The \uc2 BLS\uc2  revised June\u8217\'20\'19s job gain down to 185,000, a cut of 24,000 jobs. It also cut May\u8217\'20\'19s jobs number. Together, June and July represent the two weakest monthly gains in two and a half years.\par \par \uc2 While the jobs market remains robust, July\u8217\'20\'19s gains represent a considerable dip compared to January of this year, when 472,000 jobs were added. The unemployment rate has remained stable at 3.5%, the same rate seen in June and close to the historically low rates seen before 2020.\par \par \uc2 Healthcare and social assistance accounted for the most new positions in July, adding 87,000 new jobs. The government added 15,000 new jobs.\par \par \uc2 Black unemployment had risen for the last two months, often a sign of trouble ahead. The rate dipped in July from 6% to 5.8% \u8211\'20\'13 still far higher than the 3.1% unemployment rate for White Americans.\par \par \uc2 Wage gains continued to outpace inflation \u8211\'20\'13 rising 4.5% from April to June compared to a year ago. But the pace of rises slowed from 4.8% in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 Angela Hanks, chief of programs at Demos and former acting assistant secretary at the Department of Labor, said the figures showed the job market remained \u8220\'20\'1Cresilient overall\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe saw in June that wages were finally beginning to outpace inflation, and this month\u8217\'20\'19s report shows that trend continues. Which means that people are actually getting to feel those wage gains,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 But she said there was Black unemployment rate was still troubling. \u8220\'20\'1CIt remains nearly double the white unemployment rate, and above the historic lows it hit earlier this year. There\u8217\'20\'19s still room to improve on this front, and this remains the number to watch in subsequent reports.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 July\u8217\'20\'19s job figures, along with these other economic indicators, suggest that the Fed may be able to pull off a \u8220\'20\'1Csoft landing\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 bringing down inflation without causing a recession, as some economists expected to happen as soon as later this year. In June, the annual rate of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/12/us-inflation-rate-june-2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inflation}}}\uc2  reached a two-year low at 3%, three times lower than it was a year prior.\par \par \uc2 Many economists are expecting the Fed to stop its rate hikes soon as it allows the economy to stabilize. That job figures have continued to be strong represents a sign of the resilience of the economy, but the economy may not fully yet have fully responded to the impact of high rates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve covered a lot of ground, and the full effects of our tightening have yet to be felt,\u8221\'20\'1D Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said after the interest rate hikes were announced. As Powell has said over the last year, he also said that getting inflation to the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s target of 2% \u8220\'20\'1Chas a long way to go\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Hanks warned the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s actions could still harm the economy. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile these numbers are positive, there\u8217\'20\'19s still reason to worry that the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s actions \u8211\'20\'13 including resuming rate hikes in July \u8211\'20\'13 could have a negative impact on the labor market. While the Fed is no longer predicting a recession, its own actions could continue to threaten one,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230804ej84001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
