\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The police see us as disposable\u8217\'20\'19: what life\u8217\'20\'19s really like in New York\u8217\'20\'19s maligned \u8216\'20\'18red light district\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Wilfred Chan in New York \par \uc2 2,150 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2023\par 22:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As rightwing media and the mayor target a Queens neighborhood, sex workers say they\u8217\'20\'19re just trying to survive\par \par \uc2 In 2000, Cecilia Gentili worked late nights as a sex worker on Roosevelt Avenue, a dusty corridor linking Queens\u8217\'20\'19 most diverse communities, under the shadow of the clattering 7 train. It was the only way she could survive as a new immigrant. After coming out as trans, she had been shut out by employers in her native Argentina, and in New York, \u8220\'20\'1CI really thought that things would be different,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CBut in a way, it was a double level of discrimination: being trans and being undocumented. So again, I found myself engaged in street sex work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She rented a $150-a-month room in a shared apartment in Jackson Heights, a neighborhood full of other immigrants and queer and trans sex workers trying to help each other scrape by. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I needed a place to live, it was not the city of New York that facilitated it, it was another sex worker. When I needed to eat, it was another trans person,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. If she felt unsafe on the street, she\u8217\'20\'19d walk to a late-night street vendor and stand in their lamp\u8217\'20\'19s warm glow.\par \par \uc2 Roosevelt Avenue has been a hub for immigrant sex workers for decades \u8211\'20\'13 particularly Asian cis women and South American trans women, says Mateo Guerrero, a trans justice organizer with the Make the Road non-profit. Jessica Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez-Rojas, a queer New York state assemblywoman whose district includes a long stretch of Roosevelt, often runs into sex workers at marches and when she\u8217\'20\'19s out at gay bars. \u8220\'20\'1CThese workers are just as much a part of the community as anyone else,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, \u8220\'20\'1Cand many are just trying to survive each day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the number of street-based sex workers in Queens has increased since the pandemic, due to increasing economic pressure. Living near Roosevelt has become much more expensive in recent years, and as wealthier residents move into newly built luxury high-rises, it\u8217\'20\'19s led to a familiar pattern: a middle-class backlash against street workers, followed by pledges from authorities to \u8220\'20\'1Cclean up\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, the conservative tabloid the New York Post ran stories \u8220\'20\'1Cexposing\u8221\'20\'1D the sex work on Roosevelt, which it described as a \u8220\'20\'1Csex-plagued \u8230\'20\'26red light district taking over the neighborhood filled with families with children\u8221\'20\'1D. The coverage \u8211\'20\'13 picked up by Fox News as well as the major Spanish-language broadcaster \uc2 Univision\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 criticized police for targeting sidewalk food sellers while ignoring the \u8220\'20\'1Chordes of hookers\u8221\'20\'1D. It quoted a newly formed neighborhood of \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerned moms\u8221\'20\'1D and a local city councilman, Francisco Moya, who demanded that Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cget enforcement in here to clean up Roosevelt Avenue\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Adams responded quickly. At an unrelated press conference on Monday, the mayor said he had taken a late night trip to Roosevelt Avenue to see the neighborhood for himself. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was filthy,\u8221\'20\'1D he told reporters. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was just a state of disorder \u8230\'20\'26 and it was clear, there was prostitution.\u8221\'20\'1D Adams \u8211\'20\'13 a former cop who as mayor has led highly publicized police sweeps of subway trains and homeless encampments \u8211\'20\'13 vowed to launch \u8220\'20\'1Can operation to deal with the sex workers\u8221\'20\'1D, though he offered no other details.\par \par \uc2 But sex workers say more policing won\u8217\'20\'19t do anything to stamp out their industry \u8211\'20\'13 and will only leave the entire neighborhood worse off.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Sex work has always been a service industry at the edges of society, and in Queens, it\u8217\'20\'19s often inextricable from the immigrant experience. Many sex workers\u8217\'20\'19 clients are also undocumented people \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cworkers who are in construction, people who may be all alone in this country and are looking for company\u8221\'20\'1D, says Guerrero. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat many of the sex workers that I work with talk about is how often the men cry. Or they talk about how lonely they feel. So it\u8217\'20\'19s not just sexual services, but sometimes it\u8217\'20\'19s like a conversation to release some of the emotional things that are coming up for them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It also points to why there are growing numbers of visible sex workers in Queens: more people are struggling. \u8220\'20\'1CIn a post-Covid world, people who were already on the margins have just been pushed deeper into economic and social desperation and isolation, and this is only going to be magnified in the sex industry,\u8221\'20\'1D says Melissa Broudo, a prominent sex worker advocate and defense attorney.\par \par \uc2 That creates a sense of solidarity \u8211\'20\'13 contrary to tabloid narratives \u8211\'20\'13 between sex workers and street vendors, who are also harassed by city officials.\par \par \uc2 Rene Tlecuil Luna, a Mexican snack vendor on Roosevelt, says he views street-based sex workers as part of the community. Like sex workers, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe just want to work and earn our money honestly\u8221\'20\'1D, he says. Yolanda, an Ecuadorian food truck worker down the block, says sex workers don\u8217\'20\'19t deserve to be policed. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are also working,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI know that it is frowned upon by people, but it is their job.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The police see us as disposable. It\u8217\'20\'19s not the same way they arrest a Wall Street investor\par \par \uc2 Or, as Gentili puts it: \u8220\'20\'1CThe reality is that people who engage in sex work in the streets are customers of street vendors, and street vendors are customers of sex workers. So we\u8217\'20\'19re all a whole economy supporting each other.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But street-based sex work is particularly tough, and a reflection of inequality. White, light-skinned, and more highly educated workers tend to market themselves online as escorts to high-paying customers \u8211\'20\'13 whereas Black and brown workers are more likely to do street-based sex work, which is often riskier.\par \par \uc2 Francesca is a trans sex worker who came to Jackson Heights from Mexico a year ago and sees no choice but to work on Roosevelt. Her apartment costs $2,200 a month, and she has to stay extremely focused to survive. \u8220\'20\'1CIf I could get good employment without discrimination, then I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be doing sex work,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CThe truth is, I\u8217\'20\'19ve worked in the streets since I was young.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest dangers is the police. Prostitution is a jailable offense in New York, and sex workers have long been an easy target for police abuse, says Gentili, who says she\u8217\'20\'19s been harassed, arrested, and beaten by cops. \u8220\'20\'1CThe police see us as disposable. It\u8217\'20\'19s not the same way they arrest a Wall Street investor,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 For many years, New York police, led by the infamous \u8220\'20\'1Cvice squad\u8221\'20\'1D, have prowled poorer neighborhoods like Jackson Heights, targeting people who appear to be sex workers or trans \u8211\'20\'13 whether they\u8217\'20\'19re working or even sex workers at all.\par \par \uc2 Guerrero rattles off a list of trans women he knows in the neighborhood who have been harassed by cops for doing nothing: going to the grocery store. Walking to the park. Going to the pharmacy. One trans woman was walking down the street when someone pulled up offering her a ride home \u8211\'20\'13 the driver turned out to be an undercover cop. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was no sexual exchange in that ride. They drove one block and then the police cars came and surrounded them,\u8221\'20\'1D Guerrero says, and that woman was deported.\par \par \uc2 When the policing of sex work meets immigration enforcement, the results can be deadly. Jackson Heights\u8217\'20\'19 trans community is still reeling after the loss of the longtime neighborhood figure Melissa Nu\u241\'00\'F1ez, a Jackson Heights street-based sex worker and trans rights activist who was deported last fall to Honduras \u8211\'20\'13 where she was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/caribbean-tegucigalpa-honduras-ffbbf0c6ed59b229565bccd21bb2f2f4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shot and killed}}}\uc2  by unidentified gunmen weeks later. Her arrest record for sex work had made her ineligible for permanent status in the United States. \u8220\'20\'1CShe was very lovely, very bubbly, and very committed to social justice,\u8221\'20\'1D Guerrero says. \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t want anyone else from our community to have the same story as Melissa. And that\u8217\'20\'19s why it\u8217\'20\'19s so dangerous what \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  is attempting to do with more police units on the ground.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While cities have long targeted sex work on morality grounds, in recent years officials have increasingly reframed enforcement around human trafficking concerns. But \uc2 FBI\uc2  data shows that in 2021, there were 8,888 arrests for prostitution nationally compared with just 625 for sex trafficking. Melissa Broudo, a longtime sex worker defense attorney, says criminalizing sex work only makes it harder to fight trafficking. \u8220\'20\'1CPolice are actively hurting the trust that needs to be established, the relationships that need to be built, in order to actually target traffickers,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Criminalization doesn\u8217\'20\'19t reduce the amount of street-based sex work either, Broudo says. One reason sex workers work on the streets at all is that many online platforms were effectively banned by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/apr/10/sex-workers-fear-violence-as-us-cracks-down-on-online-ads-girls-will-die"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fosta/Sesta}}}\uc2 , a federal law passed in 2018. Sex workers who work exclusively indoors have also been targeted by police, including in the 2017 raid on a Queens massage parlor that resulted in the death of a 38-year-old massage worker, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theappeal.org/family-former-attorney-of-queens-woman-who-fell-to-her-death-in-vice-sting-say-she-was-sexually-d67461a12f1/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Yang Song}}}\uc2 , when she fell from the fourth floor.\par \par \uc2 Esther K, an activist at Red Canary Song, which organizes with Asian massage workers in Queens, says police often seize all the assets they find when raiding a massage parlor: \u8220\'20\'1Cphones, jewelry, and cash that massage workers have been trying really hard to save to send back home to family or to provide for their children\u8221\'20\'1D. All this does is push workers to make even more desperate decisions and take more risks to survive, she says.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that authorities aren\u8217\'20\'19t providing real solutions. \u8220\'20\'1CUnless the police are going in with buckets of cash and visas, they\u8217\'20\'19re not getting to the root of what people really need,\u8221\'20\'1D Broudo says. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s simply, \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re going to chase you into these massage parlors; now we\u8217\'20\'19re going to chase you into the street.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe have significantly reduced the number of arrests for prostitution itself as we work in every case to connect the victims of human trafficking with the services they need. Yet prostitution in all forms remains prohibited by law. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  deploys where crime is reported and we enforce the law impartially.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Anti-trans legislation from other states gets to us. People from Texas, Florida, they come to New York City\par \par \uc2 Gentili sees the situation in Jackson Heights as a reflection not only of what\u8217\'20\'19s happening in New York, but also the rest of the country. \u8220\'20\'1CEven if we\u8217\'20\'19re in a progressive city, anti-trans legislation from other states gets to us. People from Texas, Florida \u8211\'20\'13 where people can\u8217\'20\'19t even get their hormones \u8211\'20\'13 they don\u8217\'20\'19t go to Alabama, they don\u8217\'20\'19t go to Schenectady. They come to New York City. And if you\u8217\'20\'19re Latina, you go to Queens. And they find themselves unemployed, with an incredible lack of services.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s also the latest example of a well-worn cycle in New York City history. Queer people find refuge in a neglected urban neighborhood \u8211\'20\'13 from the gay men of Times Square\u8217\'20\'19s former porn theaters, to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/jun/21/stroll-documentary-trans-sex-workers-new-york-city-hbo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trans sex workers}}}\uc2  of the Meatpacking District. Then that neighborhood starts becoming attractive to middle-class professionals, who try to force out street workers: \u8220\'20\'1CBecause they want a luxury apartment in Queens, and they don\u8217\'20\'19t look at who is getting the short end of the stick.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What would actually help, advocates say, is the decriminalization of sex work, so that workers can make a living and access essential resources without fear. One of the most prominent efforts is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/feb/27/sex-work-new-york-decriminalize-safely"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stop Violence in the Sex Trades Act}}}\uc2 , a state bill that would eliminate penalties for consensual sex work and strengthen labor protections for sex workers. Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez-Rojas, a sponsor of the bill, says decriminalization simply acknowledges \u8220\'20\'1Cthe reality that sex work will continue to exist and prohibition is not a model that has ever worked. It didn\u8217\'20\'19t work for cannabis and it won\u8217\'20\'19t work for this\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That still leaves the question of whether Jackson Heights\u8217\'20\'19 increasingly wealthy residents can recognize street workers as their neighbors.\par \par \uc2 Gentili says she hopes there can be more conversation. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat are some things that we can change as sex workers to help the community? And what are some things the community can do to stop being such a pain in the ass?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople will say, \u8216\'20\'18We care for sex workers, just not in my block.\u8217\'20\'19 But the folks that are complaining are folks who are just getting into Queens. And Queens was Queens before they got here,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 What those people should realize? \u8220\'20\'1CSex work, not for nothing, is the oldest profession. People need to survive. And we\u8217\'20\'19re not going anywhere.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230803ej83002e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\