\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 Dry cleaners, restaurants, hotels: the ripple effects of Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrew Gumbel in Burbank \par \uc2 1,618 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2023\par 13:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the actors\u8217\'20\'19 and writers\u8217\'20\'19 strikes settle in, restaurants, cleaners and other businesses prepare for a financial downturn\par \par \uc2 At first glance, not much seems amiss at Milt & Edie\u8217\'20\'19s dry cleaners, a stone\u8217\'20\'19s throw from the \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2  studio where a long summer of labour turmoil in Hollywood has brought production to a shuddering halt.\par \par \uc2 The decor is an optimistic Barbie-pink, the counters are jammed with customers, even on a weekday morning, and the cavernous rear of the store is so stuffed with freshly pressed clothes snaking along the line it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to spot any obvious portents of trouble.\par \par \uc2 Then Robert Shapiro, the manager, takes a moment to explain what has happened since Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s writers stopped work in May, followed by actors earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 First, he said, the studio stopped sending its chatshow guests for alterations or last-minute cleanings because, without writers, the chatshows could no longer go on the air.\par \par \uc2 Then, when the actors stopped working, the usual nightly loads from film and television productions dried up. So did the regular stream of tailoring and cleaning jobs that come in ahead of galas and premieres, because the actor\u8217\'20\'19s union has forbidden its members to do publicity for as long as the strike endures.\par \par \uc2 Business is down 20% because of the hit we\u8217\'20\'19ve taken to our studio accounts\par \par \uc2 Robert Shapiro of Milt & Edie\u8217\'20\'19s dry cleaners\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBusiness is down 20% because of the hit we\u8217\'20\'19ve taken to our studio accounts,\u8221\'20\'1D Shapiro said. And it\u8217\'20\'19s not just Warner Bros. Disney is barely a mile away, and \uc2 Universal\uc2  about two.\par \par \uc2 Milt & Edie\u8217\'20\'19s is an institution (\u8220\'20\'1COver 70 years cleaning clothes in southern California,\u8221\'20\'1D says a sign on top of a 1940s-era cleaning truck stationed in its parking lot), which means it has a lot of loyal customers. Even institutions are not immune to the Hollywood strikes, however, especially in the workaday city of Burbank, where more than 1,000 companies employing 30,000 people {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.burbankca.gov/documents/107269/300694/20201210.AdoptedCIP18_19.pdf/0b7b111e-6740-1b31-8103-262a4eef94b7?t=1607636422278#:~:text=Studios%20and%20The%20Walt%20Disney,industry%2C%20employing%20over%2033%2C000%20employees."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 depend}}}\uc2  in some way on the entertainment industry.\par \par \uc2 When the studios lay off large numbers of security guards because their lots are empty and are likely to stay that way, that hits the dry-cleaning business. When company executives are no longer dressing up for meetings, that hits the dry-cleaning business. Shapiro said loyal customers worried about paying their rent have started asking him for work, but of course as long as the strike persists Milt & Edie\u8217\'20\'19s isn\u8217\'20\'19t hiring.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat really sucks,\u8221\'20\'1D Shapiro said, \u8220\'20\'1Cis that business was finally back to normal [after the pandemic]. We were finally hitting our pre-Covid numbers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a similar story across large swaths of Los Angeles. The industry is not confined to the area of the city called Hollywood (home to the Walk of Fame and the Kodak Theater that hosts the Oscars), but is spread across a much larger geographical area, from the flatlands of Burbank and Glendale behind the Hollywood Hills, through the mini-malls of the suburban San Fernando valley, and over the mountains to Culver City (where \uc2 MGM\uc2  once made its classic musicals and it is now home to \uc2 Sony\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 In many of these places, businesses with an industry clientele \u8211\'20\'13 whether they service Hollywood directly as drivers, caterers and makeup artists, or are part of a secondary economy of restaurants, coffee shops, photo studios, hair and nail salons, and so on \u8211\'20\'13 are experiencing the strike as a double whammy after Covid and its aftershocks.\par \par \uc2 You couldn\u8217\'20\'19t put Los Angeles in a worse position\par \par \uc2 Angela Marsden of the Pineapple Hill Saloon & Grill\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou couldn\u8217\'20\'19t put Los Angeles in a worse position,\u8221\'20\'1D said Angela Marsden, an outspoken restaurant owner whose Pineapple Hill Saloon & Grill in the San Fernando valley regularly hosts musicians and comedians and has industry clients on both sides of the dispute.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe went through all the horrendousness of Covid. Then we had a crime wave, and prices were going through the roof \u8230\'20\'26 Finally we thought we were getting a little bit of a reprieve, then bam! I was set to raise my prices this month, but now the actors and writers who support us are on strike. Maybe they still have savings, but they\u8217\'20\'19re going to start feeling it soon and they\u8217\'20\'19re not going to be able to spend money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Visits to popular industry coffee shops, bakers and cafes appeared to bear out Marsden\u8217\'20\'19s analysis that real trouble may be only starting. Managers at spots in and around Burbank said they were seeing no signs yet of a drop-off in lunch custom, but they were experiencing unusual dead spots during the mornings and were thinking about cutting staff.\par \par \uc2 For businesses that depend more directly on film and TV production, the impact has been much more immediate. Jordyn Palos, who owns a public relations firm, said the strike was worse than Covid because at least then there was room to pivot to new ways of doing the job in cyberspace. \u8220\'20\'1CNow,\u8221\'20\'1D she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2023/biz/columns/hollywood-publicist-sag-strike-should-end-column-1235675728/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  in the industry publication Variety, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe are being told we cannot do our jobs at all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ken Fritz, who has a fleet of 900 cars he rents to the industry, said he\u8217\'20\'19d just been robbed of $250,000 worth of catalytic converters, and with his income now down to a trickle he had no way to pay for replacements. Greg Twiford, the head of a Hollywood animal trainers\u8217\'20\'19 association, said his members didn\u8217\'20\'19t know how much longer they could go on feeding their animals \u8211\'20\'13 everything from snakes and house pets to lions and elephants, many of them kept on ranches in the northern reaches of Los Angeles county where the mountains meet the desert.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe animals are starving,\u8221\'20\'1D Twiford said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a saying in this industry: \u8216\'20\'18One day you\u8217\'20\'19re eating chicken, the next day you\u8217\'20\'19re eating feathers.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a saying in this industry: \u8216\'20\'18One day you\u8217\'20\'19re eating chicken, the next day you\u8217\'20\'19re eating feathers\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Greg Twiford\par \par \uc2 Assessing the overall damage of a protracted strike is difficult. Los Angeles has a highly diversified economy, people who work multiple jobs and consumers who can easily switch their spending to other things \u8211\'20\'13 sports, or video games \u8211\'20\'13 if, say, they run out of content that interests them on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 . One widely cited estimate put the economic cost of the 100-day writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike in 2007-2008 at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://milkeninstitute.org/article/writers-strike-leaves-california-21-billion-loss-more-come-if-sag-contract-not-resolved"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $2bn}}}\uc2 , though other experts suggest that figure is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://files.static-nzz.ch/2023/07/14/b3a4568f-1972-4702-8bee-ebd715b75c7a.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 greatly exaggerated}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For now, at least, community support for the strike is strong, and despite the economic upheavals \u8211\'20\'13 social media is rife with stories of people facing eviction or defaulting on their debts \u8211\'20\'13 it is difficult to find Angelenos outside studio executive suites who oppose what the writers and actors are fighting for.\par \par \uc2 Initially, a perception took hold in some quarters that this was a dispute pitting Hollywood millionaires against each other, but that has shifted as the strike leaders have articulated their grievances and more people understand that the vast majority of actors and writers struggle like everyone else to make a decent living.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2  picket line one day last week, drivers zipping past in everything from a big food delivery truck to a beaten-up two-seater sedan honked in support. Some local businesses have started distributing free iced water or offering discounts even though they are tightening their own belts. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople need help,\u8221\'20\'1D said Patricia Rivera, who owns Tequilas Canteen & Grill and offers 15% off to any struggling union member, not just those on strike. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen it was difficult for us, during the pandemic, many customers were there for us. This is about helping the community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Konstantine Anthony, the mayor of Burbank who is a former actor and a regular presence on the picket lines, said he\u8217\'20\'19d had several conversations with skeptical business owners and helped them understand that their own financial wellbeing was at stake, too. \u8220\'20\'1CThey realize that if the actors and writers get less and less, they won\u8217\'20\'19t have money to spend,\u8221\'20\'1D Anthony said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey see that they have more in common with the strikers than they thought.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I stand by them in their fight. I hope every other union will join them\par \par \uc2 Angela Marsden of the Pineapple Hill Saloon & Grill\par \par \uc2 It certainly helps that many restaurants and bars employ aspiring actors. It helps, too, that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-07-20/-hot-strike-summer-hollywood-ups-workers-are-making-labor-history?in_source=embedded-checkout-banner"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unions}}}\uc2  across different economic sectors have been vocal this summer about taking a stand against their employers and seeing all their struggles as one. Hotel workers, truck drivers and even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2023/06/writers-strike-strippers-broadway-lin-manuel-miranda-1235418560/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strippers}}}\uc2  have shown up on the Hollywood picket lines, all of them arguing they are in the same fight for basic fairness against corporate executives who care about their shareholders more than their employees.\par \par \uc2 Marsden, the outspoken restaurant owner, likened the fight to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/style/angela-marsden-video-pineapple-hill/2020/12/18/5a69eb9e-3e54-11eb-9453-fc36ba051781_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 attention-grabbing moment}}}\uc2  during the Covid lockdown when she erupted in fury at local elected officials because they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t let her open her outdoor patio to diners, even though a Hollywood production was serving food to its workers in a parking lot less than 50ft away.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was calling out the big people who were picking and choosing who\u8217\'20\'19s essential and who isn\u8217\'20\'19t. That\u8217\'20\'19s the fight the actors and writers are in now,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think they have a choice \u8230\'20\'26 If they give in, the industry\u8217\'20\'19s not going to need them at some point. There are businesses that won\u8217\'20\'19t make it through if this goes on. But I do stand by them in their fight. I hope every other union will join them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230729ej7t001ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
