\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It feels like it\u8217\'20\'19s strike summer\u8217\'20\'19: US unions flex muscles across industries\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2023\par 21:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Labor is increasingly militant after years of inaction, energized by star power from the actors\u8217\'20\'19 and writers\u8217\'20\'19 strikes\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been a fast and furious few weeks for labor. First, 3,000 workers went on strike at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jun/23/starbucks-strike-pride-decorations-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  150 Starbucks}}}\uc2 , then 6,000 Los Angeles {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/04/los-angeles-hotel-workers-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hotel workers}}}\uc2  walked out, and now 11,500 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jul/22/sag-aftra-wga-strike-artificial-intelligence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Hollywood writers}}}\uc2  and 160,000 television and movie actors have gone on strike. Not only that, 340,000 \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers seemed ready to walk out on 1 August, and the \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW) union\uc2  is threatening to strike one or more Detroit automakers later this summer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like it\u8217\'20\'19s strike summer,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kent Wong, director of the UCLA Labor Center. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s tremendous energy within the labor movement, and there\u8217\'20\'19s tremendous energy on the strike lines.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTik tock #HotLaborSummer,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  tweeted last week as they counted down to a strike at \uc2 UPS\uc2  that could have cost the company more than $800m and the country over $7bn, according {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/business/ups-teamsters-restart-talks-effort-avoid-strike-2023-07-25/#:~:text=One%20estimate%20put%20the%20potential,company%20losses%20of%20%24816%20million."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to one estimate}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But on Tuesday a \uc2 UPS\uc2  strike was averted as the company increased its offer and the union declared victory in what could be a significant win for the labor movement. In announcing the settlement, Sean O\u8217\'20\'19Brien, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 general president, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis contract sets a new standard in the labor movement,\u8221\'20\'1D adding that \uc2 UPS\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Chas put $30bn in new money on the table as a direct result of these negotiations\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 ??\u8220\'20\'1CWE\u8217\'20\'19VE CHANGED THE GAME\u8221\'20\'1D: TEAMSTERS WIN HISTORIC \uc2 UPS\uc2  CONTRACT??\par \par \uc2 Today, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Teamsters?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Teamsters}}}\uc2  reached the most historic tentative agreement for workers in the history of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/UPS?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @UPS}}}\uc2 , protecting and rewarding more than 340,000 UPS Teamsters nationwide. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/1u?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #1u}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4Ap3j4XUTD"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/4Ap3j4XUTD}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  (@Teamsters) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Teamsters/status/1683867683845021697?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 25, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Maite Tapia, a professor of labor relations at Michigan State, said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not just a hot, labor summer \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19re in a protest and strike wave. It\u8217\'20\'19s fascinating and inspiring to see how these workers are leveraging their power against massive corporations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Employee frustration and anger have fueled the work stoppages. Many frontline workers are still fuming about how poorly they were treated during the pandemic, and many are upset that their pay increases have lagged far behind inflation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are coming out of more than three years of pandemic where people felt that economic inequality has grown,\u8221\'20\'1D Wong said. \u8220\'20\'1CMany workers were called essential workers, but they often felt they weren\u8217\'20\'19t respected or appreciated, yet at the same time they have seen all this outrageous corporate greed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the corporations hit by strikes or threatened with them say they have made generous contract offers, although management and labor often seem to be talking past each other. Many unions argue that it\u8217\'20\'19s only fair that workers receive larger-than-usual raises to offset the 9% inflation that coursed through the US economy, but many corporations resist giving raises of more than 3% a year.\par \par \uc2 My co-workers don\u8217\'20\'19t feel they\u8217\'20\'19re treated with respect. They\u8217\'20\'19re treated like they\u8217\'20\'19re just a piece of trash\par \par \uc2 Diana Rios-Sanchez\par \par \uc2 Diana Rios-Sanchez, a housekeeping supervisor at the InterContinental Hotel in Los Angeles, said thousands of hotel workers walked out for three days at more than 30 hotels because they feel underappreciated and underpaid. \u8220\'20\'1CMy co-workers don\u8217\'20\'19t feel they\u8217\'20\'19re treated with respect,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re treated like they\u8217\'20\'19re just a piece of trash.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rios-Sanchez said that because of Los Angeles\u8217\'20\'19 soaring rents, hotel workers are desperate for sizable raises. She makes $26 an hour, but she and her husband can only afford a one-bedroom apartment for themselves and their three children. \u8220\'20\'1CI might take home $3,500 a month with overtime, but a two-bedroom apartment costs $2,000 to $2,500 a month, and then there\u8217\'20\'19s childcare and food bills,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The tight labor market helps make it a good time for workers to strike. Unions are more likely to strike when the jobless rate is low; that hampers companies\u8217\'20\'19 ability to find replacement workers during a walkout. Also, strikes are contagious, emboldening other workers to walk out.\par \par \uc2 In California, the epicenter of today\u8217\'20\'19s labor strife, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/dec/27/university-of-california-strike-settlement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 48,000 University of California graduate teaching assistants, researchers}}}\uc2  and other academic workers walked out last November and won a settlement that included raises of more than 55% for the lowest-paid workers. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2023/03/25/business/la-school-strike-growing-trend/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  In April, 30,000 Los Angeles school district custodians}}}\uc2 , bus drivers, cafeteria workers and teacher\u8217\'20\'19s aides struck for three days. They won a 30% raise. Those victories helped embolden LA\u8217\'20\'19s hotel workers to demand a 40% raise.\par \par \uc2 Workers\u8217\'20\'19 attitudes about going on strike have also changed significantly. \u8220\'20\'1CFor many decades, unions wanted to avoid a strike because strikes could mean disaster,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nelson Lichtenstein, a longtime labor historian at the \uc2 University of California, Santa Barbara\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThat was certainly true in the 1980s and 1990s.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In 1981, President Ronald Reagan fired more than 11,000 striking members of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/08/05/1025018833/looking-back-on-when-president-reagan-fired-air-traffic-controllers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Professional Air Traffic Controllers Organization}}}\uc2  (Patco) union in a move that was seen as a devastating blow to organized labor \u8211\'20\'13 it led to that union\u8217\'20\'19s collapse and discouraged other unions from striking.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the wake of the Patco strike, companies saw strikes as opportunities to weaken unions or even break them. That\u8217\'20\'19s not the case today. Today there\u8217\'20\'19s no fear that calling a strike will result in disaster,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lichtenstein.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday there\u8217\'20\'19s a sense that unions are on the offensive,\u8221\'20\'1D Lichtenstein continued. \u8220\'20\'1CTake the actors. They say they don\u8217\'20\'19t want just a good contract. They want a transformative contract.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He said today\u8217\'20\'19s younger generation of workers \u8211\'20\'13 often inspired by Bernie Sanders, often irked about high rents and student debt, often unfamiliar with labor\u8217\'20\'19s setbacks in decades past \u8211\'20\'13 is more inclined to strike than older workers.\par \par \uc2 Several business trends have spurred the strike wave and increased worker anger. Like many companies, \uc2 UPS\uc2  has relied heavily on part-time workers to hold down costs, and many of those workers complain that their limited hours mean they earn far too little. Similarly, television writers increasingly say they\u8217\'20\'19re not being given enough work to live on \u8211\'20\'13 they often used to work on series that had more than 20 episodes a season, but now they often work on series with just six or eight episodes a season. With the explosion of streaming, TV actors are upset that they\u8217\'20\'19re earning far less from residuals than they did in the era before streaming.\par \par \uc2 Corporations are not happy about the increased labor militancy. The Association of Motion Picture and Television Producers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://press.amptp.org/node/19216"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  said it \u8220\'20\'1Coffered historic pay and residual increases\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , adding that the actors\u8217\'20\'19 union, by striking, \u8220\'20\'1Chas regrettably chosen a path that will lead to financial hardship for countless thousands of people who depend on the industry\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Eager to avert a \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  strike, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wdrb.com/news/business/teamsters-seeking-north-of-20-base-hourly-pay-for-ups-workers-zuckerman-says/article_d40dad46-2588-11ee-9346-9b5e27ac9177.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UPS}}}\uc2  agreed to significantly increase wages for full- and part-time workers. The wage gains are double the increases from the union\u8217\'20\'19s previous five-year contract and include a 48% pay raise for part-timers over the life of the contract.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve changed the game \u8230\'20\'26 This contract raises the bar for all workers\par \par \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president Sean O\u8217\'20\'19Brien\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve changed the game, battling it out day and night to make sure our members won an agreement that pays strong wages, rewards their labor and doesn\u8217\'20\'19t require a single concession,\u8221\'20\'1D O\u8217\'20\'19Brien said. He added that \u8220\'20\'1Cthis contract \u8230\'20\'26 raises the bar for all workers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  win is likely to embolden the \uc2 UAW\uc2  as that union considers a strike, too. \u8220\'20\'1CWithout a credible strike threat, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  could not have gotten this much,\u8221\'20\'1D said Joseph McCartin, a labor historian at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1CIn recent memory, we haven\u8217\'20\'19t had three such large labor situations, one following the other, each of which has national implications and each of which could provide momentum for the other. A big strike that moves the needle for workers \u8211\'20\'13 we haven\u8217\'20\'19t seen that in a long while at the national level.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This summer\u8217\'20\'19s strikes come as public approval of unions is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/398303/approval-labor-unions-highest-point-1965.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at its highest since 1965}}}\uc2 , and some labor experts say the strike wave could increase support for labor organizing, even though strikes often inconvenience the public.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/jul/18/hollywood-stars-actors-strike-sag-aftra"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 When these actors go on strike, it has a huge impact}}}\uc2  way beyond their numbers; everyone knows who these people are,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lichtenstein. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s extraordinarily important when a star like Harrison Ford \u8211\'20\'13 3 or 4 billion people know who he is \u8211\'20\'13 says I\u8217\'20\'19m for unions. I back the strike.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He noted that when 185,000 \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  walked out at \uc2 UPS\uc2  for 15 days in 1997, \u8220\'20\'1Cthat was a very popular strike. Everyone knows their \uc2 UPS\uc2  driver.\u8221\'20\'1D He argued that strikes by well-liked \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers and Hollywood celebrities could boost support for labor. Indeed, Lichtenstein said that if the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and \uc2 UAW\uc2  are very successful in their contract negotiations, whether with or without a strike, that could help President Biden and other Democrats in 2024, especially in midwest states, where the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is strongest.\par \par \uc2 More militant union leadership is another catalyst for strikes. Over the past two years, insurgent candidates won the presidency of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , having promised a more confrontational approach in bargaining and a greater willingness to strike.\par \par \uc2 Speaking about the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, Shawn Fain, Michigan State\u8217\'20\'19s Tapia said: \u8220\'20\'1CHe seems to be gearing up the workers to strike. He has said the workers\u8217\'20\'19 true enemy is multibillion-dollar corporations that refuse to give union members their fair share. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders have talked about the significance of strikes not just for their members, but for workers across the whole country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In recent months, unions have shown significantly increased energy both in striking and in organizing, for instance at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWhat these two phenomena make clear is the importance of collective action,\u8221\'20\'1D McCartin said. \u8220\'20\'1CHistorically, to move the needle for workers, they need to engage in collective action.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230726ej7q001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
