\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Summer of discontent: will US strikes spell trouble for \u8216\'20\'18union guy\u8217\'20\'19 Biden?\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,572 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2023\par 11:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Widespread industrial action could cloud president\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18Bidenomics\u8217\'20\'19 pitch to voters but some see a chance to shine as a champion of working people\par \par \uc2 It became known as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winter_of_Discontent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 winter of discontent}}}\uc2 . After the Labour government tried to freeze wages to stem inflation, Britain was convulsed by labour strikes and disruptions in public services. Rubbish piled in the streets, bodies went unburied \u8211\'20\'13 and a fierce political backlash swept \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Conservatives into power.\par \par \uc2 Forty-five years later a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2023/06/27/us-workers-strike-jobs-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 summer of strikes}}}\uc2  is roiling industries from coast to coast in America.\par \par \uc2 Unions have launched or are threatening stoppages that could affect everything from airline travel and parcel deliveries to car manufacturing and film and TV production. They could also disrupt the economic growth that Joe Biden wants to campaign on in 2024.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt takes him off message because strikes are visual, strikes are hot video, and they\u8217\'20\'19re a focal point for media,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://johnzogbystrategies.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Zogby}}}\uc2 , an author and pollster. \u8220\'20\'1CIt becomes lame trying to explain, \u8216\'20\'18But the numbers are good, but the numbers are good, but the numbers are getting better,\u8217\'20\'19 when the video just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t appear to show it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The coronavirus pandemic had many aftershocks and labour turmoil may be among them. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/jul/14/the-hollywood-actors-strike-everything-you-need-to-know"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hollywood production is shut down}}}\uc2  as the Writers Guild and the \uc2 Screen Actors Guild\uc2  are striking, partially over concerns about streaming revenues as well as artificial intelligence taking away jobs from creative workers. The action has put films and TV shows in limbo and could cost the economy an estimated $3bn.\par \par \uc2 There is also the prospect of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/uaw-strike-auto-electric-vehicles-contract-wages-f97630a69a3b62079bd65d3821f9a5d8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a United Auto Workers strike}}}\uc2  as contract talks get under way and the industry wrestles with a transition toward electric vehicles. The \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/ups-strike-teamsters-biden-delivery-cb586d2f6160a92cda9318d6290ac8ea"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its drivers might walk off the job}}}\uc2  as they struggle to reach a new contract with UPS (\uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2 ). And more than 26,000 flight attendants at \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/business/4105255-american-airlines-flight-attendants-move-closer-to-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 set to hold a strike vote}}}\uc2  over the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Among other examples mushrooming across the country, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/business/story/2023-07-20/hotel-workers-third-wave-strikes-hollywood-pasadena"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thousands of hotel workers}}}\uc2  in Los Angeles have also been striking this month while healthcare workers at a major Chicago hospital are planning to do likewise in a dispute over wages and lack of staffing.\par \par \uc2 And last month there were localised walkouts at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , while hundreds of journalists across eight states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/gannett-walkout-journalists-7cb859c29662b0f18e246fd1b56a21bd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went on strike}}}\uc2  to demand an end to painful cost-cutting measures and a change of leadership at Gannett, the country\u8217\'20\'19s biggest newspaper chain.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.drexelheard.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Drexel Heard}}}\uc2 , a public affairs strategist based in Los Angeles, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is what I believe is the start of a trend that was inevitable post-pandemic: workers knowing and understanding that things cannot go back to normal. We all work hybrid now, for the most part.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are understanding that their need for healthcare is something that\u8217\'20\'19s critical. Their need for better pay and better work hours is essential, especially when we have things that happen like a pandemic, and people want to feel safe. The only people who are fighting for workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights are unions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Scenes of industrial strife heading into winter would provide fodder for rightwing media who already accuse Biden of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/feb/27/bernie-sanders-biden-influence-progressives"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 embracing the leftwing ideas}}}\uc2  of Senator Bernie Sanders. It might also create a headache for a president who is focusing much of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/17/biden-re-election-campaign-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his re-election campaign}}}\uc2  on the strength of the economy.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday he was at the Philadelphia Shipyard in Pennsylvania to promote \u8220\'20\'1CBidenomics\u8221\'20\'1D, a recently adopted slogan. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/speeches-remarks/2023/07/20/remarks-by-president-biden-on-bidenomics-philadelphia-pa/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The president said}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a plan that\u8217\'20\'19s turning things around pretty quickly. \u8216\'20\'18Bidenomics\u8217\'20\'19 is just another way of saying \u8216\'20\'18Restore the American Dream\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But that message is still struggling to break through with voters. In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2023/07/20/bidens-economic-approval-rating-rises-slightly-but-is-still-just-37percent-despite-bidenomics-push.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNBC All-America Economic Survey}}}\uc2  released this week, 37% approve of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s handling of the economy and 58% disapprove. In a \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll, only three in 10 Americans feel the country is doing a better job recovering economically than the rest of the world since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 There is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2023/jul/10/america-economy-robert-reich"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a baffling disconnect}}}\uc2  between these opinions and data that shows America defying predictions of recession and curbing price rises faster than other major economies. Inflation has fallen from 9% to 3% and is now at its lowest point in more than two years.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Biden may have helped create the very conditions that make strikes more likely. White House officials say that unions are empowered to press for more benefits and better pay because of the strong job market. Unemployment is just 3.6% and job openings are relatively high.\par \par \uc2 This is one reason why {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/robert-reich"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Robert Reich}}}\uc2 , a former labour secretary under President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , does not believe that the current wave of strikes and potential strikes will overwhelm Biden\u8217\'20\'19s effort to highlight economic growth.\par \par \uc2 He explained in an email: \u8220\'20\'1C(1) the strikes and potential strikes still represent a tiny segment of the American workforce, (2) overall job gains and wage gains continue to roar, (3) a big reason workers feel able to strike is that the labor market continues to be tight, which is another good sign for Biden.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reich, a professor of public policy at the \uc2 University of California at Berkeley\uc2  and a Guardian US columnist, added: \u8220\'20\'1CThe potential problem for Biden isn\u8217\'20\'19t the wave of strikes and potential strikes but the seeming determination of the Fed to continue to raise interest rates, thereby risking a recession before Election Day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Widespread industrial action would pose a fresh test for Biden, a self-proclaimed \u8220\'20\'1Cunion guy\u8221\'20\'1D born in blue-collar Scranton, Pennsylvania. Past attempts to intervene in such disputes have not always gone smoothly.\par \par \uc2 Last year his administration helped forge a tentative agreement between rail companies and their unionised workers to avoid a strike that could have rocked the economy before the midterm elections. The tentative deal prevented a strike but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/nov/30/us-rail-strike-unions-decry-biden-proposal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failed to appease workers}}}\uc2 , and Congress ultimately had to intervene by imposing an agreement.\par \par \uc2 Biden, who is pushing the Senate to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2023/07/20/julie-su-confirmation-vote-record"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 confirm Julie Su}}}\uc2  as his new labour secretary, has already expressed support for the striking Hollywood actors and writers, insisting that all workers deserve fair pay and benefits. Such an approach could work to his advantage against Republicans seeking to rebrand themselves as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/04/13/986549868/top-republicans-work-to-rebrand-gop-as-party-of-working-class"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the party of the working class}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Faiz Shakir\uc2 , chief political adviser to Sanders and executive director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://perfectunion.us/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More Perfect Union}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1681110286047105024?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who met with Biden}}}\uc2  and young labour organisers at the White House this week, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you think about some of the working-class people who are in the swing voter category, they tend to carry an anger and frustration about an economy that hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been working for them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt would be wrong to go back to them and tell them, \u8216\'20\'18Hey, everything is much better since I was president.\u8217\'20\'19 I think you want to say, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m fighting for you and I\u8217\'20\'19m improving the situation for you. However, there\u8217\'20\'19s way more work to do and the people who are standing in the way are these corporate bosses, and I\u8217\'20\'19m taking them on from you.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In the UPS dispute, Shakir argues, Biden should make clear he stands with the workers. \u8220\'20\'1CStand boldly with those workers. Say, \u8216\'20\'18I stand with you. You want to go on strike? That\u8217\'20\'19s fine. Yes, there will be costs to consumers, yes, there will be some challenges in the economy, but your work is essential and important and you deserve your fair share.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat I think will end up happening is you usually get some criticism from the business elite who are going to say, why is the president of the United States siding with these workers and making it even harder for these people to reach a negotiated outcome? To that, I say that is good for politics, because when working people see that you\u8217\'20\'19re sticking your neck out for them, they will reward you. They see you taking arrows for them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shakir also believes that the dispute will be resolved in a few weeks with a positive outcome for labour, and that Biden will be able to justifiably claim that he helped improve the lot of hundreds of thousands of workers.\par \par \uc2 In his remarks in Philadelphia on Thursday, Biden was again careful to align himself with workers and unions against Wall Street and companies that made record-high profits during the pandemic. If a disruptive wave of strikes comes to pass, this is likely to be the least risky strategy.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/people/william-a-galston/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bill Galston}}}\uc2 , a senior fellow at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  thinktank, said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s my distinct impression that support for labour unions has gone up significantly so we\u8217\'20\'19re not talking about [British miners\u8217\'20\'19 leader] Arthur Scargill in the 1970s. We\u8217\'20\'19re talking about an extended period during which a lot of Americans believe that workers have got the short end of the stick.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re much less worried about \u8216\'20\'18big labour\u8217\'20\'19 than they used to be, in part because labour isn\u8217\'20\'19t as big as used to be, especially in the private sector where labour unions have weakened enormously. There\u8217\'20\'19s a basic sense of fair play operating to increase support for not just working people but organised labour, so I don\u8217\'20\'19t think this is a bad time to strike and I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that will necessarily redound to Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s discredit.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230723ej7n000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
