\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Bargaining for our very existence\u8217\'20\'19: why the battle over AI is being fought in Hollywood\b0\par\par\uc2 Lois Beckett in Los Angeles and Kari Paul in Oakland \par \uc2 1,928 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2023\par 12:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ramifications of artificial intelligence are of concern to the actors and writers on strike \u8211\'20\'13 from big stars to bit players\par \par \uc2 To get her start in Hollywood, Chivonne Michelle studied acting at \uc2 New York University\uc2 . But what helped her break into the industry and gave her the key training she needed was working on set as a background actor.\par \par \uc2 Today, the rise of artificial intelligence (AI) technology threatens to put those \u8220\'20\'1Centry-level and working-class\u8221\'20\'1D Hollywood jobs at risk, Michelle and other striking actors say.\par \par \uc2 AI is threatening jobs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jun/03/ai-danger-doomsday-chatgpt-robots-fears"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 across many sectors}}}\uc2 , from doctors and lawyers to data scientists and journalists. But Hollywood actors and writers, currently united in their first \u8220\'20\'1Cdouble strike\u8221\'20\'1D in more than 60 years, are fighting back in an unprecedented way, vowing to protect every worker in their industry, from the extras to the stars, from being replaced by new technologies. The dual strike by the \uc2 Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists\uc2  (Sag-Aftra) and the Writers Guild of America (WGA) have forced the major studios to halt production, resulting in a standoff that is expected to drag on for months.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re forcing us to negotiate and bargain for our very existence\par \par \uc2 Zeke Alton of the Sag-Aftra negotiating committee\par \par \uc2 On the picket lines outside \uc2 Netflix\uc2  this week, one \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2  member after another described being \u8220\'20\'1Cfired up\u8221\'20\'1D about the potential threats AI poses to their jobs.\par \par \uc2 If actors don\u8217\'20\'19t win serious protections against being replaced by AI, \u8220\'20\'1Cit ends the profession,\u8221\'20\'1D Zeke Alton, a member of the Sag-Aftra negotiating committee, said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re forcing us to negotiate and bargain for our very existence.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Actors are also very aware that the battle between workers and bosses over AI and other new technologies does not end with them, he added. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen our very recognizable members speak, the whole world tends to listen, and we\u8217\'20\'19re aware of that. UPS is having issues, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is having issues, teachers are having issues, nurses are having issues. We have a really loud voice at \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2 , and we\u8217\'20\'19re speaking for everyone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Why here? Why now?\par \par \uc2 It should not be a surprise that the battle lines over labor and AI are being drawn in Hollywood, said Jennifer Coates, a partner at law firm \uc2 Dorsey & Whitney\uc2  who specializes in tech-related cases.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis has come to such a head in the entertainment industry because it has to do with something so fundamentally human, which is creativity,\u8221\'20\'1D Coates said. \u8220\'20\'1CCan there really be an AI-generated version of Marlon Brando in The Godfather? Can you create that with a computer, or do you need to understand something about human beings, about human expression?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hollywood artists may be also benefiting from more public empathy than previous disrupted workers have received. The disruptions of new AI technology have been \u8220\'20\'1Cmoving up the economic and labor food chain\u8221\'20\'1D, Coates said, from laborers and people working in manufacturing plants, to the legal profession, \u8220\'20\'1Cto teaching, to now acting, to writers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Can there really be an AI-generated version of Marlon Brando in The Godfather? Can you create that with a computer?\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Coates of \uc2 Dorsey & Whitney\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople did not consider the jobs of people working on a factory line, jobs that were replaced by robots, to be creative,\u8221\'20\'1D Coates said. \u8220\'20\'1CI think the people on the line may argue differently. But there\u8217\'20\'19s no argument that what an actor does is creative.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The actors\u8217\'20\'19 concerns around AI are multifold. Celebrities are increasingly targets of deepfake technology, which can use their voices and likeness for everything from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/GMA/Culture/video/deep-fake-advertisements-push-fake-celebrity-endorsements-92114229"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advertisements}}}\uc2 , to shockingly believable {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/2023/apr/18/ai-song-featuring-fake-drake-and-weeknd-vocals-pulled-from-streaming-services"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sham songs}}}\uc2 , to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/social-media/emma-watson-deep-fake-scarlett-johansson-face-swap-app-rcna73624"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pornography}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A major flashpoint of the first week of the actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike was a comment from Duncan Crabtree-Ireland, \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief negotiator, who said that studios had \u8220\'20\'1Cproposed that our background performers should be able to be scanned, get paid for one day\u8217\'20\'19s pay, and their company should own that scan, their image, their likeness, and to be able to use it for rest of eternity, on any project they want, with no consent and no compensation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a description the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  (\uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ) has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://labor.amptp.org/node/19219"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disputed}}}\uc2 , calling it a \u8220\'20\'1Cmischaracterization\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThe union has distorted the facts in an effort to garner support for its work stoppage,\u8221\'20\'1D the producers said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  said in a statement that its current AI proposal would require \u8220\'20\'1Cadvance, specific consent\u8221\'20\'1D to both create and use digital replicas of performers, including background actors, and that the contract would prohibit later use of the replica unless performers consent to the new use and are paid for it, and that they would also ban \u8220\'20\'1Cdigital alterations\u8221\'20\'1D of performances beyond \u8220\'20\'1Ctraditional editing/post-production practices\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2  did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Actors on the picket line said what Crabtree-Ireland had described was outrageous, and that replacing human extras with digital scans could also lead to cuts of other on-set jobs, like makeup and costume artists.\par \par \uc2 They are also concerned about consent \u8211\'20\'13 worried that directors and producers could do things to a performer\u8217\'20\'19s digital replica, like putting them onscreen naked, or making them play a racist or offensive role, that a human actor can currently refuse to do \u8211\'20\'13 like the Black Mirror episode Joan Is Awful {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2023/jul/13/joan-is-awful-black-mirror-striking-actors-nightmare"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 become reality}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Justine Bateman, an actor, writer and director who has become a prominent AI critic, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/p/CusUSz7Skr-/?hl=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote on social media}}}\uc2  that the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  had not only wanted to own background actors\u8217\'20\'19 likenesses \u8220\'20\'1Cforever\u8221\'20\'1D, but that the studios had also \u8220\'20\'1Cwanted to feed 100 years of acting performances (for a nominal fee)\u8221\'20\'1D into AI models \u8220\'20\'1Cso all our work could be frankensteined into \u8216\'20\'18new\u8217\'20\'19 AI characters\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Several actors on the picket line outside \uc2 Netflix\uc2  on Tuesday said they had not yet faced the decision of whether or not to be digitally scanned while working on a film, but said they were concerned about it.\par \par \uc2 Ja\u8217\'20\'19Quan Cole, who worked as a background actor before getting larger roles in Winning Time, Wu-Tang: An American Saga and Snowfall, said he feared that AI would become so ubiquitous in music and film that in 10 years younger people would no longer be able to tell the difference between an artificial voice and a human voice.\par \par \uc2 At the moment, it tends to be major actors on film sets, and the production\u8217\'20\'19s background actors, who are most likely to be asked for digital scans on film sets, said Alton, who was part of the union contract negotiations that have sparked the actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike.\par \par \uc2 Background actors have become a focus of AI concerns because they are an obvious target of cost-cutting measures, said Chris Gomes Muffat, an AI expert and founder of AI-powered content creation tool Promptify.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a movie, you have a lot of people around the protagonist who do not do anything but be part of the scene, without dialogue. AI can easily replace and populate people in the background artificially. It\u8217\'20\'19s cheaper to do that than to manage and pay 100 people,\u8221\'20\'1D Gomes Muffat said.\par \par \uc2 Already, on sets, \u8220\'20\'1Cbackground actors are run through the scanning truck and are told in the moment, \u8216\'20\'18You\u8217\'20\'19re just going to do this,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Alton said. \u8220\'20\'1COur principal performers are being told, \u8216\'20\'18In the event that you die, we need to scan you and have a model of you, a la Paul Walker in The Fast and the Furious.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In both cases, these requests are happening outside the purview of \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s standard contract \u8211\'20\'13 putting actors in precisely the kinds of unequal negotiating positions that unions are designed to prevent, Alton said.\par \par \uc2 Alton himself said he refused to be scanned around 2018, when he was a background actor on the set of Wonder Woman 1984 in Virginia, along with an estimated 350 other background performers. \u8220\'20\'1CIt didn\u8217\'20\'19t smell right to me,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Is the threat of AI overhyped?\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say that the union rhetoric around AI is overhyped.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAI is so new, people are just throwing the term around while the vast majority of them do not fully understand what AI can and cannot do at this point. So actors are throwing it in as another concern, when in my judgment, it\u8217\'20\'19s just a red herring,\u8221\'20\'1D said Steve Schiffman, an adjunct professor at Georgetown University\u8217\'20\'19s McDonough School of Business.\par \par \uc2 Schiffman said he blamed studio management for having \u8220\'20\'1Cdone a really bad job\u8221\'20\'1D of explaining the crisis in industry revenue over the past decade, as film and TV have increasingly shifted to online streaming services, and having \u8220\'20\'1Cdone an even worse job explaining AI\u8221\'20\'1D, leaving workers \u8220\'20\'1Cscared\u8221\'20\'1D and emotions \u8220\'20\'1Cvery high\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Because technology in the film industry has advanced so quickly in the past decades, Paul Barrett, deputy director of the NYU Stern Center for Business and Human Rights, said, \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople are extrapolating from the experiences they\u8217\'20\'19ve already had to a future where the sheer amount of human participation in art is going to be reduced and machine participation is going to be maximized.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Other activists say that AI technology is being overhyped and misunderstood \u8211\'20\'13 but argue that it is the studio executives and Wall Street investors who are failing to understand what the new technology can and cannot do.\par \par \uc2 The media frenzy over AI this year is the result of tech industry marketing, said Adam Conover, a comedian and member of the writers\u8217\'20\'19 union negotiating committee. A wide variety of technologies, many of which aren\u8217\'20\'19t actually \u8220\'20\'1Cartificial intelligence\u8221\'20\'1D or even that new, are now being marketed under the sexy, investor-friendly label of \u8220\'20\'1CAI\u8221\'20\'1D, he said.\par \par \uc2 Having failed in recent years to create the \u8220\'20\'1Cnew disruptive innovations\u8221\'20\'1D that its investors demand, the tech industry has started to \u8220\'20\'1Cpropose a new technology that they simply claim is going to change everything, and a lot of time it isn\u8217\'20\'19t even coherent\u8221\'20\'1D, Conover said.\par \par \uc2 While some of the AI scenarios that actors fear may not be happening yet, Alton, the Sag-Aftra negotiating committee member, said the technology appears to be moving fast enough that he believes that within the terms of the actors\u8217\'20\'19 next contract, which lasts for three years, \u8220\'20\'1Call of this stuff is going to be possible\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Union contract protects stars and bit players alike\par \par \uc2 One reason the actors\u8217\'20\'19 union is uniquely positioned in the labor dispute over AI is that A-list celebrities are bound by the same union contract protections as extras working for a day rate, said Barrett, a configuration that strengthens the solidarity between workers with more and less power.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are not many industries that have the experience of having its whole economic framework that applies to everybody, from the best-paid stars to entry-level performers, negotiated at once,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Extra jobs may not seem that important to studio executives, or even to audiences, but these low-level positions serve a crucial role within the profession, as a place where actors can get their first experience of working on a film set, network with each other and even get discovered.\par \par \uc2 Outside \uc2 Netflix\uc2  on Tuesday, Elizabeth Oliver saw the raucous pro-union chants on the picket line as a sign of how willing actors are to support the rights of everyone in their profession. \u8220\'20\'1CI hope people making their living as background actors and workaday actors feel supported,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230722ej7m0010c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
