\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18You really can\u8217\'20\'19t make a living any more\u8217\'20\'19: Hollywood actors join writers in strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Lois Beckett in Los Angeles \par \uc2 1,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2023\par 17:08 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Joint strike by the two unions at the heart of the US television and film industry aims to force studios to share profits\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been a hot, frustrating summer on the picket lines in Los Angeles, where film and TV writers have been striking for more than two months, no deal in sight.\par \par \uc2 But outside of the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  building on Sunset Boulevard on Thursday, the striking writers finally got some news they wanted to hear. \u8220\'20\'1CSag is going on strike!\u8221\'20\'1D someone called, and the crowd of picketing writers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sdunnewold/status/1679580786574626816"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 roared their approval}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists\uc2  (Sag-Aftra), whose 160,000 members include some of the most famous performers in the world, had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/13/hollywood-actors-union-recommends-strike-as-talks-deadline-passes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 officially announced}}}\uc2  its members would be joining Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s 11,500 writers on the picket lines outside \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 , and other major film and TV studios.\par \par \uc2 But it\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , and fellow streaming disruptors like \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , that are at the heart of the growing labor disputes that have now brought the film industry to a virtual standstill.\par \par \uc2 Both writers and actors say that technological changes in the industry are forcing them to negotiate hard for major changes in their contracts. The new era of digital streaming has led to a dramatic decrease in how much money they make, they say. The rise of new artificial intelligence technologies is only adding to their concern.\par \par \uc2 You really can\u8217\'20\'19t make a living any more. It\u8217\'20\'19s a constant hustle and it\u8217\'20\'19s very demoralizing\par \par \uc2 Actor Felicia Day\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou really can\u8217\'20\'19t make a living any more,\u8221\'20\'1D said actor Felicia Day, who has been working in the industry for more than two decades. Day, who showed up at the Sag-Aftra headquarters for the official announcement of the strike on this sweltering Thursday, said she gets bigger checks for television residuals for roles she played back in 2004 than she does for much more recent performances. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are having a harder and harder time just supporting themselves and staying in the industry,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 As pay for individual roles declines, Day said, actors have to secure twice as much work just to make rent \u8211\'20\'13 even though auditioning for and obtaining roles has not become any easier. A few years ago, four to five guest star roles on television shows would be enough to get an actor through a year, she said. Now, it takes double that number of roles to make the same amount of money: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a constant hustle and it\u8217\'20\'19s very demoralizing,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s striking writers have made similar arguments, pointing to television writing contracts that have become shorter and more precarious, a development they argue is increasingly turning writers into gig workers.\par \par \uc2 Inside Sag-Aftra\u8217\'20\'19s headquarters, union president Fran Drescher made it clear that actors were fed up and fired up, saying, \u8220\'20\'1CWe are the victims,\u8221\'20\'1D and telling studio executives: \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re sitting on the wrong side of history.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The star of The Nanny, a sitcom from the 1990s about a wealthy Broadway producer whose family is rescued by a straight-talking woman from Queens, Drescher is now being hailed as a labor hero for her blunt denunciations of studio executives.\par \par \uc2 The Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers had released a statement ahead of the formal strike announcement blaming the actors\u8217\'20\'19 union for the breakdown of negotiations, saying it had offered actors \u8220\'20\'1Chistoric pay and residual increases\u8221\'20\'1D and that the union\u8217\'20\'19s refusal would lead to widespread economic suffering among its members.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a level of expectation that they have, that is just not realistic,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/bob-iger-wga-sag-aftra-strike-contract-extension-interview-1235535185/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Disney CEO Bob Iger told CNBC}}}\uc2  in an interview on Thursday, speaking of both writers and actors. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd they are adding to the set of the challenges that this business is already facing that is, quite frankly, very disruptive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some industry analysts have argued that the transition to digital streaming has left Hollywood as a whole in a shaky financial situation. \u8220\'20\'1CThe studios are in crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D Kim Masters, the editor-at-large for The Hollywood Reporter, told NPR in advance of the strike. \u8220\'20\'1CStreaming has really hurt them. They haven\u8217\'20\'19t figured out how to make money on it except \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , finally, after a lot of investment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But during a week in which anonymous studio sources told Deadline that their plan for the writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike was to delay further negotiations until the fall, when they thought {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2023/07/writers-strike-hollywood-studios-deal-fight-wga-actors-1235434335/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writers would be out of money and at risk of losing their homes and apartments}}}\uc2 , many Hollywood workers don\u8217\'20\'19t have much sympathy for the financial struggles of the studios. Iger\u8217\'20\'19s reported compensation at \uc2 Disney\uc2  is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/bob-iger-disney-new-contract-details-chapek-severence-1235266921/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $27m per year for two years}}}\uc2 . His predecessor, \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2 , was forced out but reportedly received {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/bob-chapek-disney-pay-bob-iger-1235285478/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a $20m severance deal}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Drescher, the Sag-Aftra president, did not dispute that a prolonged, dual strike by Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s actors and writers would lead to economic hardship for the workers on strike and many other Angelenos affected by the fallout of halted productions, studio cutbacks and belt-tightening by tens of thousands of workers on strike.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur hearts bleed that we had to make this decision, but we can\u8217\'20\'19t not get what these members deserve, because it\u8217\'20\'19s only going to get worse,\u8221\'20\'1D Drescher said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe were forced into it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Are you going to take my image and my voice, replicate that, and then I\u8217\'20\'19m expendable?\par \par \uc2 Actor Danny Hogan\par \par \uc2 Outside Sag-Aftra headquarters, Danny Hogan, an actor who lives in New Jersey, said he had flown to Los Angeles to be on the ground during a historic moment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince 1960, WGA and Sag have not been united like this,\u8221\'20\'1D Hogan said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are at a crisis point. Most actors want to have this as their full-time work. It seems like studios are forcing people to do this as a hobby more than a job.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 With artificial intelligence, he added, \u8220\'20\'1CAre you going to take my image and my voice, replicate that, and then I\u8217\'20\'19m expendable?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After trying to build a career for 17 years, Hogan said, he\u8217\'20\'19s finally been seeing momentum in his career, with recent roles in several Lifetime movies and on NCIS: Hawaii. The strike will halt that momentum, he said, but he still voted to support it: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s an unfortunate necessity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cour job, as actors, to be focused and present\u8221\'20\'1D, Hogan said, and for now, he sees that job not on a set, but on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230714ej7e001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
