\par\par \uc2 Global development\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Suit up for the backlash!\u8217\'20\'19: The battle to regulate New York\u8217\'20\'19s gig economy\b0\par\par\uc2 Naaman Zhou \par \uc2 1,416 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2023\par 14:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The progressive politician Brad Lander says his attempts to establish a minimum wage for food delivery workers have met well-funded resistance from the sector\u8217\'20\'19s leading companies\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, Brad Lander, arguably the second most powerful official in New York City, rode a bike six times around the perimeter of City Hall. Inside, his colleagues were debating a bill to institute a minimum wage for food delivery riders. Lander, now comptroller of New York, had joined a convoy of delivery drivers in support of a protest for basic rights and fair pay.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey did six times around because there were six pieces of legislation,\u8221\'20\'1D Lander says, who as comptroller oversees New York\u8217\'20\'19s mayoral budget. \u8220\'20\'1CI thought it should have been seven, because that\u8217\'20\'19s how many times they went around Jericho in the Bible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In June, New York became the first US city to establish a permanent minimum hourly wage for food delivery workers. Lander was the lead sponsor of the originating bill, which he first wrote in 2021 as a council member, before he was elected comptroller.\par \par \uc2 Change has come slowly. The new wage law was supposed to come into effect on 1 January this year, but was delayed for six months \u8211\'20\'13 which Lander attributes to gig companies\u8217\'20\'19 lobbying. The proposed rate was also lowered: from $23.82 (\u163\'00\'A318.40) an hour by 2025, to $19.96 by 2025.\par \par \uc2 But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://iapps.courts.state.ny.us/nyscef/ViewDocument?docIndex=yFHEtQJwD89IH8k5ZI_PLUS_0pw=="}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last Thursday}}}\uc2 , days before the new wage took effect, four gig work companies \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Uber\uc2 , \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  and Relay \u8211\'20\'13 sued the city of New York to block the new law. Manhattan supreme court judge Nicholas Moyne temporarily delayed the start of the landmark minimum wage. It is now unclear when it will come into effect.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou got to know that the gig companies are going to come strong against this,\u8221\'20\'1D Lander told the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  on the day the lawsuit was announced. Outside, it was hot and muggy. New York had seen a string of 30C days; a few weeks earlier, the city\u8217\'20\'19s delivery riders had worked through heavy smoke and smog. That morning, Lander had found out about the lawsuit through the press. His advice to other legislators or politicians who are trying to regulate the gig economy is \u8220\'20\'1Csuit up for the backlash\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re skilled political players,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CThey make a lot of campaign contributions. They hire the right lobbyists. They threaten that the service will collapse or they won\u8217\'20\'19t stay in New York City.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lander, a progressive who was endorsed in the comptroller race by \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , Elizabeth Warren and Bernie Sanders, says that even the announced wage was lower than it should be. His office {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://comptroller.nyc.gov/newsroom/comptroller-lander-criticizes-adams-administrations-6-months-delayed-deliverista-subminimum-wage-weakened-by-app-corporate-lobbying/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has estimated}}}\uc2  that the take-home pay for delivery riders will start out at only $12.69 an hour, rather than $19.96. This shortfall is in part due to a two-year \u8220\'20\'1Cphase-in\u8221\'20\'1D period that knocks the hourly rate down by $2 to $17.96 \u8211\'20\'13 but also because of a provision that gig workers will be paid $3.60 less an hour if they engage in \u8220\'20\'1Cmulti-apping\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 using more than one app at a time to find work.\par \par \uc2 Lander describes the concept of \u8220\'20\'1Cmulti-apping\u8221\'20\'1D as \u8220\'20\'1Cmade up\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CI do struggle to explain why it\u8217\'20\'19s total bullshit,\u8221\'20\'1D he says, \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause once they make up regulatory words, it sounds like it makes sense. But it\u8217\'20\'19s just total bullshit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no time when more than one app is paying a worker. That never happens. When you\u8217\'20\'19re on a trip, one app is paying for you. They used the idea of multi-apping to just cut the hourly rate by three bucks an hour. It\u8217\'20\'19s a fancy way to pay their workers less.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Vilda Vera Mayuga, the head of New York\u8217\'20\'19s Department of Consumer and Worker Protection, which determined the $17.96 rate, says she is \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely disappointed\u8221\'20\'1D at the gig companies\u8217\'20\'19 lawsuit. \u8220\'20\'1CThese apps currently pay workers far below the minimum wage,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  says it was \u8220\'20\'1Cpleased\u8221\'20\'1D with the decision to delay the law. An \uc2 Uber\uc2  spokesperson says the company wants to \u8220\'20\'1Cfigure out a minimum pay rule that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have devastating consequences for couriers, consumers and restaurants\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We were saying these are essential workers, but just not protecting them from harm and not treating them with dignity\par \par \uc2 Brad Lander\par \par \uc2 The political will to improve delivery riders\u8217\'20\'19 lives, Lander says, came largely from the pandemic. At the start of 2020, riders were often not even allowed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/sep/23/gig-economy-doordash-grubhub-food-delivery"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 use the bathrooms}}}\uc2  of the restaurants they were delivering from.\par \par \uc2 Los Deliveristas Unidos, a workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights group for delivery riders formed during the pandemic, delivered a series of proposed reforms to politicians including Lander, a city council member at the time. Initially, the riders did not imagine a minimum wage was possible. \u8220\'20\'1CThey had an initial package of legislation to just confront the worst abuses,\u8221\'20\'1D Lander says.\par \par \uc2 But in 2019, New York had already established a minimum wage law for rideshare drivers, in which Lander had been involved. This became a model for the food delivery riders who, in the pandemic, were becoming essential.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of delivery riders have also been killed at work. They have been hit by cars, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abc7ny.com/funeral-e-bike-robbery-miah-sala-food-delivery-worker/11145190/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stabbed}}}\uc2 , assaulted and shot. In 2021, Francisco Villalva Vitinio, a 29-year-old {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gothamist.com/news/after-delivery-cyclist-killed-armed-robbery-gig-economy-workers-say-enough-enough"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  migrant from Mexico}}}\uc2 , was murdered by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amny.com/news/killer-east-harlem-delivery-worker-sentenced-april-27-2023/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 someone who was trying to steal his e-bike}}}\uc2 . Villalva Vitinio had moved to the US 10 years earlier, and had worked in a restaurant until he lost his job at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gothamist.com/news/after-delivery-cyclist-killed-armed-robbery-gig-economy-workers-say-enough-enough"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  start of the pandemic}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lander and the future mayor, Eric Adams, attended a funeral service and protest for Villalva Vitinio, and Lander remembers it being a turning point. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was so much grief in the community,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CReal agony and grief over this loss, but also just anger \u8211\'20\'13 we were saying these are essential workers, but just not protecting them from real physical harm and death, and not paying or treating them with dignity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Gig companies have claimed that the new law will disrupt the food delivery in New York, and force higher prices on to consumers. A spokesperson for \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , Eli Scheinholtz, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecity.nyc/2023/7/6/23786444/doordash-grubhub-uber-sue-delivery-minimum-wage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  says the }}}\uc2  wage was an \u8220\'20\'1Cextreme earnings standard\u8221\'20\'1D that would have \u8220\'20\'1Charmful and lasting impacts \u8230\'20\'26 that resulted from a fundamentally broken process\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Lander says that New York\u8217\'20\'19s minimum wage for rideshare drivers was also delayed by a court challenge, from \uc2 Lyft\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CBut it finally got implemented,\u8221\'20\'1D he says, \u8220\'20\'1Cand it\u8217\'20\'19s worked great. Drivers make several thousand more dollars a year, can earn a living wage, and can feed their families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The next step, he says, is to roll out minimum wage laws to other cities. Lander is part of a national network of progressive elected officials called Local Progress.\par \par \uc2 But he admits it can be hard to hold on to regulatory changes in the face of lobbying. In 2019, the California legislature passed a bill classifying rideshare drivers as employees, but it was overturned by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/04/california-election-voters-prop-22-uber-lyft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballot measure Proposition 22, a referendum in which Uber, Lyft}}}\uc2  and other rideshare and food delivery apps such as \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  spent about $200m campaigning. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  bought the referendum,\u8221\'20\'1D Lander says. \u8220\'20\'1CEven if you do win hearts and minds, these gains can be overturned.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But in New York, Lander and other politicians are not yet done. There is a bill before the city to grant sick leave to gig workers, written by Lander\u8217\'20\'19s successor on the council, Shahana Hanif.\par \par \uc2 Lander is also supporting a bill from council member Tiffany Cab\u225\'00\'E1n called the Secure Jobs Act, which would protect employees from unfair dismissal. He wants to see similar protections for gig workers, who can often be barred from the apps they use to find work due to poor ratings or factors that he says are opaque and obscure.\par \par \uc2 Due to the legal action, New York\u8217\'20\'19s minimum wage is still on hold. But even in its current state, Lander says, the law doesn\u8217\'20\'19t go far enough. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is still, on average, a sub-minimum wage \u8230\'20\'26 and even that\u8217\'20\'19s not enough for [the gig companies],\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CHaving captured the relevant regulatory process, cut workers hourly wages by about four bucks an hour, they\u8217\'20\'19re still suing to overturn it altogether. That\u8217\'20\'19s not surprising. Their whole model is built on exploiting their workforce. That\u8217\'20\'19s what gig work largely is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230713ej7d0010f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
