\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: Hollywood actors\u8217\'20\'19 union recommends strike as talks deadline passes\b0\par\par\uc2 Nicola Slawson \par \uc2 1,843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2023\par 11:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sag-Aftra committee decision raises prospect of first simultaneous strike by writers and actors in more than 60 years. Plus, a surfing sea otter leaves experts puzzledDon\u8217\'20\'19t already get First Thing in your inbox? Sign up here\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Due to an error, Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s First Thing was not sent out. We apologize for the inconvenience.\par \par \uc2 The union representing Hollywood actors has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/13/hollywood-actors-union-recommends-strike-as-talks-deadline-passes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recommended strike action}}}\uc2  after a midnight negotiation deadline passed with no agreement. The industry is bracing for the possibility of the first simultaneous strike by Hollywood writers and actors in more than 60 years.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the \uc2 Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists\uc2  (Sag-Aftra), the union that represents 160,000 actors, said its negotiating committee had voted unanimously to recommend a strike. The national board will decide this morning.\par \par \uc2 It said: \u8220\'20\'1CAfter more than four weeks of bargaining, the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  (\uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ) \u8211\'20\'13 the entity that represents major studios and streamers, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , Paramount, \uc2 Sony\uc2  and \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 remains unwilling to offer a fair deal on the key issues that are essential to Sag-Aftra members.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The statement said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe companies have refused to meaningfully engage on some topics and on others completely stonewalled us. Until they do negotiate in good faith, we cannot begin to reach a deal. We have no choice but to move forward in unity, and on behalf of our membership, with a strike recommendation to our national board. The board will discuss the issue this morning and will make its decision.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What impact would the strike have? A strike is expected to have an immediate impact on publicity efforts for the summer\u8217\'20\'19s top films; this evening\u8217\'20\'19s premiere of \uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2  in London has been moved to start an hour earlier so the cast can attend regardless of the outcome. Other major commercial films, including Barbie and Mission: Impossible \u8211\'20\'13 Dead Reckoning Part One, have already hosted their world premieres, though their stars will be restricted from participating in further promotional events. The strike might also delay the Emmy awards until late autumn, or even next year, industry publications reported.\par \par \uc2 Russian spy chief confirms call to CIA director after Wagner revolt\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s foreign intelligence chief, Sergei Naryshkin, has said that he and his CIA counterpart discussed the short-lived mutiny by the Russian mercenary boss \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  and \u8220\'20\'1Cwhat to do with Ukraine\u8221\'20\'1D in a phone call late last month.\par \par \uc2 Sergei Naryshkin, the head of the SVR foreign intelligence service, told Russia\u8217\'20\'19s Tass new agency yesterday that Bill Burns had raised \u8220\'20\'1Cthe events of June 24\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 when fighters from the Wagner mercenary group took control of a southern Russian city and advanced towards Moscow before reaching a deal with the Kremlin to end the revolt.\par \par \uc2 But he said that for most of the call, lasting about an hour, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe considered and discussed what to do with Ukraine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The CIA declined to comment on his remarks.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and Wall Street Journal reported on 30 June that William Burns had called Naryshkin to assure the Kremlin that the United States had no role in the Wagner revolt.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What has Ukraine said? Ukraine, which was invaded by Russia in February 2022, says other countries should not negotiate its future on its behalf, and the US has repeatedly backed this principle, which it describes as \u8220\'20\'1Cnothing about Ukraine without Ukraine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Are Burns and Naryshkin talking regularly? The pair {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/nov/14/cia-director-meets-russian-counterpart-as-us-denies-secret-peace-talks-ukraine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have maintained a line of communication since the start of the Ukraine war}}}\uc2 , at a time when other direct contacts between Moscow and Washington are at a minimum, with relations at their lowest point since the 1962 Cuban missile crisis.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans grill \uc2 FBI\uc2  director as Democrats deride attacks on agency\par \par \uc2 House Republicans grilled the director of the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2 , \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/12/fbi-director-christopher-wray-testifies-house-judiciary-committee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 frequently contentious committee}}}\uc2  hearing yesterday. While Republicans accused the \uc2 FBI\uc2  of political bias in its handling of investigations into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s son, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , Democrats derided the attacks on the bureau as a smokescreen driven by conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 The Republican chair of the House judiciary committee, Jim Jordan of Ohio, kicked off the hearing with a litany of complaints about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s alleged targeting of rightwing leaders and activists, lamenting the supposed \u8220\'20\'1Cdouble standard that exists now in our justice system\u8221\'20\'1D. Jordan suggested that the allegedly misguided leadership of Wray, a Trump appointee, could jeopardize government funding for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s planned new headquarters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hope [Democrats] will work with us in the appropriations process to stop the weaponization of the government against the American people,\u8221\'20\'1D Jordan said in his opening statement.\par \par \uc2 The top Democrat on the committee, \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  of New York, countered Jordan\u8217\'20\'19s allegations by accusing Republicans of acting as Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attack dog at the expense of Americans\u8217\'20\'19 safety. Last month, Trump was indicted on 37 federal counts, including 31 violations of the Espionage Act, over allegations that he intentionally withheld classified documents from federal authorities.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What did Nadler say? \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans may want to downplay Trump\u8217\'20\'19s behavior and blame the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for his downfall. But no matter what they say, Trump risked the safety and security of the United States to remove those documents from the White House, then lied to the government instead of returning them,\u8221\'20\'1D Nadler said. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  must be held accountable, and attempts to shield him from the consequences of his own actions are both transparent and despicable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The former chaplain of a Roman Catholic high school in Louisiana has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/12/louisiana-catholic-chaplain-sentenced-sexual-abuse"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pleaded guilty to molesting two minors}}}\uc2  whom he met through his work, and was\par \par \uc2 given a five-year\par \par \uc2 prison sentence. Patrick Wattigny\u8217\'20\'19s plea and sentence came after both of his victims strongly advocated for a harsher punishment.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Fox News was hit with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2023/jul/12/fox-news-faces-another-defamation-lawsuit-involving-tucker-carlson"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 defamation lawsuit}}}\uc2  by the Trump supporter Ray Epps after its former host \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  repeatedly called Epps an undercover \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent who orchestrated the January 6 insurrection at the US Capitol. Carlson said Epps \u8220\'20\'1Chelped stage-manage the insurrection\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a conspiracy he broadcast in nearly 20 episodes.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The leader of Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s most popular party has warned of a high cost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jul/13/leading-thailand-candidate-for-pm-warns-of-high-price-if-blocked-from-power"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 if he is blocked from taking power}}}\uc2 , after he was dealt a series of blows on the eve of a parliamentary vote to decide the next prime minister. Pita Limjaroenrat, of Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s Move Forward, is facing an uphill battle due to election rules.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Tornadoes and thunderstorms battered the Chicago area yesterday, forcing airports to halt air traffic and prompting officials to advise residents to seek shelter as tornado sirens echoed through the third largest US city. At least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/12/chicago-tornadoes-storms-airport-traffic-ohare-midway"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eight tornadoes hit}}}\uc2  north-eastern Illinois, with no immediate reports of injuries.\par \par \uc2 Stat of the day: \uc2 Twitter\uc2  owes ex-employees $500m in severance, lawsuit claims\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  allegedly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jul/12/twitter-severance-payments-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 refused to pay at least $500m in promised severance}}}\uc2  to thousands of employees who were laid off after \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  acquired the company, a lawsuit filed on Wednesday claims. Courtney McMillian, who oversaw \uc2 Twitter\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s employee benefits programs as its \u8220\'20\'1Chead of total rewards\u8221\'20\'1D before she was laid off in January, filed the proposed class action in San Francisco federal court. McMillian claims that under a severance plan created by \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in 2019, most workers were promised two months of their base pay plus one week of pay for each full year of service if they were laid off. Senior employees, such as McMillian, were owed six months of base pay, according to the lawsuit. But \uc2 Twitter\uc2  only gave laid-off workers one month of severance pay at most, and many did not receive anything, McMillian claims.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: How one of Canada\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18oldest unsolved and most unique\u8217\'20\'19 cases was cracked\par \par \uc2 For nearly five decades, little was known about the woman whose body was pulled from the Nation River, 90 miles west of Montreal, on 3 May 1975. She was white, with shoulder-length brown hair recently dyed strawberry blond and well-manicured nails. But she had no identification nor any clear identifying features. Over time, the woman \u8211\'20\'13 known simply as the Nation River Lady \u8211\'20\'13 became \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the oldest unsolved and most unique\u8221\'20\'1D cases the local police force had ever dealt with, stumping successive generations of investigators.\par \par \uc2 Last week, after 47 years, Ontario provincial police announced that the Nation River Lady had been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jul/12/canada-nation-river-lady-case-solved-genetic-genealogy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 identified as Jewell Parchman Langford}}}\uc2 , a prominent businessperson from Jackson, Tennessee, in a breakthrough marking one of the first times investigative genetic genealogy has been used in Canada. Police said they had charged Rodney Nichols, 81, with murder in connection to the case. Nichols, who lives in Florida, is now the subject of an extradition request.\par \par \uc2 Climate check: World\u8217\'20\'19s oceans changing colour due to climate breakdown, study suggests\par \par \uc2 Earth\u8217\'20\'19s oceans are changing colour and climate breakdown is probably to blame, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/jul/12/worlds-oceans-changing-colour-due-to-climate-breakdown-study-suggests"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to research}}}\uc2 . The deep blue sea is actually becoming steadily greener over time, according to the study, with areas in the low latitudes near the equator especially affected. \u8220\'20\'1CThe reason we care about this is not because we care about the colour, but because the colour is a reflection of the changes in the state of the ecosystem,\u8221\'20\'1D said BB Cael, a scientist at the National Oceanography Centre at the \uc2 University of Southampton\uc2  and author of the study published in Nature. When comparing changes in colour with those hypothesised from a computer model simulating what the oceans would look like if human-caused global heating had never taken place, the change was clear. The changes have been detected over 56% of the world\u8217\'20\'19s oceans \u8211\'20\'13 an area greater than all of the land on Earth.\par \par \uc2 Last Thing: An \u8216\'20\'18aggressive\u8217\'20\'19 sea otter is snatching surfers\u8217\'20\'19 boards. Experts are puzzled\par \par \uc2 A California otter is making headlines for her unique, and worrying, interactions with surfers. In recent weeks, Mark Woodward, a photographer who goes by Native Santa Cruz on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  has been sharing photos and videos of southern sea otters riding on surfboards they\u8217\'20\'19ve commandeered. Authorities are particularly concerned about one sea otter, known as 841, who was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/12/sea-otter-surfing-santa-cruz-california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 involved in multiple incidents}}}\uc2  and can be seen in a video relentlessly gnawing on a board that the aquatic mammal scared a rider off of. While the videos and photos have elicited jokes about the otter reclaiming her watery home, Woodward and the \uc2 US Fish and Wildlife Service\uc2  warn that its behavior is highly unusual and puts people and the animals in danger. Wildlife authorities and experts still don\u8217\'20\'19t have a clear explanation of the five-year-old otter\u8217\'20\'19s behavior, but say they are working to find, capture and relocate her.\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Get in touch\par \par \uc2 If you have any questions or comments about any of our newsletters please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:newsletters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newsletters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230713ej7d001bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
