\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Tech companies block wage increase for New York City delivery workers\b0\par\par\uc2 Victoria Bekiempis \par \uc2 1,037 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2023\par 11:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Doordash\uc2 , \uc2 GrubHub\uc2 , \uc2 Uber\uc2  and Relay lawsuits claim that the $17.96 per hour wage set to start on 12 July would deal blow to business\par \par \uc2 A week before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York City}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s app-based {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/sep/23/gig-economy-doordash-grubhub-food-delivery"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 delivery workers }}}\uc2  were poised to see an hourly minimum wage increase, several tech companies fired a legal salvo that has temporarily delayed this change.\par \par \uc2 Doordash\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/18/this-cant-be-real-grubhub-promotion-turns-new-york-city-restaurants-into-a-war-zone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 GrubHub}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/uber"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Uber}}}\uc2  and Relay filed lawsuits in Manhattan state civil court on 6 July, alleging that a recently greenlit $17.96 hourly minimum wage for app delivery workers \u8211\'20\'13 set to start on 12 July \u8211\'20\'13 would deal a blow to their business, also claiming it would harm this {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/dec/24/new-york-storm-delivery-workers-winter-weather"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legion of workers}}}\uc2 . Under the law, the minimum wage would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/12/nyregion/nyc-delivery-workers-minimum-wage.html#:~:text=The%20city's%20more%20than%2060%2C000,an%20analysis%20by%20the%20city."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increase}}}\uc2  to some $19.96 for app delivery workers in 2025.\par \par \uc2 One day after the suits\u8217\'20\'19 filing, Judge Nicholas W Moyne, who is presiding over these companies\u8217\'20\'19 respective cases, granted their request for a preliminary injunction that blocks the new minimum wage from going into effect for at least two weeks. Moyne has scheduled arguments on this temporary restraining order for 31 July.\par \par \uc2 While Moyne\u8217\'20\'19s decision on this is just one of many steps in what will probably be lengthy, complicated litigation, advocates nationwide are expected to keep a close eye on developments, as New York City\u8217\'20\'19s labor regulations are often seen as a bellwether for feasibility elsewhere. If New York\u8217\'20\'19s efforts succeed, other municipalities might feel emboldened to enact similar regulations \u8211\'20\'13 but if they fail, concerns about onerous legal battles might deter officials elsewhere from following Gotham\u8217\'20\'19s lead, advocates say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNew York City is attempting to protect these workers and ensure that they \u8211\'20\'13 like all other workers \u8211\'20\'13 are able to be paid the minimum wage,\u8221\'20\'1D said Elizabeth Saylor, citywide director of the \uc2 Legal Aid Society\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s employment law unit.\par \par \uc2 While a state-level lawsuit in New York wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t set legal precedent outside this state, the city\u8217\'20\'19s perception as a leader means this litigation\u8217\'20\'19s outcome could cut two ways.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf New York City is prohibited from doing so, it\u8217\'20\'19s likely to chill other municipalities in states from taking on these large companies, due to the expense of fighting it,\u8221\'20\'1D Saylor said. \u8220\'20\'1CNew York City often passes protections for workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights and when other municipalities see the protections working in practice, those municipalities sometimes pass their own laws.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe judge\u8217\'20\'19s decision is already having an impact on workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights, because it will delay the implementation of the increased wages for at least two weeks,\u8221\'20\'1D Saylor also said, noting that the delivery drivers are \u8220\'20\'1Coverwhelmingly people of color and immigrants.\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Ligia Guallpa, executive director of Workers Justice Project, which helms a group of protection-seeking delivery drivers called Los Deliveristas Unidos, said they want minimum pay so as not to rely on tips as their main source of income. Local officials have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/12/nyregion/nyc-delivery-workers-minimum-wage.html#:~:text=The%20city's%20more%20than%2060%2C000,an%20analysis%20by%20the%20city."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  that delivery workers receive approximately $11 hourly after tips, given their out-of-pocket expenses \u8211\'20\'13 which is $4 less than New York City\u8217\'20\'19s minimum wage of $15.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe implications on the national level are huge here because we are setting the wage floor for not only delivery workers in New York \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19re setting the example for across the country in terms of setting up basic labor protections for app delivery workers and independent contractors who, by design have been completely excluded from labor protections such as minimum wage laws,\u8221\'20\'1D Guallpa said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis legislation will not only increase the minimum pay for app delivery workers, but will uplift the lives of thousands of workers, who right now are pretty much surviving on poverty wages.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Guallpa said advocates hoped for a \u8220\'20\'1Cquick decision in establishing a pay rate, so that workers can earn a living wage without any further delay\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Camron Dowlatshahi, a California attorney with Mills Sadat Dowlat LLP, whose work has included wage and hour violations as well as other employment disputes, said that the west coast state and New York\u8217\'20\'19s respective efforts could have ripple effects elsewhere. (California\u8217\'20\'19s highest court will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketwatch.com/story/california-supreme-court-agrees-to-hear-prop-22-appeal-after-ruling-favoring-uber-lyft-7a6cd600"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 weigh a case}}}\uc2  over a state law on gig work that permits companies to treat app-based workers as independent contractors rather than employees.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile New York law for example, isn\u8217\'20\'19t precedential for California law and vice versa, in certain circumstances, it\u8217\'20\'19s absolutely persuasive,\u8221\'20\'1D Dowlatshahi said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s unclear which will get decided first \u8211\'20\'13 I think your preliminary injunctions in the New York cases will probably get decided first [but] it could have persuasive effects on the other state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As advocates await the upcoming proceeding, delivery apps have expressed satisfaction with the preliminary injunction; they have largely insisted on their support of worker protections, maintaining they oppose the rule\u8217\'20\'19s implementation, but not its intent. \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cpleased with the judge\u8217\'20\'19s decision on Friday to delay implementation of a rule that, if allowed to stand, will have serious adverse consequences for delivery partners, consumers and independent businesses\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Commenting on the injunction, \uc2 Uber\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s senior director of public affairs, Josh Gold, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe hope to use this time to work with the city and all stakeholders to figure out a minimum pay rule that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have devastating consequences for couriers, consumers and restaurants.\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  similarly said the preliminary injunction was \u8220\'20\'1Can early and promising victory for consumers, local businesses and delivery workers across New York City, protecting them from the harmful and lasting impacts of an extreme earnings standard that resulted from a fundamentally broken process\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 New York City officials have expressed dismay over the preliminary injunction but hope that the judge will soon rule in their favor. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are extremely disappointed that the apps are delaying the implementation of the minimum pay rate. These apps currently pay workers far below the minimum wage, and this pay rate would help lift thousands of working New Yorkers and their families out of poverty,\u8221\'20\'1D Vilda Vera Mayuga, commissioner of New York City\u8217\'20\'19s department of consumer and worker protection, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe look forward to a quick decision so that the dignified pay rate that workers deserve to earn is not delayed any more than necessary.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230712ej7c001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
