\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US wage growth stronger than expected in June, but job creation slows \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 8,043 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2023\par 15:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wages rose by 0.4% last month, while total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 209,000, and the unemployment rate has dropped to 3.6%US job market remains robust though slowing, with 209,000 jobs added in June\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 Afternoon summary\par \par \uc2 Time for a recap\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a8216f8f0850c66ca2e056#block-64a8216f8f0850c66ca2e056"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 U.S. President Joe Biden has declared}}}\uc2  that June\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll report, showing continued, but slowing, job growth is \u8220\'20\'1CBidenomics in action\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Biden declared:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are seeing stable and steady growth. That\u8217\'20\'19s Bidenomics - growing the economy by creating jobs, lowering costs for hardworking families, and making smart investments in America.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a804568f089742d825aafe#block-64a804568f089742d825aafe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nonfarm payrolls increased by 209,000 jobs last month}}}\uc2 , the smallest gain since December 2020, while April and May\u8217\'20\'19s reports were revised down to show 110,000 fewer jobs created than expected.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a8089a8f089db8e0572b69#block-64a8089a8f089db8e0572b69"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wage growth strengthened}}}\uc2 , rising 0.4% in the month. But there was also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a811038f0850c66ca2df3e#block-64a811038f0850c66ca2df3e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a rise in part-time work}}}\uc2 , suggesting demand weakened.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/07/june-jobs-report-2023-unemployment-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US economy adds 209,000 jobs in June as hiring slows}}}\par \par \uc2 The US dollar has lost ground, and is now down 0.7% against a basket of currencies today, and at a two-week low against the yen.\par \par \uc2 Bond and equity markets are calm, after a volatile selloff on Thursday, with economists predicting US interest rates will be raised again later this month.\par \par \uc2 Charles Hepworth\uc2 , investment director at GAM Investors, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the month of June, the US added 209,000 people to the non-farm workforce on the previous month. This number came in just under most forecasts of a 230,000 gain, but is still probably not what the Fed wants to see if it intends to continue pausing rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAverage hourly earnings were also up more than expected, rising 0.4% on the month and showing a 4.4% advance year-on-year. The broader unemployment rate fell to 3.6%. The US economy looks far from softening. All these factors mean interest rate hikes are back on the agenda. We expect the Fed to raise rates at its meeting later this month.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In other news today\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Travellers catching ferries from Dover faced queues of up to two hours on Friday as traffic built up at border posts.\par \par \uc2 The Kent port said passengers should expect waits of up to two hours for coaches and 90 minutes for cars, as traffic built up in the morning.\par \par \uc2 Ferries were operating normally, and passengers are allowed to catch subsequent sailings.\par \par \uc2 A combination of the sun, the start of school holidays in parts of the UK, and a school teachers\u8217\'20\'19 strike offering parents a potential long weekend may have contributed to the pressure on the port.\par \par \uc2 Vehicles queuing for ferries at the Port of Dover, Kent,today Photograph: Gareth Fuller/PA\par \par \uc2 UK house prices experienced their biggest annual fall in 12 years, according to Halifax.\par \par \uc2 Prices fell by 2.6% year-on-year, in the latest sign that soaring interest rates on mortgages is bringing a halt to the housing boom.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-at-fastest-annual-rate-since-2011-says-halifax-mortgage-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK house prices fall at fastest annual rate since 2011, says Halifax}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a7c2ee8f081d0996a37f19#block-64a7c2ee8f081d0996a37f19"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK mortgage rates have continued to rise}}}\uc2 , as the financial markets expect UK interest rate to rise to 6.5% by early next year, up from 5% today.\par \par \uc2 Half of older adults who left the UK workforce amid mass redundancies in the first year of the Covid pandemic ended up falling into relative poverty, according to the \uc2 Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jul/07/older-uk-workers-retired-early-pandemic-forced-into-poverty-ifs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Older UK workers who retired early in pandemic were \u8216\'20\'18forced into poverty\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 The estimated \u163\'00\'A34.8bn cost of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2023/mar/27/revised-plans-hs2-london-euston-terminus-cost-5bn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 HS2\u8217\'20\'19s endangered Euston terminus }}}\uc2  could balloon further unless the government becomes \u8220\'20\'1Cclear what it is trying to achieve\u8221\'20\'1D, the public accounts committee has warned.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2023/jul/07/cost-of-euston-hs2-terminus-could-race-past-4-point-8bn-estimate-mps-say"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cost of Euston HS2 terminus could race past \u163\'00\'A34.8bn estimate, MPs say}}}\par \par \uc2 Air traffic control managers in mainland {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/europe-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Europe}}}\uc2  are planning to strike this summer, potentially exacerbating disruption to holiday flights should French strikes continue.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/07/air-traffic-control-strikes-could-spread-throughout-mainland-europe-this-summer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Air traffic control strikes could disrupt flights in Europe this summer}}}\par \par \uc2 South East Water, which left thousands of households without running water last month spent more on dividends and servicing its debt pile over two years than investing in infrastructure, it has emerged.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/07/south-east-water-debt-infrastructure"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 South East Water spent more on dividends and debt than infrastructure}}}\par \par \uc2 And\u8230\'20\'26.the last {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/ford"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ford}}}\uc2  Fiesta will leave the assembly line today, marking the end of an era for a model that sold 22m vehicles globally and is the UK\u8217\'20\'19s all-time bestselling car.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/07/ford-fiesta-production-ends-uk-car-electric-vehicles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ford Fiesta: production ends of UK\u8217\'20\'19s all-time bestselling car}}}\par \par \uc2 Have a lovely weekend. GW\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Erik Norland\uc2 , Senior Economist at \uc2 CME Group\uc2 , on the jobs report:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNFP surprised on the downside by 131K jobs net of revisions to previous months\u8217\'20\'19 numbers, but the overall number wasn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily weak with wage growth surprising on the high side and unemployment, which is calculated from a separate survey, coming in at 3.6%. The economy still added 209K jobs in June.\par \par \uc2 Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s JOLTS survey showed that there were 9.8 million job openings at the end of May, about three million than there were in late 2019 before the pandemic. This implies the possibility of strong demand for workers in the months ahead, even if economic activity slows in response to the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s rate hikes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden on jobs report: This is Bidenomics in action:\par \par \uc2 The US president has hailed June\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report as evidence that \u8220\'20\'1CBidenomics\u8221\'20\'1D is working, delivering sustainable economic growth.\par \par \uc2 In a statement just issued by the White House, Joe Biden says:\par \par \uc2 This is Bidenomics in action: Our economy added more than 200,000 jobs last month\u8212\'20\'14for a total of 13.2 million jobs since I took office.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s more jobs added in two and a half years than any president has ever created in a four-year term. The unemployment rate has now remained below 4% for 17 months in a row\u8212\'20\'14the longest stretch since the 1960s. The share of working-age Americans who have jobs is at the highest level in over 20 years. Inflation has come down by more than half.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing stable and steady growth. That\u8217\'20\'19s Bidenomics\u8212\'20\'14growing the economy by creating jobs, lowering costs for hardworking families, and making smart investments in America.\par \par \uc2 enltr"This is Bidenomics in action: Our economy added more than 200,000 jobs last month\u8212\'20\'14for a total of 13.2 million jobs since I took office. That\u8217\'20\'19s more jobs added in two and a half years than any president has ever created in a four-year term," says Preside\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/TOF19S9c84"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/TOF19S9c84}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Steve Herman (@W7VOA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/W7VOA/status/1677324056259973122?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 This chart shows pretty clearly that US job creation is in a downward trend, once you account for the downward revisions to the April and May jobs reports:\par \par \uc2 enltr..But what it clearly does is reassert the downward trend, which had looked to be flattening; the revisions have made the spring seasonals less favorable. I don't often like to play the extrapolation game but on this occasion the trend is so pronounced that it seems reasonable. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/HU2uDsF4Ap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/HU2uDsF4Ap}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/e3lGDx5200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/e3lGDx5200}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Ian Shepherdson\uc2  (@IanShepherdson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/IanShepherdson/status/1677322166675595266?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  have tweeted their views on today\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs report \u8211\'20\'13 which they argue shows more jobs created than you\u8217\'20\'19d normally expect.\par \par \uc2 enltrToday\u8217\'20\'19s employment report shows the U.S. economy added 209,000 jobs in June, slightly below market expectations. Employment growth in April and May was revised down by a combined 110,000 jobs. The average monthly gain over the past three months is now 244,000. 1/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/92XAR1vYnX"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/92XAR1vYnX}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1677309786419785730?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe headline unemployment rate ticked down to 3.6 percent. The broadest measure of underemployment, called U6, rose to 6.9 percent. Both measures have remained roughly around these low rates since March 2022. 2/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/kxrROU73rs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/kxrROU73rs}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1677309793680146434?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrAs the unemployment rate falls late in business cycles, job growth typically cools. Nevertheless, June\u8217\'20\'19s 209,000 job growth is higher than we would have expected with a 3.6% unemployment rate given the 2015-19 experience. 3/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/FKItAcLGEZ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/FKItAcLGEZ}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1677309796263899137?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrLabor force participation was 62.6 percent in June, unchanged from May. The participation rate for 25-54 year-olds, which is less sensitive to the aging of the population and so is preferred by many economists, rose to 83.5%, its highest rate since May 2002. 6/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/lcauMlyCYK"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/lcauMlyCYK}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1677309817570967558?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrMonthly nominal average hourly earnings growth held steady at 0.4 percent in June. We do not have the inflation report for June yet, which will include real wage growth. 8/\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1677309821870112773?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThree-month average wage growth was 4.7 percent annualized in June, lower than the 6.4 percent in January 2022 but higher than it was last month. 9/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/EHd1M3BdqB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/EHd1M3BdqB}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1677309823388467202?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 The stronger-than-expected wage growth in June suggests that US firms were prepared to sign off pay rises for workers, or paying more for new staff.\par \par \uc2 In other words, demand for workers remains \u8220\'20\'1Crobust\u8221\'20\'1D, says Greg Wilensky, head of US fixed income at Janus Henderson Investors :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJune\u8217\'20\'19s nonfarm payrolls growth of 209,000 came in slightly below consensus expectations of 230,000. The unemployment rate ticked down to 3.6% from 3.7% in May, while the labor force participation rate held steady at 62.6%. While this is the lowest monthly gain in jobs since the pandemic, this level of growth remains congruent with a strong labor market.\par \par \uc2 Notably, Average Hourly Earnings rose more than expected in June, another signal that demand for labor remains robust.\par \par \uc2 In our view, there is nothing in the data that would cause the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  (Fed) to hit the brakes on a rate hike in July. The labor market continues to show sufficient strength that we expect the Fed to follow through with another 25 basis point rate hike in July, and make few changes to its previous statements regarding the strength of the labor market.\par \par \uc2 Looking forward to the likelihood of a September rate hike, we think that decision will largely be driven by data releases between now and then.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Futures trading indicates there is a 90% chance that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  lifts rates at its meeting later this month, having paused in June.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/interest-rates/cme-fedwatch-tool.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the CME FedWatch Tool}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrFed rate hike in July now more than 90% likely, futures trading suggests {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/87X5D873Yb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/87X5D873Yb}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Breaking Fed News (@BreakingFedNews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BreakingFedNews/status/1677312144898150400?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 The New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) building. Photograph: \uc2 Xinhua\uc2 /\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has opened in the red.\par \par \uc2 This suggests investors anticipate that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise US interest rates higher, given the resilience in the US joba markets.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average, which tracks 30 large US companies, fell 85.19 points, or 0.25%, at the open to 33,837.07.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 opened 7.05 points lower, or -0.16%, at 4,404.54, while the tech-focused Nasdaq Composite lost 10.98 points, or almost 0.1%, to 13,668.07 at the opening bell.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 US short-term government bonds have recovered some of their earlier losses, after the jobs report was released.\par \par \uc2 This has pulled the yield, or interest rate, on 2-year Treasury Bills down to 4.98%. That\u8217\'20\'19s back below the 5% mark hit yesterday for the first time since March, as yields hit 15-year highs.\par \par \uc2 Yields rise when bond prices fall, and the recent surge in yields follows increases in US interest rates and expectations of further rises to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Part-time work rose in June as hours were cut back\par \par \uc2 There was also a rise in Americans being moved onto part-time work due to weakening demand in June, the jobs report shows.\par \par \uc2 The number of people employed part-time for economic reasons increased by 452,000 to 4.2 million in June.\par \par \uc2 This "partially\u8221\'20\'1D reflects an increase in the number of people whose hours were cut due to slack work or business conditions, the report says, even though they would have preferred full-time employment.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobs report: What the experts say\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report is \u8220\'20\'1Cslightly weaker\u8221\'20\'1D than many expected, says Richard Flynn, managing director at \uc2 Charles Schwab UK\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The labour market remains tight, but investors will likely interpret these numbers as a sign that cracks are beginning to emerge. As wage growth is yet to substantially ease, the Fed will likely feel continued pressure to maintain high interest rates. When inflation remains an issue for the US economy, slower and weaker pay growth will make it easier for the Fed to maintain price stability.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 John Leiper\uc2 , chief investment officer at Titan Asset Management, agrees that today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery different\u8221\'20\'1D than previous months.\par \par \uc2 Non-farm payroll came in below expectations for the first time in a year and revisions to the two prior readings remove another 110,000 jobs. Despite fewer jobs, the unemployment rate fell.\par \par \uc2 In summary, 209,000 additional jobs is still strong, unemployment is historically low and average hourly earnings rose. It was interesting to see, given weakness in the sector, that manufacturing employment actually rose.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, despite the noticeably softer number versus yesterday, which is catalysing a rebound in risk sentiment, this doesn\u8217\'20\'19t detract from the narrative that the Fed has more work to do on rates.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 A 209,000 increase in payrolls can \u8220\'20\'1Chardly be described as weak\u8221\'20\'1D, argues \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , chief global strategist at Principal Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 Jobs growth has slowed but remains too strong to justify an extended Fed pause. More significantly, with average hourly earnings surprising to the upside, wage pressures are still too strong. Today\u8217\'20\'19s report will give the Fed little reason to hold off from hiking at the July meeting.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow attention will turn to next week\u8217\'20\'19s CPI report. Without a slowdown in monthly core inflation, market focus may start turning to whether rate hikes will persist into September and, from there, questioning how much more monetary tightening the U.S. economy can realistically take before something breaks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hugh Grieves, fund manager of the Premier Miton US Opportunities Fund, says America seems to be avoiding falling into an often-predicted recession:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUS workers continue to be in strong demand as the American economy remains remarkably resilient, adding 209,000 workers to the national payroll in June, despite the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s best efforts to apply the brakes.\par \par \uc2 With the unemployment rate remaining at just 3.6%, employees are in short supply resulting in workers\u8217\'20\'19 hourly wages now rising faster than headline inflation. With numbers like these, a recession looks improbable any time soon.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Full story: US job market remains robust though slowing\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/useconomy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US economy}}}\uc2  added 209,000 new jobs in June as hiring slowed, our US business editor Dominic Rushe reports.\par \par \uc2 The rise was lower than the 240,000 jobs economists had expected and lower than the 309,000 jobs added in May.\par \par \uc2 But the gain was the 30th consecutive month of jobs gains and the unemployment rate ticked down to the historically low rate of 3.6%.\par \par \uc2 The US job market has remained robust despite the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s aggressive attempts to slow the economy and tamp down inflation with more than a year of interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 The Fed chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , has indicated that the central bank is likely to raise rates again this month after announcing a pause in June. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/07/june-jobs-report-2023-unemployment-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More here\u8230\'20\'26.}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/07/june-jobs-report-2023-unemployment-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US job market remains robust though slowing, with 209,000 jobs added in June}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Disappointingly, US job growth in April was weaker than previously thought.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s payroll report shows that April\u8217\'20\'19s data has been revised down by 77,000, from +294,000 to +217,000.\par \par \uc2 Include May\u8217\'20\'19s downward revision of 33k, and this means 110,000 fewer jobs were created in these two months than first reported.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe change in total nonfarm payroll employment for April was revised down by 77,000 to +217,000, and the change for May was revised down by 33,000 to +306,000. With these revisions, employment in April and May combined is 110,000 lower than previously reported. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/US?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #US}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/NFP?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #NFP}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Nonfarm?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Nonfarm}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CN Wire (@Sino_Market) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Sino_Market/status/1677296642528247808?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 These revisions aren\u8217\'20\'19t unusual \u8211\'20\'13 NFP reports are typically adjusted after their first release. But it does raise questions over whether the strength of the labor market has been overstated..\par \par \uc2 enltrWith all these big revisions to US nonfarm payrolls, what is going on with the economy? If companies aren\u8217\'20\'19t hiring as many, perhaps talks of more rate hikes are based on wrong data. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/KZRCJVtfBH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/KZRCJVtfBH}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Brian Tycangco ??? (@BrianTycangco) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BrianTycangco/status/1677297864635211777?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US dollar has weakened slightly, following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a804568f089742d825aafe#block-64a804568f089742d825aafe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the news }}}\uc2  that US job growth was lower than expected in June.\par \par \uc2 Against a basket of currencies, the dollar is down 0.3% today, while the pound is up half a cent against the greenback at $1.2788.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 US wages rise faster than expected\par \par \uc2 Average pay rose in June, in a boost to US employees \u8211\'20\'13 but a potential headache for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In June, average hourly earnings rose by 12 cents, or 0.4%, to $33.58, ahead of expectations of a 0.3% rise.\par \par \uc2 Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 4.4%, ahead of expectations of a 4.2% annual rise.\par \par \uc2 That indicates earnings are rising ahead of inflation, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jun/13/us-inflation-rate-may-2023-cpi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which fell to 4% in May}}}\uc2  (we get June\u8217\'20\'19s CPI inflation report next week).\par \par \uc2 enltrWages came in stronger than expected thats the fly in the ointment here, +0.4% m/m verus +0.3% exp, and +4.4% y/y versus 4.2%. This is not hot hot, but last month was bumped up a tenth on each too. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/dQXMknxkuS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/dQXMknxkuS}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bill Blue Line (@bill_baruch) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/bill_baruch/status/1677297701283586048?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Employment in government, health care, social assistance, and construction continued to trend up in June, the jobs report shows.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in government increased by 60,000 in June.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Health care added 41,000 jobs\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Social assistance added 24,000 jobs\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in construction rose by 23,000\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.38pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a804568f089742d825aafe#block-64a804568f089742d825aafe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 At 209,000}}}\uc2 , the increase in US employment last month was below recent averages.\par \par \uc2 Nonfarm employment has grown by an average of 278,000 per month over the first 6 months of 2023, lower than the average of 399,000 per month in 2022.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobless rate dips to 3.6%\par \par \uc2 The US unemployment rate has dropped to 3.6%, down from 3.7% in May.\par \par \uc2 The latest Household Survey Data, just released, also shows there were 5.957m people unemployed in the US in June, down from May\u8217\'20\'19s 6.097m.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate has ranged from 3.4% to 3.7% since March 2022, says the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 US non-farm payroll report released\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The US economy added fewer jobs than expected last month, as job creation slowed.\par \par \uc2 June\u8217\'20\'19s Non-Farm Payroll expanded by 209,000, missing forecast that around 225,000 new jobs would be created.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a slowdown on May, when a downwardly-revised 306,000 jobs were created [that\u8217\'20\'19s down from the first estimate of 339k jobs].\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a blow to US workers, and suggests the US labor market is weakening \u8211\'20\'13 which may take some pressure off the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates higher.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.18pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US jobs market has been particularly strong of late, with nonfarm payrolls data beating expectations in the last 14 months in a row.\par \par \uc2 That trend could continue today, says \uc2 Fawad Razaqzada\uc2 , market analyst at City Index.\par \par \uc2 He adds:\par \par \uc2 If it does, it will mean interest rates will likely rise and remain higher for longer. This could benefit the US dollar in the short-term, even if high rates for longer might be something that could ultimately hurt the economy at some later point in time.\par \par \uc2 Headline jobs growth is expected to have risen by 224,000 in June, causing the unemployment rate to drop back to 3.6% after it unexpectedly rose to 3.7% in the previous month. Average hourly earnings, a key measure of inflation, are expected to have risen by 0.3% month-over-month.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 A crucial US jobs report looms\par \par \uc2 Tension is mounting in the financial markets, as investors eagerly await the latest US employment report, due in 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 June\u8217\'20\'19s Non-Farm Payroll (NFP), due at 1.30pm UK time or 8.30am EDT, will have a major influence on whether US interest rates continue to rise.\par \par \uc2 Economists predict that job creation slowed in June, with an estimated 225,000 new hires, down from May\u8217\'20\'19s 339,000 (which may be revised).\par \par \uc2 But after yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s ADP private payroll report, which showed 497,000 new hires at US companies last month, traders are bracing for a strong NFP which could prompt the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates later this month.\par \par \uc2 Conversly, a weak payroll report today could indicate that the economy is weakening.\par \par \uc2 Jim Reid, analyst at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , point out that unemployment rarely rises before a recession\par \par \uc2 He told clients today:\par \par \uc2 In the 12 months leading up to a US recession, unemployment tends to be flat with very little variation around this. It then doesn\u8217\'20\'19t increase until the recession starts, and then rises quite sharply over the next 12-15 months on average.\par \par \uc2 The strength of wage growth last month will also influence the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s hand, as it tries to cool demand in the economy. Average earnings growth is expected to remain at +0.3% month-on-month in June.\par \par \uc2 enltrwhat about the nfp later and will the data be much different from the previous one? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ckdgKYAw1X"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ckdgKYAw1X}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 FxHeaven (@HeavenInsidelaw) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HeavenInsidelaw/status/1677248615457366016?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 The impact of UK food inflation will be painfully obvious to anyone shopping for a barbeque this weekend.\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest drivers behind the cost of the British barbecue is meat. In the past five years, the price of four frozen beef burgers has jumped 64.4%, from \u163\'00\'A32.02 to \u163\'00\'A33.32, much of that in the past year alone, when the price has increased by 80p.\par \par \uc2 Vegeburgers are 50% pricier than in 2018, while peppers and prawns are much more expensive too. You can track it all {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/07/how-much-see-how-the-cost-of-a-barbecue-has-gone-up-in-the-uk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/07/how-much-see-how-the-cost-of-a-barbecue-has-gone-up-in-the-uk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 How much!? See how the cost of a barbecue has gone up in the UK}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.58am BST\par \par \uc2 enltrUK house prices are falling at their fastest annual pace since 2011, Halifax says, as the property market buckles in the face of rising borrowing costs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/krisaqnews?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @krisaqnews}}}\uc2  breaks it down: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/mn8yjQVbIe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/mn8yjQVbIe}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/bd3lQ6s3Fa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/bd3lQ6s3Fa}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bloomberg (@business) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/business/status/1677221201746206721?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Inflationary pressures are easing in the eurozone, according to \uc2 Luis de Guindos\uc2 , vice-president of the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Speaking at King\u8217\'20\'19s College London this morning, de Guindos said that there are \u8220\'20\'1Csome signs of softening\u8221\'20\'1D in the indicators tracking underlying price pressures.\par \par \uc2 And \u8220\'20\'1Cwhile still wide by historical standards, the range of measures of underlying inflation recently began to narrow\u8221\'20\'1D, de Guindos says, adding:\par \par \uc2 This suggests that the unusually high level of uncertainty around the downward trajectory of inflation over the medium term has started to ease somewhat.\par \par \uc2 De Guindos cautioned, though, that the \uc2 ECB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s job \u8220\'20\'1Cis not yet done\u8221\'20\'1D, having raised interest rates by 400 basis points (four percentage points).\par \par \uc2 He adds:\par \par \uc2 Services inflation, and labour costs in particular, need to be closely monitored, as they are now an important driver of overall inflation.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.39am BST\par \par \uc2 The UK competition regulator has called on bed-in-a-box brand Emma Sleep to change its online sales practices or face potential court action.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Competition and Markets Authority\uc2  is concerned that customers are being misled through pressure selling tactics such as \u8220\'20\'1Ccountdown timers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The CMA also said it found evidence that \u8220\'20\'1Cdiscount claims made by Emma Sleep did not stack up against the actual savings made by customers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Cardell, chief executive of the CMA, explains:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCompanies that use fake countdown clocks or misleading \u8216\'20\'18discounts\u8217\'20\'19 risk pressuring people into making quick purchases and often spending more money than they otherwise would for fear of missing out.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is especially concerning given the current pressure on people\u8217\'20\'19s pockets.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have put a number of detailed concerns to Emma Sleep about its sales tactics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe CMA looks to Emma Sleep to agree to change the way it does business to avoid the risk of court action.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltr??? We\u8217\'20\'19ve written to Emma Sleep setting out our concerns about their online sales tactics.\par \par \uc2 Our evidence shows that their use of count-down clocks, discount claims and \u8216\'20\'18high demand\u8217\'20\'19 claims could break consumer protection law.\par \par \uc2 Read more: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/8Abog3vvU6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/8Abog3vvU6}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/nbSWTk1KaH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/nbSWTk1KaH}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Competition & Markets Authority\uc2  (@CMAgovUK) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CMAgovUK/status/1677241280433143808?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.13am BST\par \par \uc2 The money markets are still predicting further increases in UK interest rates this year, to battle inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  is expected to raise rates to around 6.5% by next February, up from 5% at present.\par \par \uc2 This prospect is likely to weigh on house prices this year.\par \par \uc2 The CEBR economic consultancy has warned that \u8220\'20\'1Chigher-for-longer interest rate expectations\u8221\'20\'1D wukk reinforce the likelihood of a contraction in house prices this year.\par \par \uc2 They explain:\par \par \uc2 House prices are forecast to fall by 1.8% on average across 2023. This follows strong annual growth of 9.9% over 2022. This would mean a peak-to-trough decline of 7.1% in average prices between Q4 2022 and Q4 2023.\par \par \uc2 Due to the majority of mortgagers being on fixed rate deals, higher mortgage rates will continue to dampen market activity throughout the next year. As at least a million homeowners are required to renegotiate their fixed-term agreements in 2024, we expect average house prices to contract by 0.8% in that year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.08am BST\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2023/jul/06/uk-homeowners-share-your-experience-of-having-a-variable-rate-mortgage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK homeowners: share your experience of having a variable rate mortgage}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Vehicles queuing for ferries at the Port of Dover, Kent, where passengers are facing up to a two-hour wait for checks by French border officials due to a high volume of tourist traffic beginning the summer getaway early and heavy freight traffic. Photograph: Gareth Fuller/PA\par \par \uc2 Travellers arriving in Dover for cross-Channel ferries are facing long queues, PA Media reports.\par \par \uc2 The Kent port said processing times for passing through checks by French border officials are two hours for coaches and one-and-a-half hours for cars.\par \par \uc2 In a message to waiting passengers, the port stated:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday is already proving a popular travel day at Dover, and the port is busy processing strong volumes of tourist traffic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTeams from the port, Police aux Frontieres and our ferry operators are working to get you through as swiftly as possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Delays in processing passengers have been blamed on French border officials carrying out extra checks and stamping UK passports following Brexit.\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/PODOVER?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #PODOVER}}}\uc2  - There are currently queues of 90 mins for cars and 120 mins for coaches to reach border checks in Dover. The rest of the port is currently free flowing and we will get you on your way asap. Please ensure you arrive prepared and carry spare bottles of water with you.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 P&O Ferries\uc2  Updates (@POferriesupdate) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POferriesupdate/status/1677245110709374977?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.21am BST\par \par \uc2 Charts: rising mortgage rates and falling house prices\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a chart showing how the cost of fixed-rate mortgages has climbed over the last few weeks, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a7c2ee8f081d0996a37f19#block-64a7c2ee8f081d0996a37f19"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including today}}}\uc2 , as the markets have anticipated higher interest rates.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2023/07/mortgage-rates-chart/giv-13425iw6C3m24zxAQ/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26and here\u8217\'20\'19s the fall in UK house prices, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a7a33e8f089db8e0572686#block-64a7a33e8f089db8e0572686"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  by Halifax this morning:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2023/07/house-prices/giv-13425OJc7Q3RQGZKv/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s economy has suffered its biggest drop in productivity in a decade, new data shows.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2 , output per hour worked fell by 0.6% in January to March, the weakest annual growth since Quarter 1 2013, excluding the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Output per worker fell by 0.9%, while output per job dropped 1% year-on-year in Q1 2023, the weakest annual growth for both since July-September 2009 (again, if you exclude the coronavirus lockdowns).\par \par \uc2 enltrUK {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ONS}}}\uc2  confirms output-per-hour productivity dropped 0.6% in Q1 - the biggest such decline in 10 years.\par \par \uc2 In terms of levels, output-per-hour is 0.6% higher than in 2019. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/KAslfDjibz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/KAslfDjibz}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/FBAEgMpDo6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/FBAEgMpDo6}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Andy Bruce (@BruceReuters) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BruceReuters/status/1677236378361380865?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.30am BST\par \par \uc2 After weeks of turbulence in the mortgage market, there are signs of \u8220\'20\'1Ca semblance of normality\u8221\'20\'1D returning as lenders slow down the readjustment of their rates, says Karen Noye, mortgage expert at \uc2 Quilter\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 But, she warns, the increase in mortgage rates will make it harder for borrowers to service their debts:\par \par \uc2 House prices have seen yet another modest drop of 0.1%, according to Halifax this morning. Given the huge squeeze on affordability at the moment this is likely to be sign of things to come but its worth remembering that the housing market despite everything that has been thrown at it over the past few years has remained resilient. However, the economic storm currently battering Britain seems like an insurmountable hurdle and downward pressure on house prices is inevitable.\par \par \uc2 With two year fixed rate mortgages now well above 6%, people\u8217\'20\'19s ability to continue to service their mortgage will be called into question and also first time buyer\u8217\'20\'19s desire to jump into paying such a high monthly rate will also be tested. This could create an environment where those who have overstretched themselves look to offload their properties at a time where there is a dearth of demand.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.14am BST\par \par \uc2 World food prices hit two-year low in June\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Global food commodity prices have fallen to their lowest level in over two years.\par \par \uc2 The United Nations food agency\u8217\'20\'19s world price index, just released, dropped in June to its lowest level since spring 2021.\par \par \uc2 It was pushed down by a drop in the cost of sugar, vegetable oils, cereals and dairy products, away from the highs set in 2022 after the Ukraine invasion.\par \par \uc2 The Food and Agriculture Organization\u8217\'20\'19s (FAO) price index, which tracks the most globally-traded food commodities, averaged 122.3 points in June, down from 124.0 in May.\par \par \uc2 The FAO reports that world prices of all major cereals fell in June, pulling cereal prices down by 2.1% during June, and by 23.9% year-on-year. It adds:\par \par \uc2 International coarse grain prices fell the most, down 3.4 percent since May. A fifth consecutive monthly decline in international maize prices was mostly driven by increased seasonal supplies from ongoing harvests in Argentina and Brazil\par \par \uc2 Vegetable oil prices fell by 2.4% during June, to the lowest level since November 2020.\par \par \uc2 Dairy prices fell 0.8% in the month, and were 22.2 lower than a eary ago\par \par \uc2 The repor explains:\par \par \uc2 The decline in June was again led by lower international cheese prices, reflecting ample export availabilities, especially in Western Europe, where milk production tracked seasonally higher, while retail sales were somewhat subdued.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, whole milk powder prices fell slightly on lower import purchases by North Asian buyers and increased supplies, especially from New Zealand. By contrast, world butter prices rose, driven by active demand for spot supplies, mainly from the Middle East, and increased internal retail sales in Western Europe.\par \par \uc2 Skim milk prices increased slightly on higher import purchases to meet short-term needs amid concerns over supplies in the months ahead during the seasonally declining production phase in Western Europe.\par \par \uc2 Hard-pressed households will hope to see these reductions feeding through to lower prices on the shelves\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.15am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Moneyfacts also reports that savings rates have risen higher today \u8211\'20\'13 at a time when pressure is mounting on banks to offer more to savers.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the details:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The average 1-year fixed savings rate today is 4.83%. This is up from an average rate of 4.80% on the previous working day.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The average easy access savings rate today is 2.49%. This is up from an average rate of 2.48% on the previous working day.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The average 1-year fixed Cash ISA rate today is 4.52%. This is up from an average rate of 4.49% on the previous working day.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The average easy access ISA rate today is 2.61%. This is up from an average rate of 2.59% on the previous working day.\par \par \uc2 UK bank chiefs met with the UK\u8217\'20\'19s financial regulator yesterday, and were urged to \u8220\'20\'1Caccelerate\u8221\'20\'1D savings rates\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/06/uk-banks-meets-finance-regulator-as-anger-grows-over-savings-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK banks urged to \u8216\'20\'18accelerate\u8217\'20\'19 savings rates by financial regulator}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Moneyfacts: 2 and 5-year fixed mortgage rate rise again\par \par \uc2 UK fixed mortgage rates have climbed again this morning, to levels last seen in autumn 2022 after the mini-budget caused market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Moneyfacts reports that two-year, and five-year, rate have both risen, leaving them both above 6%.\par \par \uc2 They say:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The average 2-year fixed residential mortgage rate today is 6.54%, up from 6.52% on Thursday\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The average 5-year fixed residential mortgage rate today is 6.04%. This is up from an average rate of 6.02% on the previous working day.\par \par \uc2 There are currently 4,621 residential mortgage products available, up from 4,594 yesterday.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Over in the City, stocks have opened lower after yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s heavy selloff.\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 share index has dropped by almost 0.4%, down 26 points at 7254 points, having ended Thursday at its lowest closing level since last November.\par \par \uc2 It has touched 7,232 points this morning, the Footsie\u8217\'20\'19s lowest intraday level since March, when the panic in the banking sector triggered losses.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX dipped by 0.2% at the open, while France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC is flat, after both share indices also fell sharply on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.10am BST\par \par \uc2 New figures from low-cost housebuilder MJ Gleeson this morning have confirmed the housing market slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Its subsidiary, \uc2 Gleeson Homes\uc2 , reports that house sales slumped more than a fifth year-on-year in the six months to the end of June, as first-time buyers struggled to get onto the housing ladder \u8211\'20\'13 while the number of older buyers doubled.\par \par \uc2 Gleeson said that 829 homes were sold in the second half of its financial year, down from 1,068 in the same period last year, \u8220\'20\'1Creflecting the downturn in the wider economy and the immediate impact on buyer confidence as a result of higher interest rates\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 While the company said that house prices remained \u8220\'20\'1Cresilient\u8221\'20\'1D, the average price increased 11.3% to \u163\'00\'A3167,300 during the year, it noted a \u8220\'20\'1Csignifcant shift\u8221\'20\'1D in buyer demographics in recent months.\par \par \uc2 In the first half of this year the proportion of first-time buyers slumped from 71% in the same period last year to around 50%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the proportion of over-55 year old buyers doubled from 10% to a fifth of all sales.\par \par \uc2 Overall, Gleeson saw annual house sales drop from 2,000 in its last financial year to 1,723 in the year to the end of June.\par \par \uc2 It said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLooking ahead, whilst the board believes that demand from first-time buyers will continue at the levels seen through the last few months, it anticipates that interest from other value-driven buyers will increase as purchasers look to take advantage of Gleeson\u8217\'20\'19s more affordable price points and high quality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.01am BST\par \par \uc2 UK house price correction remains modest, says EY ITEM Club\par \par \uc2 UK house prices continue to display a surprising degree of resilience, says the economic forecasters at the \uc2 EY\uc2  ITEM Club, by only dipping 0.1% during June (although -2.6% on an annual basis)\par \par \uc2 However, they fear that this resilience will likely fade.\par \par \uc2 Martin Beck\uc2 , chief economic advisor to the EY ITEM Club, warns that the rise in mortgage rates over the last month, if sustained, will increase the challenges faced by those renegotiating fixed rate mortgages.\par \par \uc2 It could, in theory, prompt a bigger fall in prices than the 10% peak-to-trough decline in values the EY ITEM Club expects.\par \par \uc2 Beck says:\par \par \uc2 The reaction of financial markets to a series of upside surprises for inflation and pay growth has caused interest rate expectations to increase and this is feeding into higher mortgage interest rates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe rise in swap rates reflects markets\u8217\'20\'19 view that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  will continue to raise rates significantly, with Bank Rate now widely expected to peak at 6.5% early next year. But the EY ITEM Club thinks an improving inflation outlook means the market view is too downbeat and that rates will stabilise after two further rises by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 . If that prediction is correct, mortgage rates should fall back during the second half of this year, albeit to levels still high by the standards of the last decade or so.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd there are other reasons to think that, while house prices are likely to drift down, a serious correction should be avoided.\par \par \uc2 enltrInstant Info \u8211\'20\'13 Halifax UK House Price Index {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/TXQNUNbObi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/TXQNUNbObi}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 BuiltPlace (@BuiltPlace) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BuiltPlace/status/1677206946498093057?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.51am BST\par \par \uc2 The drop in UK house prices shows that the rise in UK interest rates is having a \u8216\'20\'18major ripple effect\u8217\'20\'19 on the housing market, says Victoria Scholar, head of investment at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ii.co.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interactive investor}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She says:\par \par \uc2 Mortgage rates have been increasingly sharply with two-year fixed deals averaging 6.52% according to the latest data from Moneyfacts while five-year deals surpassed 6% for the first time in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s desperate attempts to bring sky-high inflation back down towards its 2% target is starting to have a major ripple effect across the housing market, dampening demand for properties and in turn weighing on prices. This is of course the intended effect of monetary tightening, but there\u8217\'20\'19s no shying away from the pain it is causing for those on variable rate mortgages and in terms of the wealth effect for many homeowners who are seeing their prized asset drop in value.\par \par \uc2 While a drop in house prices will be a welcome development for those looking to get onto the property ladder, the cost of borrowing is still a major deterrent with the era of rock bottom interest rates now nothing more than a nostalgic memory.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.53am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.34am BST\par \par \uc2 Halifax: Household squeeze will weigh on house prices\par \par \uc2 It is hard to predict how deep or persistent the downturn in house prices will be, says Kim Kinnaird, director of Halifax Mortgages.\par \par \uc2 Kinnaird explains:\par \par \uc2 Consumer price inflation is likely to come down in the near term as energy and food prices look set to reverse their steep rises, but core inflation is clearly proving stickier than originally expected.\par \par \uc2 With markets now forecasting a peak in Bank Rate of over 6%, the likelihood is that mortgage rates will remain higher for longer, and the squeeze on household finances will continue to put downward pressure on house prices over the coming year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.32am BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/jul/07/uk-house-prices-fall-halifax-stock-markets-us-jobs-ftse-bonds-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-64a7a33e8f089db8e0572686#block-64a7a33e8f089db8e0572686"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s report from Halifax}}}\uc2  is unlikely to be the last national house price index to fall into negative territory this year, warns \uc2 Tom Bill\uc2 , head of UK residential research at \uc2 Knight Frank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Bill also predicts we won\u8217\'20\'19t see a house price crash, saying:\par \par \uc2 Mortgage rates will keep edging up as wage growth keeps core inflation stubbornly high and we expect prices to fall by around 5% this year.\par \par \uc2 However, this isn\u8217\'20\'19t the global financial crisis part two for house prices and any decline will be kept in check by rising wages, low unemployment, cash sales, record-high levels of housing equity, longer mortgages and savings amassed during the pandemic. The UK housing market is coming back down to earth after a strong three years, not falling off a cliff.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This also isn\u8217\'20\'19t the first report to show a fall in house prices. Last week, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jun/30/uk-house-prices-rise-in-june-but-annual-rate-falls-mortgage-nationwide"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nationwide}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jun/30/uk-house-prices-rise-in-june-but-annual-rate-falls-mortgage-nationwide"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  reported that annual prices fell at the fastest rate since 2009}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.30am BST\par \par \uc2 The regional picture: Prices fall fastest in southern England\par \par \uc2 The South of England remains the area where house prices are facing the most downward pressure, as higher interest rates hit the market.\par \par \uc2 At -3.0%, the annual fall in the South East was the largest since July 2011, knocking the average price down to \u163\'00\'A3384,106.\par \par \uc2 In London, prices fell by 2.6% \u8211\'20\'13 matching the national average fall \u8211\'20\'13 but the biggest drop recorded since October 2009. This pulled the average price of property sold in the capital to \u163\'00\'A3533,057, a drop of around \u163\'00\'A315,000 over the last year.\par \par \uc2 Welsh house prices fell by 1.8%, the first annual fall in the principality sine 2013, while they were only slightly lower in Scotland (-0.1%, the first annual drop in three years).\par \par \uc2 Average house prices are now falling on an annual basis in most parts of the UK.\par \par \uc2 But the West Midlands (+1.5%), Yorkshire & Humberside (+0.2%) and Northern Ireland (+0.2%) bucked the trend.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.23am BST\par \par \uc2 The average price of new build property prices rose by 1.9% in the year to June \u8211\'20\'13 stronger than the wider market, but the slowest rate in over three years.\par \par \uc2 Existing properties, Halifax says \u8220\'20\'1Cwere instrumental in driving prices up during the pandemic related housing rush\u8221\'20\'1D, were down by -3.5% year-on-year in June. That was the steepest decline since August 2009.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.21am BST\par \par \uc2 enltrAvg house prices begin to show signs of wear & tear as they fall for the 3rd consecutive month by -0.1% to \u163\'00\'A3285,932. When pitched against June 2022 growth peak, house prices faired far worse decreasing by -2.6% = the largest fall since 2011 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HalifaxBank?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @HalifaxBank}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/uKpNVlVLOx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/uKpNVlVLOx}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1677200664902901762?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 July 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Here are the key points from Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s new house price report, just released.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Average house price fell by -0.1% in June, a third consecutive monthly decline\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Annual rate of house price growth fell to -2.6%, from -1.1% in May\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Typical UK property now costs \u163\'00\'A3285,932 (vs peak of \u163\'00\'A3293,992 last August)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 New build prices more resilient compared to existing homes\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Southern England sees most downward pressure on property prices\par \par \uc2 A graph showing UK house prices Photograph: Halifax\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Halifax reports fall in UK house prices\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 UK house prices fell at the fastest annual pace since June 2011 last month, Halifax reports this morning, as interest rate rises cool the market.\par \par \uc2 The latest healthcheck on the UK housing market found that the average price of a home sold in June fell by 2.6% year-on-year, the largest drop in 12 years on Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s index.\par \par \uc2 That follows a 1.1% fall in the year to May.\par \par \uc2 On a monthly basis, prices dipped by 0.1%, taking the average price to \u163\'00\'A3285,932.\par \par \uc2 Kim Kinnaird, director at Halifax Mortgages, says:\par \par \uc2 With very little movement in house prices over recent months, this rate of decline largely reflects the impact of historically high house prices last summer \u8211\'20\'13 annual growth peaked at +12.5% in June 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 supported by the temporary Stamp Duty cut.\par \par \uc2 Despite June\u8217\'20\'19s dip, average house prices are actually up by +1.5% (or \u163\'00\'A34,000) so far this year. Most of that growth came in the first quarter of 2023, following the sharp fall in prices we saw at the end of last year in the aftermath of the mini-budget.\par \par \uc2 However, Kinnaird warns that the housing market remains sensitive to volatility in borrowing costs, adding:\par \par \uc2 Concerns about persistent inflation have led to a significant increase in the cost of funding. Coupled with base rate rising by another 50bp, this contributed to a big jump in typical mortgage rates over the last month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe resulting squeeze on affordability will inevitably act as a brake on demand, as buyers consider what they can realistically afford to offer. While there\u8217\'20\'19s always a lag effect when rates go up, many existing mortgage holders with variable deals or rolling off fixed rates will likely face an increase in the next year.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, financial data provider Moneyfacts reported that the average two-year fixed rate mortgage rose again to 6.52%, the highest since last October.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/charts/embed/jul/2023-07-03-09:41:05/embed.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\par \par \uc2 Reaction to follow\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up today\par \par \uc2 Financial markets are on edge after a sharp selloff in shares and bonds yesterday. It knocked the UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 down by 2.2% to its lowest closing level since last November, while government borrowing costs soared to a 15-year high.\par \par \uc2 The rout was triggered by fresh fears of higher interest rates, after US companies added many more jobs than expected last month, around 497,000.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/06/global-share-prices-fall-higher-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 FTSE 100 falls to lowest closing level in 2023 as interest rate fears grip markets}}}\par \par \uc2 Asia-Pacific stock have joined the selloff today, with Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Nikkei down 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 Today we get the official US jobs report. which was expected to show an increase of 225k jobs, down from May\u8217\'20\'19s 339K.\par \par \uc2 A strong jobs report could cause more market jitters, as good economic news will spur central banks to keep tightening policy.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 7am BST: Halifax house price index for June\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 7am BST: German industrial production for May\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am BST: An overview of UK productivity in January to March 2023\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: US non-farm payroll jobs report for June\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 3.30pm BST: \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  interest rate-setter \uc2 Catherine Mann\uc2  speaks in a panel discussion in New York\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230707ej77000p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
