\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Teen dies in sawmill accident as US states aim to roll back child labor laws\b0\par\par\uc2 Maya Yang \par \uc2 573 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2023\par 16:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Investigation underway after police were called to find unresponsive teenager last week at Florence Hardwoods sawmill\par \par \uc2 A 16-year-old boy has died following an industrial accident at a sawmill in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Police received an early-morning call last Thursday regarding an unresponsive teenager at Florence Hardwoods, a sawmill in northern Wisconsin, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/photo/?fbid=572782875016299&set=a.157941546500436"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to}}}\uc2  the Florence county sheriff\u8217\'20\'19s office. Deputies and paramedics transported the teenager to a local hospital before transferring him to Milwaukee children\u8217\'20\'19s hospital.\par \par \uc2 The boy, whom authorities have not identified by name, died from his injuries on Saturday. \uc2 The Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  has been notified about the incident and an investigation is taking place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur thoughts and prayers go out to his family, friends, students and co-workers,\u8221\'20\'1D the sheriff\u8217\'20\'19s department said.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  page {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gofundme.com/f/michael-schuls"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appears}}}\uc2  to have been set up for the family following the incident, saying: \u8220\'20\'1COur small community is in absolute shock.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis tremendous loss will bring new life to seven more people, including his mother,\u8221\'20\'1D the page added in reference to the teenager\u8217\'20\'19s status as an organ donor.\par \par \uc2 Fourteen states across the country \u8211\'20\'13 including Wisconsin \u8211\'20\'13 have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/iowa-governor-signs-one-of-the-most-dangerous-rollbacks-of-child-labor-laws-in-the-country-14-states-have-now-introduced-bills-putting-children-at-risk/#update"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 introduced }}}\uc2  proposals to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2023/may/01/us-surge-efforts-reduce-child-labor-regulations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 roll back}}}\uc2  child labor protections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe trend reflects a coordinated multi-industry push to expand employer access to low-wage labor and weaken state child labor laws in ways that contradict federal protections,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/iowa-governor-signs-one-of-the-most-dangerous-rollbacks-of-child-labor-laws-in-the-country-14-states-have-now-introduced-bills-putting-children-at-risk/#update"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to }}}\uc2  the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the Guardian, Skip Mark, a \uc2 University of Rhode Island\uc2  professor specializing in labor and human rights, noted the prevalence of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/child-labour"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 child labor}}}\uc2  in the country\u8217\'20\'19s agriculture industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe US Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 helped limit child labor in many ways. However, it did and still does not apply to the agricultural sector, where most child labor in the US and most child injuries and deaths occur,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mark. \u8220\'20\'1CChild labor is most common in agriculture where children are maimed, killed, exposed to dangerous chemicals, underpaid, and we\u8217\'20\'19ve known about these issues for decades.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CChild labor is hard to measure, and most estimates are terrible and should not be taken at face value. Governments don\u8217\'20\'19t want to report how bad child labor is because it makes them look bad.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBusinesses don\u8217\'20\'19t want to report that they have hired children because then they would have to pay fines. Children and their parents don\u8217\'20\'19t want to report child labor because they need the job to support themselves and would lose their job if it was reported.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Terri Gerstein, the director of the Project on State and Local Enforcement at the Harvard Law School Center for Labor and a Just Economy, echoed similar sentiments.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo stop violations of child labor laws, we need more funding for enforcement, higher penalties, and clear, attainable ways to hold lead corporations accountable for violations in their supply chains,\u8221\'20\'1D Gerstein told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 In a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/2023-06-14%20-%20Testimony%20-%20Gerstein.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  testimony}}}\uc2  to the Senate judiciary committee, Gerstein laid out various law reform measures surrounding the issue.\par \par \uc2 Those include creating a private right of action for child labor violations, creating and funding a right to counsel for unaccompanied minors in their immigration cases which would increase the chances of detecting child labor violations, as well as adding liquidated or compensatory damages to the child labor statute which would help compensate victims for their experiences.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230706ej76001xi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
