\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Union wins at New York farms raise hopes for once-powerful UFW\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse in New York \par \uc2 1,299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2023\par 10:36 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Five hundred farmworkers have unionized \u8211\'20\'13 the biggest success in years for the union co-founded by Cesar Chavez\par \par \uc2 For decades, the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a powerhouse under American labor leader and civil rights activist Cesar Chavez in the 1970s \u8211\'20\'13 has declined in size and prominence, its membership falling from 60,000 to around 6,000. Now, after years of scant success in organizing, the UFW has something big to boast about: it has unionized 500 workers at five farms in New York state.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the union\u8217\'20\'19s biggest organizing success in years, and the first time the California-based union has organized in the north-east. The New York victories will increase the union\u8217\'20\'19s membership by 8%, with some labor experts saying these successes show new promise and energy in the long-languishing UFW.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s extraordinary,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://law.stanford.edu/directory/william-b-gould-iv/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 William B Gould IV}}}\uc2 , a labor law professor at Stanford and former chairman of the California Agricultural Relations Board. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a sharp contrast with everything we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen. There was considerable lethargy with the UFW. This is a significant departure. It\u8217\'20\'19s very surprising and welcome.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In New York state, the UFW has won union drives at four orchards and one vegetable farm. The vast majority of those unionized are workers from Jamaica or Mexico with H-2A visas to do seasonal work.\par \par \uc2 Teresa Romero, president of the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2 , told the Guardian that the victories were made possible by a four-year-old New York law \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dol.ny.gov/farm-laborers-fair-labor-practices-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Farm Laborers Fair Labor Practices Act}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which gives farm workers a state-protected right to unionize and prohibits retaliation against farm workers seeking to organize. To ease unionization, the law requires farms to recognize a union once a majority of workers sign pro-union cards.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s amazing. When the laws change, workers really can win,\u8221\'20\'1D Romero said.\par \par \uc2 Explaining why the workers unionized, she said: \u8220\'20\'1CDignity and respect is one reason. In many cases, the housing for farm workers is deplorable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Junior Johnson, a Jamaican worker at Wafler Farms, an apple grower, said one reason he backed unionization was the immense pressure to work fast. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have no say. We have no rights,\u8221\'20\'1D Johnson said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we go to the morning meeting and a worker wants to complain, the boss says, \u8216\'20\'18I don\u8217\'20\'19t want to hear it.\u8217\'20\'19 We have no one beside the union to stand up for us. We have to keep our mouths closed.\u8221\'20\'1D Some workers say that if workers speak up, they are told to stay home that day and thus not get paid.\par \par \uc2 Some growers have pressed workers not to sign union cards, telling them they could avoid paying 3% of their pay toward union dues. But Johnson said: \u8220\'20\'1CBecause of what we go through daily, I\u8217\'20\'19d be happy to pay more than 3% just to have someone to stand up for us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Owen Salmon, a fellow Wafler worker from Jamaica, said: \u8220\'20\'1CIf a worker speaks up, you can lose your job\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 meaning a seasonal worker is not brought back the following year. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you don\u8217\'20\'19t speak up, you keep your job.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Santos Mendoza Escamilla, a Mexican worker at Lynn-Ette & Sons, said: \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes we are pushed to work so hard, it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t feel doable. It was always the boss\u8217\'20\'19s word. Now with a union, we feel we have someone pushing back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The UFW says it has unionized four apple farms \u8211\'20\'13 Wafler Farms, Cahoon Farms, Porpiglia and A&J Kirby \u8211\'20\'13 and a vegetable grower, Lynn-Ette. The three biggest unionized farms, Wafler, Cahoon and Lynn-Ette, did not respond to questions from the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 Two other unions have also been organizing farm workers in New York: the Retail Wholesale and Department Store Union (RWDSU) and the \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers\uc2 . They have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://documentedny.com/2021/10/07/first-farmworker-union-forms-in-new-york-state/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 organized}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://documentedny.com/2021/10/07/first-farmworker-union-forms-in-new-york-state/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two vineyards}}}\uc2 , a dairy farm and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ufcw.org/actions/victories/regular-and-seasonal-farmworkers-form-their-union-with-ufcw-local-888/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several other farms}}}\uc2 , around 125 workers in all.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was impossible to unionize farm workers before the new law,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jessica Garcia, assistant to the president of the Retail Wholesale union. \u8220\'20\'1CThe new law, it\u8217\'20\'19s made it possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jessica Ramos, a state senator from Queens, was the main sponsor of the farm worker legislation. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is something that people thought couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be done,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, noting that progressive lawmakers had struggled for over a decade to enact the legislation. She said the law passed because in 2018, Democrats won control of the state senate for the first time in a decade, giving the party trifecta control.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe wanted to unapologetically create this environment where the UFW, the RWDSU and other organizations could organize,\u8221\'20\'1D Ramos said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe wanted farmworkers to have a life with dignity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The National Labor Relations Act, passed in 1935, gave most private-sector workers a federally protected right to unionize. That law did not cover farm workers largely because southern senators wanted to exclude the region\u8217\'20\'19s farm laborers, most of them Black, from coverage.\par \par \uc2 Kerry Kennedy, head of RFK Human Rights, a nonprofit advocacy group, lobbied hard to enact the New York law. Her father, Bobby Kennedy, was a strong supporter of the UFW and Chavez. Kennedy even lobbied in Albany alongside \uc2 Dolores Huerta\uc2 , who co-founded the UFW with Chavez.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe became involved with farm workers in New York because they had no right to form a union,\u8221\'20\'1D Kennedy said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey could be fired for attempting to form a union, and they were systematically threatened with deportation if they complained to their employer about working conditions. They had no right to a day off each week. No overtime pay. No unemployment insurance. We worked for about a decade to pass the farm worker Fair Labor Practices Act, and that remedied nearly all those issues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Romero said she hoped the UFW would create partnerships with unionized farms to help both workers and growers.\par \par \uc2 Steve Ammerman\uc2 , communications director for the \uc2 New York Farm Bureau\uc2 , a growers association which lobbied against the unionization law, said only a handful of farms are going through contract negotiation. He said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt must be a fair process on both sides of the equation, as there have been concerns raised over questionable tactics and false promises used to encourage farmworkers to sign union authorization cards.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 UFW officials denied making any false promises.\par \par \uc2 Ammerman added that higher wages make it more difficult \u8220\'20\'1Cfor farms in particular, to be able to compete in the marketplace\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The UFW is seeking to organize more New York farms to add to its five victories \u8211\'20\'13 quite a contrast to California, where there have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calmatters.org/california-divide/2023/03/farmworkers-union-vote-by-mail/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 just six union elections for farm workers since 2016}}}\uc2 . Labor experts say it has been hard to unionize farm workers in California because the UFW could not use card check \u8211\'20\'13 although a new California law now enables that \u8211\'20\'13 and because many farms are large operations and fiercely anti-union. Moreover, newly unionized growers sometimes refused to ever agree to a first contract, a bitter frustration for workers.\par \par \uc2 With California\u8217\'20\'19s new card check law, Antonio De Loera-Brust, the UFW\u8217\'20\'19s communications director, said the successes in New York are \u8220\'20\'1Ca good preview of what we hope to achieve in California in the next couple of years\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 UFW president Romero said that only three states \u8211\'20\'13 New York, California and Hawaii \u8211\'20\'13 have laws \u8220\'20\'1Cwhere farm workers can organize.\u8221\'20\'1D UFW officials point to a conflict in Washington state, where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.yakimaherald.com/news/local/farmworkers-march-to-ostrom-mushroom-farms-announce-vote-for-unionization/article_a7e76158-3786-11ed-90ed-5336c4977bd8.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a majority of workers at a large mushroom farm}}}\uc2  voted to unionize, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ufw.org/ostrom-mushroom-sold-ufw-condemns-treatment-of-mushroom-workers-by-new-anti-union-management/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the company refused to recognize}}}\uc2  or bargain with the UFW. That state\u8217\'20\'19s law does not require farms to do so.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you\u8217\'20\'19re able to change the law and make it easier and safer for workers to organize, it is always good,\u8221\'20\'1D Romero said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230706ej760018p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
