\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18More work in fewer hours\u8217\'20\'19: LA\u8217\'20\'19s hotel workers detail backbreaking conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh \par \uc2 746 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 July 2023\par 10:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Striking employees, who accuse the hotels of cutting staff levels and increasing workloads, want an immediate $5 hourly raise\par \par \uc2 Thousands of southern California hotel workers have gone on strike over the holiday weekend, pushing for better wages in one of the most expensive metro areas in the US.\par \par \uc2 About 15,000 cleaners, cooks, cloakroom attendants, front desk agents, bellmen, servers and dishwashers were picketing across Los Angeles and Orange counties. During one of the busiest tourist weekends in the region, workers are demanding wage increases amid a spiraling cost-of-living crisis in the region.\par \par \uc2 Jose Luis Zepeda, 74, was protesting outside the LA Grand Hotel, where he has been working as a cook. \u8220\'20\'1CEverything has been going up,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe cost of insurance, gas, household expenses.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Zepeda, who has worked in the hotel industry for more than 40 years, moved east to Victorville a few years back because he couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford to live in LA. Now he drives two hours to get to work \u8211\'20\'13 and he\u8217\'20\'19s not the only one. A survey by \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11 found that 53% of workers had moved in the past five years, or were planning to move due to rising housing costs.\par \par \uc2 The union is demanding a $5 hourly wage increase immediately, plus a $3 bump each year for 2024 and 2025. It is also asking for affordable healthcare, better pensions and safe workloads. Hotels have used the Covid-19 pandemic as an excuse to cut staffing levels and increase workloads, workers say.\par \par \uc2 Zepeda said he tried retiring, but came back to work \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause I want to, I\u8217\'20\'19m not sick, I have no reason not to,\u8221\'20\'1D he said \u8211\'20\'13 and he wanted to continue to support his family.\par \par \uc2 So far, the union\u8217\'20\'19s biggest employer, the Westin Bonaventure, has reached an agreement with striking workers, agreeing to wage increases as well as providing affordable health insurance at less than $20 a month, and increases in pension contributions. The deal, which was reached a day before workers went on strike, also addressed workload issues and adopted a policy against the use of a federal system for checking employment eligibility, which imperils immigrant workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow all of these other hotels need to do the same \u8211\'20\'13 and just sign the agreement,\u8221\'20\'1D said Maria Hernandez, a communications director at the union.\par \par \uc2 Talks between the union and a coalition of 40 other hotels have stalled. The coalition is offering a $2.50 hourly wage increase over the next 12 months, and $6.25 over four years.\par \par \uc2 But the union has countered that the industry, which received billions in Covid relief funds, should commit to a significant pay bump for workers who are struggling to make rent in LA. Tourism and hotel occupancy levels have recovered, and ahead of the upcoming Fifa World Cup in 2026 and the summer Olympics in 2028 \u8211\'20\'13 which are expected to bring in record visitors and profits for the industry \u8211\'20\'13 workers are demanding improvements to their contracts.\par \par \uc2 A big point of contention the union-backed contract addresses is the pandemic-era elimination of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jul/05/overworked-underpaid-hotel-workers-employees"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 daily room cleanings}}}\uc2 . Rather than wiping down and touching up rooms on a daily basis, workers are given fewer hours to deep-clean days of buildup after long stays. Several large hotel corporations have proposed permanently reducing their housekeeping staff, which could result in $4.8bn in lost wages for workers across the industry.\par \par \uc2 Iris Acosta, 55, said she had developed shoulder and knee injuries because of the backbreaking nature of her housekeeping job. \u8220\'20\'1CTo the surprise of everyone, during the pandemic, the hotel was renovated, and now there is glass everywhere,\u8221\'20\'1D which she now painstakingly cleans, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are expecting us to do more work in fewer hours.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On top of that, management had threatened to cut health benefits, she said \u8211\'20\'13 which would make it harder for her to get a necessary surgery for her injuries and afford her monthly medications. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m a cancer survivor, and I need a lot of medications,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI really need that health insurance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since immigrating from Honduras to the US 32 years ago, Acosta said, she\u8217\'20\'19s been working the whole time \u8211\'20\'13 and she still doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have enough money to live, let alone retire in the US. \u8220\'20\'1CWith the cost of living here \u8211\'20\'13 I cannot live,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo we are waiting \u8211\'20\'13 waiting for a deal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230704ej7400130\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
