\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Not for employee use\u8217\'20\'19: why are US retail workers being denied chairs?\b0\par\par\uc2 Alaina Demopoulos \par \uc2 1,407 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2023\par 10:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees condemn workplaces that deny them rest, leaving them in pain and leading to class-action suits\par \par \uc2 When Zay clocked into her customer service job one recent morning, she noticed things looked different. There were no chairs in the break room. She had nowhere to sit at the table where she usually files invoices. When she reached the back of the store, there was one lonely folding chair propped against the wall. \u8220\'20\'1CNot for employee use,\u8221\'20\'1D read a handwritten note taped on the metal.\par \par \uc2 When employees asked their boss what had happened, they learned about a new no-sitting policy. Hopefully, the business owner said, this would \u8220\'20\'1Cincrease worker productivity\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But Zay, who is 27 and lives in Atlanta, said it was hard to feel productive when she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t rest during her breaks, especially as the process of opening the store required a lot of heavy lifting. It could leave her feeling back pain for the rest of the day, and sitting down made things much more comfortable. (Zay asked that her last name not be used.)\par \par \uc2 I think it\u8217\'20\'19s a very classist thing. No one would dare criticize a lawyer for sitting on the job\par \par \uc2 Standing for long periods of time is part of the job description for servers, cashiers and retail employees \u8211\'20\'13 and managers want it that way. In the 1960s, \uc2 Ray Kroc\uc2 , the CEO who took McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s from a small-time local chain to a global franchise, had a catchphrase: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19ve got time to lean, you\u8217\'20\'19ve got time to clean.\u8221\'20\'1D Today, it\u8217\'20\'19s still uttered by bosses in kitchens and warehouses to deter workers from slacking off \u8211\'20\'13 or merely taking breaks.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a uniquely American ethos. Cashiers in Europe, and notably at the German grocery store \uc2 Aldi\uc2 , are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fastcompany.com/90836431/why-dont-cashiers-in-the-u-s-get-to-sit-like-the-ones-in-europe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 welcome to sit down}}}\uc2  during their shifts. Zay said a British friend who had visited the US recently was jarred by the sight of workers constantly on their feet: \u8220\'20\'1CWhy are they all standing?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The same rules do not apply, obviously, to people with desk jobs. (Though sedentary workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2023/may/14/should-i-worry-about-how-long-i-spend-sitting-down"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 face their own share}}}\uc2  of ailments that come from a life of sitting down from 9 to 5.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s a very classist thing,\u8221\'20\'1D Zay said. \u8220\'20\'1CNo one would dare criticize a lawyer for sitting on the job. But in retail or the service industry, when the job is deemed to be unskilled labor, people get irrationally offended when they see you sitting.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite her boss\u8217\'20\'19s hope, Zay does not think removing chairs will make anyone work harder. In fact, she knows it has gutted staff morale. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t wrap my mind around the idea of standing being more productive, because from my perspective, people are much less productive when they\u8217\'20\'19re in pain,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe irrationality of it is so astounding.\u8221\'20\'1D\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The boss\u8217\'20\'19s warning has not stopped Zay from finding a spot to sit down. She\u8217\'20\'19s noticed there\u8217\'20\'19s a patch of floor that is just out of reach of security cameras. There, she plops down in between customers.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , there is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blogs.cdc.gov/niosh-science-blog/2014/12/09/standing/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ample evidence}}}\uc2  documenting the negative health effects of standing too long at work. Some of it is obvious: fatigue, lower back pain, tiredness and swollen legs. But there are long-term risks also associated with being on one\u8217\'20\'19s feet for too long: it increases the risk of cardiovascular problems and adverse pregnancy outcomes.\par \par \uc2 Zay posted about her experience on the subreddit r/antiwork, a group for people who \u8220\'20\'1Cwant more information on anti-work ideas and want personal help with their own jobs/work-related struggles\u8221\'20\'1D. The post received more than 1,500 comments, in which service, retail and other customer-facing workers agreed that they were made to stand at work for arbitrary reasons.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEmployers who expect their staff to stand during an entire shift deserve exactly zero employees,\u8221\'20\'1D one user wrote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe no-sit-down policy is the stupidest thing ever created by managers,\u8221\'20\'1D another said.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@lillipasquinelli/video/7243559586110885166?q=lilli%20pasquinelli&t=1687543028840"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 TikTok video}}}\uc2  viewed more than 2.8m times, Dairy Queen employees showed how they passed the time while being denied seats in the kitchen. One squatted on the ground, another balanced precariously on a trash can and another did lunges with a mini stool.\par \par \uc2 Courtney, who is 35 and lives in Ohio, worked for five years as a cashier at a large cosmetics retail chain. There was a strict no-sitting policy, and workers who were caught resting would be written up. Sometimes she would go home after a long shift and cry when she took her shoes off. \u8220\'20\'1CMy feet were red, swollen and painful to touch,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI dropped 40 pounds [20kg] from walking over 12,000 steps a day working there.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Eva Keller used to work at a hotel in Anaheim, California, that did not allow employees to sit while working the front desk. That meant she became used to standing in one spot for eight hours a day while wearing uncomfortable dress shoes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI had cramping and charley horses in my feet,\u8221\'20\'1D Keller said. \u8220\'20\'1CI wore flats with inserts in them and we had cushions on the floor, but that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t help if you\u8217\'20\'19re doing 40 hours a week.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even after leaving her old job, Keller notices when she\u8217\'20\'19s at a business that does not allow its employees to sit. \u8220\'20\'1CI always notice it,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would never check into a hotel and ask to file a complaint with a manager because the person at the front desk was sitting. That\u8217\'20\'19s just insane.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers have advocated for the right to sit for over a century. In the 1880s, factory bosses took away chairs in the hope of speeding up production times. Their reasoning for doing so does not sound much different from what a store manager might tell their subordinates today. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was thought that workers would be more attentive and engaged if they were standing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Robert Bruno, a professor and director of the labor education department at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign. \u8220\'20\'1CIdeologically, it was a way for the business to maintain control over the workers\u8217\'20\'19 bodies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But employees fought back, and the \u8220\'20\'1Cright to sit\u8221\'20\'1D movement became a pillar for early union organizers. Some states initially passed laws requiring that women have seats at work, due to their supposed delicateness.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe initial labor legislation was clearly establishing a gender difference, that women shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be expected to work more than 12 hours, and that they should have some relief against having to stand, which was thought to be too burdensome,\u8221\'20\'1D Bruno said. \u8220\'20\'1CEventually, labor organizers understood that this was something that should apply to all workers, male and female.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By the 1910s, most states had right-to-sit laws. Some laws affecting women only were later repealed on the grounds of sex discrimination.\par \par \uc2 More than a hundred years later, American workers again find themselves advocating for the most basic of provisions. In 2018, \uc2 Walmart{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-walmart-lawsuit/walmart-to-pay-65-million-to-settle-lawsuit-over-seating-for-cashiers-idUSKCN1ML265"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed}}}\uc2  to pay $65m to 100,000 California cashiers in a class-action suit; they accused the company of failing to provide seating in accordance with state law. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  and \uc2 Safeway\uc2  paid $15m and $12m, respectively, in similar suits.\par \par \uc2 Last year, New York state lawmakers introduced legislation that would require employers to provide \u8220\'20\'1Csuitable seats\u8221\'20\'1D to all workers. The bill\u8217\'20\'19s sponsor, a Bronx assembly member named Karines Reyes, is also a registered nurse and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox5ny.com/news/standing-is-tiring-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 says}}}\uc2  the topic is personal. \u8220\'20\'1CMaking accommodations for employees to have that option, to perhaps stand and sit whatever their duties allow them to, I think is fair and humane,\u8221\'20\'1D she told a local news reporter.\par \par \uc2 Zay, the Georgia worker, quit after her boss took away seating. She says she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t the only employee to put in their notice after what her team has started to call chair-gate. Ultimately, she said, the move was emblematic of a toxic workplace in general.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI want to point out that it\u8217\'20\'19s employers like these who go on and on about how no one wants to work because they can\u8217\'20\'19t accept the simple truth that no one wants to do back-breaking labor with insulting pay all for a boss who couldn\u8217\'20\'19t care less about their wellbeing,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230628ej6s00139\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
