\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Americans are hungry to be part of unions. So why is US labor so timid?\b0\par\par\uc2 Hamilton Nolan \par \uc2 1,057 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2023\par 10:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Support for organized labor is soaring. Yet the biggest US union coalition endorsed Biden without asking anything in return\par \par \uc2 At a splashy event in Philadelphia last weekend, the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , America\u8217\'20\'19s largest union coalition, announced its endorsement of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for president in 2024. You may notice that the election is still 17 months away. This was the earliest endorsement in the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s history, amounting to an all-in bet by organized labor that the interests of the Democratic president are identical to its own. The problem with this is not so much that labor might have decided to endorse a Republican \u8211\'20\'13 whoever that party\u8217\'20\'19s candidate is, they are sure to despise the concept of working-class empowerment \u8211\'20\'13 but rather the fact that the endorsement is an implicit acceptance of the status quo.\par \par \uc2 These union leaders believe that the Biden White House as currently constituted is the best they can hope to get. Indeed, they are overjoyed by what they have gotten already. It is this lack of ambition that is the labor movement\u8217\'20\'19s biggest flaw. They have been beaten down for so long that they have lost their ability to believe that the world they deserve will ever be real. This is a sort of trauma, induced by a decades-long decline in union power. By settling for what they have, unfortunately, they have forsaken their leverage to ask for more.\par \par \uc2 At his speech accepting the endorsement, Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/17/us/politics/biden-again-has-union-support-but-the-unions-look-different-this-time.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declared}}}\uc2  himself \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most pro-union president in American history\u8221\'20\'1D. That may be a bit much \u8211\'20\'13 a few generations ago, even Republican presidents {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2020/09/06/opinion/labor-unions-republicans.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supported}}}\uc2  unions, so the standards were much higher \u8211\'20\'13 but it is certainly true that Biden is the most pro-union president of the past half century.\par \par \uc2 He signed a $36bn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/12/08/fact-sheet-president-biden-announces-historic-relief-to-protect-hard-earned-pensions-of-hundreds-of-thousands-of-union-workers-and-retirees/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bill}}}\uc2  to save union pensions; his Covid relief and infrastructure bills were boons to union workers; his nominee for \uc2 NLRB\uc2  general counsel has been tirelessly pushing invaluable labor law reforms; and he has, to a degree not seen before in my lifetime, used his bully pulpit to speak out in favor of union drives, in ways that Clinton or Obama never would have. The \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  feels that its voice is being listened to in the White House more than they can ever recall.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the single biggest labor issue of Biden\u8217\'20\'19s first term was the potential national rail strike, which he dealt with by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/dec/01/joe-biden-rail-strike-labor-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 crushing}}}\uc2  the workers\u8217\'20\'19 right to strike and imposing a contract on them that they didn\u8217\'20\'19t want. But hey, what\u8217\'20\'19s the occasional knife in the back between friends?\par \par \uc2 Seventy-one percent of Americans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/398303/approval-labor-unions-highest-point-1965.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 say}}}\uc2  they approve of labor unions. Only 40% of Americans say they approve of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Unions are more popular than the president, by a long mile. In fact, the popularity of organized labor is at a 60-year high. This is due not to the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , nor to the White House, but to a realization that swept working people across the nation as the Covid pandemic paralyzed society: your job does not care if you live or die. Your boss will not save you from disaster. There is no safety net, except for unions. That\u8217\'20\'19s it.\par \par \uc2 The wave of interest in labor organizing that has swept through coffee shops, warehouses and college campuses is fueled by a widening, bone-deep understanding that solidarity is the only shield against capitalism\u8217\'20\'19s scorching rays. I can attest, from years spent traveling America as a labor reporter, that this grassroots enthusiasm is real. It is the job of the labor movement\u8217\'20\'19s institutions to turn that enthusiasm into the maximum possible gain. That\u8217\'20\'19s where the malfunction is. We have an army ready to fight the class war led by generals who have been trained to assume that it is unwinnable.\par \par \uc2 Unions do not have to endorse the Biden agenda. Unions can set the agenda. Now is not the time to settle. Now is the time to demand. The labor market is strong, the appetite for unions is high and the discontent with inequality is everywhere. This is a time to push the president, not bow and scrape and thank him for what he has done. Working people are begging to become a part of a strong labor movement.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  and its unions could find within themselves the ambition to take advantage of current conditions to organize 10 or 20 million new union members, they could quite literally reverse the post-Reagan inequality crisis. Labor\u8217\'20\'19s early endorsement of Biden is meant to enable unions to start mobilizing their political operation now \u8211\'20\'13 but that mobilization is also their leverage. They could demand that Biden commit to federal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inthesetimes.com/article/government-fund-union-organizing-amazon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 funding}}}\uc2  for union organizing and to abolishing the filibuster so that the labor law reforms of the PRO Act might actually have a chance to pass, rather than just serving as a campaign slogan. In short, they could make the building of the labor movement itself their central political demand.\par \par \uc2 Biden will be out of power in a few years, but a labor movement with 10 million new union members would transform the entire American political landscape for decades to come.\par \par \uc2 Instead of taking this tack, the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  has committed to organize only a paltry 100,000 new union members a year (which would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inthesetimes.com/article/afl-cio-union-federation-organizing-goal-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ensure}}}\uc2  the continuing decline of union density) and handed its support to Biden in exchange for the gifts he has already given. It is not a strategy that will ever be mistaken for a master class in boldly seizing the initiative.\par \par \uc2 History\u8217\'20\'19s greatest labor leaders have not been conservative pragmatists in search of marginal gain. They have been people whose outrage over the injustices of the present fueled them to accomplish things that others dismissed as unrealistic. Too much time spent inside of existing institutions seems to extinguish this spirit. The next generation of working-class heroes is out in the world right now \u8211\'20\'13 working. It will be up to them to force the labor movement to thrive in spite of itself, long after the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost pro-union president in history\u8221\'20\'1D is gone.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hamiltonnolan.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hamilton Nolan}}}\uc2  is a labor journalist based in New York\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230621ej6l001e7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
