\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 Apple TV+ remake of sci-fi classic Metropolis cancelled due to US writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Amanda Meade \par \uc2 582 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2023\par 08:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The $188m eight-part series was to be filmed in Melbourne but has been shelved because of \u8216\'20\'18push costs and uncertainty\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The $188m Apple TV+ remake of Fritz Lang\u8217\'20\'19s 1927 sci-fi classic {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/metropolis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Metropolis}}}\uc2  has been cancelled.\par \par \uc2 The major project was in pre-production in Melbourne when \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Universal Studio Group announced the eight-part series would not go ahead.\par \par \uc2 The cancellation has been blamed on the US writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike because drafts of the scripts had not been finished before the start of the strike in May when the Writers Guild of America walked off the job over falling rates of pay.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPush costs and uncertainty related to the ongoing [US writers\u8217\'20\'19] strike led to this difficult decision,\u8221\'20\'1D UCP said in a statement, first reported by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2023/06/metropolis-tv-series-dead-writers-strike-sam-esmail-ucp-apple-tv-plus-production-costs-1235419899/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Deadline}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The series, directed by Sam Esmail, had cast Briana Middleton and Lindy Booth in major roles, but few other details were initially available.\par \par \uc2 Metropolis is considered by many critics as one of the greatest sci-fi films of all time, dealing with themes such as class conflict, industrialisation and the perils of mechanised modernity.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian film reviewer Peter Bradshaw {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2010/sep/09/metropolis-restored-film-review"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called it \u8220\'20\'1Ca crazed futurist epic\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  and a \u8220\'20\'1Cdystopian nightmare about a city-state built on slave labour, whose prosperity depends on suppressing a mutinous underground race whose insurrectionist rage is beginning to bubble\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The project had been attracted to film at Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s Docklands Studios by funding incentives from Australian state and federal governments.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian government and the federal government chipped in $41.6m and $83.8m respectively in location incentive funding for a number of projects from NBC Universal\u8217\'20\'19s UCP including Metropolis.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian government says it is \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappointing\u8221\'20\'1D it won\u8217\'20\'19t go ahead but the $41.6m incentive fee had not been paid yet.\par \par \uc2 Under location incentive funding, the final payment is only made once final Australian expenditure is incurred on the production.\par \par \uc2 The federal location incentive program and the Victorian government\u8217\'20\'19s screen incentive {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.paulfletcher.com.au/media-releases/location-incentive-attracts-metropolis-to-film-in-australia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scheme boasted}}}\uc2  that they had attracted \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the most technically ambitious film and television projects ever to film in Australia\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The government said the projects would contribute up to $621m in direct spending over five years; provide work to 700 cast and crew; employ 2,500 people directly on Metropolis and see 700 local businesses and service providers benefit.\par \par \uc2 In 2021 a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2021/jun/08/gods-favorite-idiot-netflix-pulls-out-early-from-melissa-mccarthys-australian-production?utm_term=Autofeed&CMP=soc_568&utm_medium=Social&utm_source=Twitter#Echobox=1623141274"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Netflix series with $10m}}}\uc2  in incentive funding, Melissa McCarthy\u8217\'20\'19s God\u8217\'20\'19s Favorite Idiot, was axed after only half of the series finished shooting.\par \par \uc2 Metropolis and God\u8217\'20\'19s Favorite Idiot were enticed to Australia through the incentive program, which allows overseas productions to recoup up to 13.5% of production expenses incurred in Australia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the withdrawal of any production is disappointing, Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s partnership with NBCU remains in place with a significant estimated expenditure to be delivered over the coming years,\u8221\'20\'1D a VicScreen spokesperson said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe funds initially earmarked for Metropolis will be allocated to another project or projects to be delivered through the partnership.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Part of the deal included the Victorian government contributing $12.5m to the cost of building a virtual production facility at Docklands. VicScreen says the facility will be used for other productions and education and training activities.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson from the department of the arts said no payments under the location incentive scheme have been made to the production because principal photography had not started.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230620ej6k000xi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
