\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 More than 60% of staff at Queensland detention centre quit amid record influx of young people\b0\par\par\uc2 Ben Smee \par \uc2 631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2023\par 22:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Use of solitary confinement due to chronic staffing shortages at Cleveland detention centre has detrimental effect on children, advocates sayFollow our Australia news live blog for the latest updatesGet our morning and afternoon news emails, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 More than 60% of the workforce at the troubled Cleveland youth detention centre in north Queensland quit during the past three years, data obtained by Guardian Australia shows.\par \par \uc2 The figures supplied by the youth justice department show total staff numbers at the detention centre have declined since mid-2020, about the same time the state {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://statements.qld.gov.au/statements/90032"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enacted laws designed to lock up more children}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As record numbers of young people have been sent to youth prisons, worker numbers at the Cleveland detention centre fell so sharply that by mid-last year about one in five roles at the centre was vacant.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/jun/06/like-guantanamo-the-children-locked-in-solitary-for-weeks-at-a-time-in-queensland-youth-prison"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guardian Australia reported this month}}}\uc2  that the use of solitary confinement due to chronic staffing shortages and overcrowding had created a situation where many children were not attending rehabilitation programs or regular schooling.\par \par \uc2 Guards, youth workers, teachers, judges, lawyers, social workers, the Queensland public guardian and others have raised concerns about the use of confinement, an increase in violent behaviour as a result of prolonged lockdowns, and a lack of programs to support children in custody at the centre.\par \par \uc2 In an apparent response to Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s reporting on Cleveland, the youth justice minister, Di Farmer, and the department\u8217\'20\'19s senior executive director, Michael Drane, held a press conference outside the centre this month to tout the results of a recruitment campaign.\par \par \uc2 The government says the state has hired 51 staff this financial year, and that 21 others are undergoing pre-employment training. A further 23 have been offered jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe number of youth detention workers has increased, as well as case managers who work to address issues that contribute to offending,\u8221\'20\'1D Farmer said.\par \par \uc2 But despite claims that \u8220\'20\'1Cstaffing levels have improved\u8221\'20\'1D, comparative data requested by Guardian Australia shows large numbers of employees left the centre over the same time period. Total employee numbers remain below levels in July 2020.\par \par \uc2 In 2020-21 there were 45 worker \u8220\'20\'1Cseparations\u8221\'20\'1D from Cleveland. In 2021-22 more than a quarter of the workforce \u8211\'20\'13 a total of 76 workers \u8211\'20\'13 resigned. So far this year a further 47 have left.\par \par \uc2 There are 290 approved full-time positions at Cleveland and 10% of those remain vacant, the department said. A year ago, based on the figures supplied, about 19% of those positions would have been unfilled.\par \par \uc2 At the same time as the centre has been hit chronic staff shortages, state government policies resulted in record numbers of children being placed in the detention system. The influx of youth prisoners began in mid-2020 when Queensland introduced a presumption against bail for some children.\par \par \uc2 The police minister, \uc2 Mark Ryan\uc2 , said last year the state had always made it clear that the consequences of those laws would be more young people in custody\par \par \uc2 Staff, youth workers and children in detention all say the situation at Cleveland \u8211\'20\'13 where the state\u8217\'20\'19s management of the detention workforce has failed to cope with that influx \u8211\'20\'13 has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/jun/06/like-guantanamo-the-children-locked-in-solitary-for-weeks-at-a-time-in-queensland-youth-prison"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 turbo-charged recidivism rates in the community}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The state youth justice department said detention centres \u8220\'20\'1Care complex and challenging environments\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLike any organisation, staff leave positions for a variety of reasons, including personal reasons and career opportunities elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA national and state-wide recruitment campaign to fill frontline positions at the Cleveland Youth Detention Centre has boosted staffing levels.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRecent improvements include the hiring of staff wellness officers and more safety advisors to better support staff, and additional support for new recruits including improved training.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230618ej6i001bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
