\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18This is on the company\u8217\'20\'19: \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers vote to strike as negotiations continue\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 816 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2023\par 16:31 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Push in new contract include air conditioning in vehicles, pay increases for part-time workers and end to two-tier wages\par \par \uc2 About 340,000 workers at the shipping giant \uc2 UPS\uc2 , represented by the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , have voted to authorize a strike when their current five-year contract expires on 31 July if a new tentative agreement isn\u8217\'20\'19t reached with the company by then.\par \par \uc2 Voting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://teamster.org/2023/06/ups-strike-votes-start-this-week/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 began}}}\uc2  last week at local union halls around the US. Workers voted 97% in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Teamsters/status/1669733547597344768"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  favor}}}\uc2  approving the strike authorization. Negotiations on the national agreement between \uc2 UPS\uc2  and the \uc2 Teamsters{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supplychaindive.com/news/ups-teamsters-national-contract-negotiations/649245/https://www.supplychaindive.com/news/ups-teamsters-national-contract-negotiations/649245/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 began}}}\uc2  in early May 2023 and remain ongoing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not trying to go on strike. We\u8217\'20\'19re going to lose money. We have families, this is going to affect everybody, even our customers. But this is on the company. The company can make it right and finish the contract, and give us what we\u8217\'20\'19re asking for,\u8221\'20\'1D said Viviana Gonzalez , a \uc2 UPS\uc2  package car driver in Palmdale Village, California, for nine years and a union shop steward.\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not a secret that we all worked through the pandemic. We put our lives at risk. We work crazy hours, and we never saw any sort of pay from being an essential employee. We were just making the world move and they made billions of dollars off of us and we haven\u8217\'20\'19t seen anything to compensate us for putting ourselves at risk. None of us have seen it, especially our part-timers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers at \uc2 UPS\uc2  have been pushing for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://teamster.org/2023/04/ups-teamsters-rally-for-a-strong-contract-with-national-negotiations-set-to-begin/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 numerous demands}}}\uc2  in their new contract, including air conditioning in \uc2 UPS\uc2  vehicles, pay increases for part-time workers to at least $25 an hour, elimination of two-tier wages for package drivers, ending the use of subcontracting, eliminating driver-facing cameras, an end to a forced sixth day of work in a week and more full-time opportunities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe job is physically demanding. The wages they\u8217\'20\'19re starting at now aren\u8217\'20\'19t enough to keep them in the door in the local market. They come in, they get beat up in the morning in a very physical, fast-paced job. They get part-time hours, but what I feel is like a full-time job worth of work in four or five hours,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ted Breen, a pre-load worker at \uc2 UPS\uc2  in Glens Falls, New York, for 14 years.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers have highlighted the company\u8217\'20\'19s immense profits in recent years and through the pandemic, with an emphasis on pushing for those profits to reflect gains in their next contract.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  noted \uc2 UPS\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Teamsters/status/1655260147470458883"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  $56.3bn in profits under the current contract so far, from 2019 to 2023.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe worked very hard through very tough times, when most people couldn\u8217\'20\'19t or wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t go to work, the \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers came to work at the risk of their own health. The company was always about cheerleading, \u8216\'20\'18Oh heroes work here, heroes work here,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Scott Gove, who has worked at \uc2 UPS\uc2  for 35 years. \u8220\'20\'1CThey need to start sharing the profits that were made through the pandemic across the backs of those \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Teamsters/status/1669112696686493698"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  43 non-economic changes to the UPS Teamsters national agreement achieved so far in negotiations, and announced earlier this week that the union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://teamster.org/2023/06/teamsters-secure-air-conditioning-for-ups-fleet-in-major-tentative-deal/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 secured}}}\uc2  air conditioning for the \uc2 UPS\uc2  fleet, which was a major demand for the union as \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers have reported grueling heat exposure on the job. Negotiations on economic proposals are set to begin next week.\par \par \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have taken a more aggressive stance on the new contract, as the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president, Sean O\u8217\'20\'19Brien, was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/11/19/business/economy/teamsters-sean-obrien-hoffa.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 elected }}}\uc2  in 2021 as part of a slate of reform candidates after the previous \uc2 UPS\uc2  contract in 2018 was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://truthout.org/articles/in-a-blow-to-labor-teamsters-ratify-ups-contract-despite-no-vote/?gclid=Cj0KCQjw7aqkBhDPARIsAKGa0oLcpsYwyCduQ8Uz8qXQpS45i0oCdCAxFyAN_1drqQjVmdyQpiotgAcaAriREALw_wcB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ratified}}}\uc2  by union leadership despite membership voting against it.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 UPS\uc2  said in a statement: \u8220\'20\'1CWe continue to make meaningful progress on a variety of topics in our negotiations with the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They also provided a May report by \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  conducted for \uc2 UPS\uc2  on the assessment of a strike risk at \uc2 UPS\uc2 , which noted \u8220\'20\'1Cwe feel comfortable that a strike will not occur\u8221\'20\'1D, claimed the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 financial readiness for a strike is limited, and predicted \uc2 UPS\uc2  shares performance would rebound after the strike threat passes.\par \par \uc2 They added on the strike vote, \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  announced it received approval from its \uc2 UPS\uc2  members who voted on its strike authorization. The results do not mean a strike is imminent and do not impact our current business operations in any way. Authorization votes and approvals are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.ups.com/us/en/newsroom/negotiations/negotiations-basics/the-bargaining-process.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 normal steps in labor union negotiations}}}\uc2 . We continue to make progress on key issues and remain confident that we will reach an agreement that provides wins for our employees, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , our company and our customers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230616ej6g0023a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
