\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I have not seen one cent\u8217\'20\'19: billions stolen in wage theft from US workers\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,181 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2023\par 19:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees across the country are not getting paid what they are owed, and critics say government is toothless to help\par \par \uc2 Jose Martinez worked for a construction contractor in New York City for six months in 2019 when he and his co-workers suddenly stopped getting paid.\par \par \uc2 Martinez said the contractor, Star Builders, initially blamed the owners of the building for not dispersing money for the project.\par \par \uc2 Martinez said he and his colleagues were eventually paid late, but the delays kept happening. The contractor came up with more excuses for the lack of payment. Eventually Martinez and several of his co-workers left after not getting paid for four weeks of work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have not seen one cent from that money that is owed yet,\u8221\'20\'1D added Martinez, who filed a wage theft claim with the New York state labor department in 2019 with assistance from the non-profit \uc2 Make the Road New York\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt affected me a lot because at the time, I had to start finding other work, I had to pay bills and pay rent and I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have money, so I had to get loans that I eventually had to pay back once I got another job.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The contractor, Star Builders, did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Martinez is far from alone.\par \par \uc2 Workers in the US have an estimated $50bn-plus stolen from them every year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/epidemic-wage-theft-costing-workers-hundreds/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according }}}\uc2  to the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , surpassing all robberies, burglaries and motor vehicle thefts combined. The majority of these stolen wages are never recovered by workers.\par \par \uc2 Between 2017 to 2020, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/wage-theft-2021/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $3.24bn}}}\uc2  in stolen wages were recovered by the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2 , state labor departments and attorney generals, and through class- and collective-action litigation.\par \par \uc2 Wage theft disproportionately affects lower-wage workers, women, people of color and immigrant workers, and negatively affects local economies and tax revenues.\par \par \uc2 There are numerous forms of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/wage-theft-2021/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wage theft}}}\uc2 , from employers not compensating workers for time worked, violating minimum wage and overtime laws, misclassifying employees as independent contractors, not providing legally required meal breaks, confiscating worker tips, or illegally taking deductions from worker wages.\par \par \uc2 Wage-theft violators include some of the largest employers in the US; Amazon {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wweek.com/news/business/2022/11/17/amazon-settles-oregon-wage-theft-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid $18m}}}\uc2  in November 2022 to settle a wage-theft class-action lawsuit in Oregon, the largest in the state\u8217\'20\'19s history, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/02/02/amazon-to-pay-61point7-million-in-ftc-driver-tipping-settlement-.html#:~:text=Contractors%20working%20for%20the%20Amazon,to%20customers%20in%20San%20Francisco.&text=Amazon%20will%20pay%20%2461.7%20million%20to%20settle%20allegations%20by%20the,of%20tips%20received%20from%20customers."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid}}}\uc2  a $61.7m fine in 2021 over allegations of stealing tips from Amazon Flex drivers.\par \par \uc2 According to a 2018 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.goodjobsfirst.org/wp-content/uploads/docs/pdfs/wagetheft_report_revised.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report }}}\uc2  by Good Jobs, between January 2000 to 2018, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  paid over $1.4bn in fines and settlements over wage theft violations, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  paid over $500m during the same period, and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  paid over $380m.\par \par \uc2 Construction contractors have a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.constructiondive.com/news/gcs-often-excluded-from-wage-theft-liability-law-may-change/640418/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 notorious reputation}}}\uc2  for wage-theft violations, often affecting immigrant workers, and exploiting loopholes to avoid paying wage-theft claims, such as shutting down businesses and reopening under a different business filing.\par \par \uc2 Martinez wants to warn others in the sector.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople need to be very careful with contractors, because they always say, \u8216\'20\'18We have a lot of work for you to do\u8217\'20\'19 to keep you there. They would say, \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s just a matter of time, we just have to wait for the payment, it\u8217\'20\'19s going to be OK,\u8217\'20\'19 and so they\u8217\'20\'19re always talking that way to keep people working there,\u8221\'20\'1D said Martinez.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t let more than one week go by without getting their payment because they\u8217\'20\'19re always going to come up with some excuse.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite the state of New York\u8217\'20\'19s worker protections and laws aimed at curtailing wage theft, the Center for Popular Democracy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://maketheroadny.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/Coming-Up-Short_-The-State-of-Wage-Theft-Enforcement-in-NY-4_8_19.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated}}}\uc2  in 2019 that wage theft may affect 2.1 million workers in New York every year, exceeding $3bn annually.\par \par \uc2 Goma Yonjan Gurung has worked as a nail technician in the New York City area for 25 years, an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/15/new-york-nail-salon-industry-council-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 industry}}}\uc2  where wage theft is rampant and scantily enforced. A February 2020 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://workersunitednynj.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/Race-to-the-Bottom-FINAL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the New York Nail Salon Workers Association noted 82% of workers reported experiencing wage theft at an average amount of $181 per week.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven though the government says the minimum wage is $15 an hour, what I know is it\u8217\'20\'19s not happening for many nail technicians, including myself. In some places they pay as low as $10 an hour,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe government in the past has passed bills mandating minimum wage or time off, but what happens is it is not implemented or being practiced by employers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Worker advocates have criticized the state\u8217\'20\'19s labor department for lack of enforcement of wage-theft violations and not recovering stolen wages from employers.\par \par \uc2 Other states, such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.orlandoweekly.com/news/floridas-lack-of-a-labor-department-leaves-workers-with-limited-options-34229517?fbclid=IwAR2w8gfp_4ULwa4y43kna6_qqrp4w-NSBTIadmFVztw99ZeMVcxR-tV00IE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida}}}\uc2 , do not have a state labor department to oversee the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://businessfacilities.com/florida-the-sunshine-state-is-bright-for-business/#:~:text=With%20the%20third%20largest%20workforce,which%20business%20can%20draw%20from.&text=Florida%20has%20been%20ranked%20as,attractive%20place%20to%20do%20business."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 third largest workforce }}}\uc2  in the US, leaving workers with fewer options to try to recover stolen wages.\par \par \uc2 A bartender in Orlando, Florida who requested to remain anonymous because they still work in the industry, said they started a new bartending job in May and pushed back when they found out their hourly wage was just a daily rate of $30, despite working eight-hour shifts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI reminded my manager that paying less than minimum wage was illegal, and added a link to a law firm\u8217\'20\'19s page about it. He fired me,\u8221\'20\'1D the worker said. \u8220\'20\'1CGetting fired for not wanting to be paid below the already insanely low minimum wage after working in the industry for over 20 years was pretty rough.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The worker attempted to file a complaint with the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  and the Florida attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office but was told they only prioritize higher wage violations and never heard back after being told someone would follow up.\par \par \uc2 Rodrigo Camarena, director of the Justicia Lab, an advocacy group, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe process for filing a wage-theft complaint form is very onerous and cumbersome, so there\u8217\'20\'19s an immediate obstacle in the process itself. But once that form is filed, it takes months if not years for the department of labor to even investigate it.\u8221\'20\'1D The Justicia Lab recently launched a digital tool, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nextcity.org/features/a-new-digital-legal-tool-helps-immigrant-workers-reclaim-their-stolen-wages"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u161\'00\'A1Reclamo!}}}\uc2 , for workers to file wage-theft claims.\par \par \uc2 Camarena said: \u8220\'20\'1CIn the meantime, that person is going without that income that they earned potentially, and the employer isn\u8217\'20\'19t being penalized in any way, so there\u8217\'20\'19s a delay in enforcement and then the enforcement process also isn\u8217\'20\'19t as thorough as it could be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 New York legislators and worker groups have been pushing to pass the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nclej.org/news/nys-failed-to-pass-sweat-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Securing Wages Earned Against Theft (Sweat) Act}}}\uc2  to enforce New York\u8217\'20\'19s wage-theft laws and make it more difficult for employers to avoid accountability for wage-theft violations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere needs to be more work at the state level, that not just holds individual employers accountable but to really hold industries accountable that are problematic and root out the ways employers get out of paying wages owed,\u8221\'20\'1D added Camarena.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to ensure that worker rights are protected in all cases, because ultimately this issue is about the dignity of everyone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230615ej6f00137\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
