\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We deserve more\u8217\'20\'19: hotel workers vote to authorize strike in California\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 849 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2023\par 17:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Decision affects 15,000 hotel workers who are pushing for raises, affordable healthcare and safe workloads and staffing levels\par \par \uc2 Hotel workers are threatening to strike in California over pay and conditions in what would be the largest ever strike by hotel workers.\par \par \uc2 In a strike authorization vote held on 8 June, workers voted 96% in favor of authorizing a strike, which could begin as early as Fourth of July weekend.\par \par \uc2 The decision impacts 15,000 hotel workers represented by \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11 in the Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 The Los Angeles area is preparing to host two of the largest sporting events in the world back to back, the Fifa World Cup in 2026 and the summer Olympics in 2028 and workers are demanding improvements in their contract ahead of what is expected to be a highly profitable and extremely busy period for the industry.\par \par \uc2 The workers at major US hotel employers, including \uc2 Hyatt\uc2 , \uc2 Hilton\uc2 , \uc2 Highgate\uc2 , Accor, \uc2 IHG\uc2  and \uc2 Marriott\uc2 , are pushing for a $10-an-hour wage increase, affordable family healthcare, retirement pensions and safe workloads and staffing levels as hotels throughout the industry have used the Covid-19 pandemic to cut staffing levels and increase workloads, such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jul/05/overworked-underpaid-hotel-workers-employees"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminating}}}\uc2  daily guest room cleaning.\par \par \uc2 The union noted the hotel industry received {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://barragan.house.gov/2021/07/27/barragan-colleagues-urge-sba-to-improve-transparency-of-ppp-loan-forgiveness-for-the-hotel-industry/#:~:text=U.S.%20hotels%20have%20received%20%2413.9,chains%20eligible%20for%20taxpayer%20aid."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 billions}}}\uc2  of dollars in Covid relief funds through the payroll protection program but still cut jobs. Hotel occupancy in the LA area {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/business/story/2021-12-16/hotel-occupancy-in-l-a-bounces-back-to-pre-covid-levels-faster-than-in-other-cities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recovered}}}\uc2  to pre-pandemic levels by late 2021 and tourism to the area has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ocregister.com/2023/05/08/california-travel-industry-edges-closer-to-a-full-recovery/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly recovered}}}\uc2  to pre-pandemic levels of 2019, which was a record-setting year of revenue for the industry.\par \par \uc2 Workers are pushing for gains that reflect what they have been through during the pandemic, the current issues they are facing with staffing cuts, high inflation and soaring rent costs.\par \par \uc2 Lupe Pitones, who has worked at the front desk of the Westin Bonaventure hotel in Los Angeles for 17 years, is still recovering from the challenges and issues she faced during the Covid-19 pandemic, which included a long furlough from her job and issues with retaining and finding health insurance while awaiting and receiving a liver transplant.\par \par \uc2 She was furloughed shortly after the pandemic started in the US in March 2020 until she started getting called into work sporadically in early 2021. In January 2022, her doctor notified her she would require a liver transplant, but because she had been furloughed and was not getting enough hours at work to maintain eligibility for health insurance through her employer, she also had to worry about finding another health insurance while awaiting a transplant.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was overwhelming. So many things go through your mind, if I\u8217\'20\'19m going to die, what if I don\u8217\'20\'19t make it, my family, insurance,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pitones.\par \par \uc2 While on the transplant list, Pitones\u8217\'20\'19 donor list scores weren\u8217\'20\'19t high enough to receive a transplant in California and in October 2022 her doctors said she would have to relocate to Arizona to receive a transplant as soon as possible or she risked dying. But by then she didn\u8217\'20\'19t have insurance and was waiting on her application for Medicaid in California.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy family got together and agreed to make the payments for insurance and we relocated to Arizona,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have insurance, I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be here. I was dying and having to worry about insurance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to fight for health insurance because it is crucial. Sometimes your life depends on having insurance for your loved ones and yourself and having it gives peace of mind,\u8221\'20\'1D added Pitones. \u8220\'20\'1CFor this contract, we want to emphasize that we deserve more because prices are higher and we\u8217\'20\'19re not getting enough hours. We\u8217\'20\'19re hard workers and when it\u8217\'20\'19s time to fight, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to fight.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Other hotel workers in the area have criticized the high cost of rent in the Los Angeles area that has pushed out workers to have to live far away from their hotels of employment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am forced to spend half my wages on transportation and risk my life on dangerous roads. I am voting yes to strike because my co-workers and I deserve a wage that allows us to live near where we work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brenda Mendoza, a uniform attendant at the JW Marriot LA Live in a press release, who commutes two hours each way from her home in \uc2 Apple\uc2  Valley, California, to work in downtown Los Angeles every day.\par \par \uc2 Kurt Petersen, co-president of \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11, said the union provided hotels with a proposal in April 2023, but only heard back from the hotels with a counter proposal recently, which he said didn\u8217\'20\'19t include any economic responses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe workers are absolutely furious,\u8221\'20\'1D said Petersen. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the contracts expire, we are prepared to call an industry-wide strike, which if we do it will be the largest strike as far as we can tell in the hotel industry in probably US history but definitely in the last 50 years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230609ej69001s8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
