\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 New York\u8217\'20\'19s essential workers bear the brunt of poor air quality from wildfires\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 543 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2023\par 18:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Delivery app workers \u8211\'20\'13 excluded from most worker protections \u8211\'20\'13 slog through dangerous conditions to keep paying their bills\par \par \uc2 Thousands of essential workers have had no choice but to work through the hazardous air from Canada\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/video/2023/jun/08/wildfires-rage-across-canada-leaving-cars-scorched-video"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raging wildfires}}}\uc2  that have triggered air quality alerts across the north-east US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe cannot afford not to go out to work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Antonio Solis, a delivery app worker and organizer with Los Deliveristas Unidos in New York City. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause of this necessity, we\u8217\'20\'19re forced to go out to work and take the risks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 New York City {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.schools.nyc.gov/alerts/air-quality-health-advisory-for-new-york-city-until-11-59-pm-on-wednesday-6-7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had}}}\uc2  the worst air quality in the world on Wednesday. Schools and public events were canceled throughout the city, as city officials encouraged residents to remain indoors, limit outdoor activity and wear masks when outside.\par \par \uc2 But Solis said he and his colleagues could not afford to take a day off. \u8220\'20\'1CThe problem is that because we don\u8217\'20\'19t have a minimum wage, and we still have to rely on tips,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 ??Many {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Deliveristas?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Deliveristas}}}\uc2  cannot afford to stay at home and are on duty.\par \par \uc2 Many are directly experiencing the effects of this air pollution, including shortness of breath. This situation serves as another example of why it\u8217\'20\'19s crucial for NYC to immediately implement the minimum pay law. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/6IoeaySX53"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/6IoeaySX53}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 Workers Justice ??? (@workersjusticep) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/workersjusticep/status/1666574859084746755?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 7, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Ligia Guallpa, executive director of the Workers Justice Project, noted that app delivery workers, because they are classified as independent contractors, are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://comptroller.nyc.gov/newsroom/elected-officials-delivery-workers-labor-leaders-rallied-against-diminished-and-delayed-minimum-pay-rule-for-deliveristas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  excluded}}}\uc2  from minimum wage and worker protections and paid sick time leave. Workers have been pushing for local legislation to mandate these protections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis situation is a great example of why New York City needs to implement the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://comptroller.nyc.gov/newsroom/elected-officials-delivery-workers-labor-leaders-rallied-against-diminished-and-delayed-minimum-pay-rule-for-deliveristas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 minimum pay law}}}\uc2  that has been delayed because of corporations\u8217\'20\'19 advocacy against having to pay a minimum wage for 65,000 deliveristas,\u8221\'20\'1D said Guallpa.\par \par \uc2 The dangerous pollution levels have reminded some of the Covid pandemic, which struck New York and its essential workers particularly hard. After the pandemic, there were renewed calls for more protections for essential workers.\par \par \uc2 In March 2023, New York City\u8217\'20\'19s mayor, Eric Adams, rolled back a $23.82 an hour minimum wage for delivery workers that was set to be enacted by 2025 to $19.96 per hour. The city has also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gothamist.com/news/delays-infighting-lobbying-the-battle-over-nyc-food-delivery-workers-pay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 delayed}}}\uc2  rolling out minimum pay scales that were scheduled to go into effect six months ago as delivery app companies, worker groups and elected officials battle over how and how much workers will be paid.\par \par \uc2 Because of the lack of protections, Los Deliveristas Unidos have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/workersjusticep/status/1666607936926277632"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 directly distributing}}}\uc2  N95 masks to workers, as the delivery app companies have not been ensuring workers are provided personal protective equipment to work through the wildfire smoke conditions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to not go to work, we\u8217\'20\'19re recommending they\u8217\'20\'19re wearing respirators, specifically N95 masks,\u8221\'20\'1D added Guallpa. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they\u8217\'20\'19re at a worksite, their employer should be providing N95 respirators, making sure there is ventilation, air purifiers if they\u8217\'20\'19re working indoors, and if they get sick, they should get paid sick time and they shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be retaliated against for speaking up against unsafe working conditions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230608ej68002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
