\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The best state for workers\u8217\'20\'19: what are Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s new labor laws?\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,306 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2023\par 10:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 New legislation will mandate paid leave, prohibit non-compete clauses and bar captive anti-union meetings, among other measures\par \par \uc2 Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic governor and legislature has enacted one of the most pro-worker packages of legislation that any US state has passed in decades which includes paid family and medical leave, prohibits non-compete clauses, bars employers from holding anti-union captive audience meetings, and strengthens protections for meatpacking workers and Amazon warehouse employees.\par \par \uc2 Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s new legislation mandates paid sick days, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://educationminnesota.org/news/press-release/walz-signs-historic-labor-and-education-bills-into-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allows teachers\u8217\'20\'19 unions to bargain over educator-to-student ratios}}}\uc2  and creates a statewide council to improve conditions for nursing home workers.\par \par \uc2 These moves come as labor rights in the US \u8211\'20\'13 which are far more limited than in other industrial nations \u8211\'20\'13 have come under sustained attack from business groups and many conservative politicians.\par \par \uc2 Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s commissioner of labor and industry, Nicole Blissenbach, said the new laws \u8220\'20\'1Ctruly make Minnesota the best state for workers and their families\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The legislation\u8217\'20\'19s backers say these pro-worker measures were made possible by the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 winning control of Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s state senate in last November\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections, giving that party trifecta control of the house, senate and governor\u8217\'20\'19s office. The Democrats now have a 34-to-33 majority in the state senate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis legislation represents work that has been a long time coming,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jennifer McEwen, a Democrat who is chair of the senate labor committee. \u8220\'20\'1CThese are things that most workers in our peer nations take for granted and enjoy.\u8221\'20\'1D The US is the only wealthy industrial nation and one of a handful of nations worldwide that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t guarantee all workers paid parental leave.\par \par \uc2 Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , a Democrat, signed the Paid Family and Medical Leave Act as well as an omnibus labor bill that contains a raft of pro-worker provisions. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mn.gov/governor/newsroom/press-releases/#/detail/appId/1/id/579303"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In signing the paid leave legislation, Walz said}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re ensuring Minnesotans no longer have to make the choice between a paycheck and taking time off to care for a new baby or a sick family member.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The law allows Minnesota workers to take up to 12 weeks a year with partial pay to care for a newborn or sick family member and also allows workers 12 weeks to recover from a serious illness or health problem. The law places a cap of 20 weeks a year for employees who use both provisions.\par \par \uc2 Alice Mann, a medical doctor and the bill\u8217\'20\'19s main senate sponsor, said: \u8220\'20\'1CPaid family and medical leave leads to so many great things: better maternal health, better child health, decreased hospitalization for children, increased rates of breastfeeding, increased employee retention, greater participation in the workforce for women and decreased gender disparities in the workplace.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The bill passed Minnesota\u8217\'20\'19s senate 34 to 33, with business groups opposing it and every Republican voting against.\par \par \uc2 This is a long time coming and it\u8217\'20\'19s definitely long overdue\par \par \uc2 Representative Ruth Richardson\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a long time coming and it\u8217\'20\'19s definitely long overdue,\u8221\'20\'1D said Representative Ruth Richardson, the bill\u8217\'20\'19s main sponsor in the house. \u8220\'20\'1CThe vast majority of Minnesotans don\u8217\'20\'19t have access to paid family and medical leave. We see who is left out \u8211\'20\'13 it is disproportionately women and communities of color and low-income workers who often can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to take leave.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some supporters say that several of the newly enacted provisions should be embraced and enacted in other trifecta blue states, such as Michigan.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPaid family and medical leave is extremely popular, not only in Minnesota, but nationally as well,\u8221\'20\'1D Richardson said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s one of those issues that are incredibly bipartisan. I believe that passing paid family and medical leave is possible in blue and red trifecta states and in everything in between.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The omnibus labor bill that Walz signed was full of pro-worker provisions ranging from paid sick leave to warehouse safety to setting up a new standards board for nursing home workers.\par \par \uc2 Under the new legislation, non-compete provisions are void and unenforceable. Walz\u8217\'20\'19s office said the law, by prohibiting a practice that bars workers from taking jobs with competing employers, gives workers \u8220\'20\'1Cthe freedom to seek better working conditions and higher wages without restrictions\u8221\'20\'1D. The ban doesn\u8217\'20\'19t invalidate non-compete clauses entered into before 1 July 2023.\par \par \uc2 To help workers take care of themselves or a family member, the new legislation also allows employees to earn one hour of sick and safe time for every 30 hours worked. The law allows accrual of up to 48 hours of sick time each year.\par \par \uc2 The law calls for creating a nursing home workforce standards board with an equal number of worker and employer representatives that will set statewide minimum standards for nursing-home employees, while seeking to improve working conditions and care.\par \par \uc2 Those of us who are more progressive say this is exactly how we win the next election. This is what we do. We do the things we promised\par \par \uc2 State senator Jennifer McEwen\par \par \uc2 It also requires Amazon and other warehouses to tell employees about every work quota they are required to meet, how their work speed is measured and what happens if they fail to meet their quota. The law also allows employees to see work speed data and prohibits employers from retaliating against workers who seek such data.\par \par \uc2 Under the new legislation, Minnesota employers also cannot require workers to attend anti-union propaganda sessions, often called \u8220\'20\'1Ccaptive audience\u8221\'20\'1D meetings. The law makes it illegal for an employer to retaliate against a worker for refusing to attend a political or religious meeting, and under the law an anti-union meeting is considered a political meeting.\par \par \uc2 The state labor department will appoint a meatpacking industry worker-rights coordinator to submit an annual report recommending ways to improve conditions for meat and poultry processing workers. The law also calls for enhanced workplace safety standards for meatpacking plants with 100 or more workers.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the new legislation tackles wage theft by ensuring a contractor entering into a construction contract assumes liability for any unpaid wages, fringe benefits or damages that a subcontractor owes to its workers.\par \par \uc2 Bernie Burnham, president of the Minnesota \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , heaped praise on the new laws, calling them \u8220\'20\'1Csome of the most sweeping pro-labor legislation in state history\u8221\'20\'1D and saying they \u8220\'20\'1Cwill improve the lives of workers in every corner of our state\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Burnham added: \u8220\'20\'1CMinnesota has a strong labor movement, and we worked hard to advance our legislative priorities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Corporate lobbyists complained that the paid leave law would impose too great a burden on business \u8211\'20\'13 employers will have to pay 0.35% of their payroll to finance paid leave, while employees will have to contribute 0.35% of their paychecks.\par \par \uc2 John Reynolds, state director of the \uc2 National Federation of Independent Business\uc2 , said the paid leave \u8220\'20\'1Cmandate\u8221\'20\'1D is \u8220\'20\'1Cdeeply flawed\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cwill cost much more than expected and make it harder for small businesses to keep their doors open\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Mann said the business community was crying wolf. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re saying businesses are all going to go out of business and close their doors,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat hasn\u8217\'20\'19t happened in the 11 other states that have passed paid family and medical leave.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mann added: \u8220\'20\'1CI am someone who believes that when we lift all boats and make a better standard of living for people, I don\u8217\'20\'19t think corporations actually lose. I think we all gain.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McEwen said: \u8220\'20\'1CThose of us who are more progressive say this is exactly how we win the next election. This is what we do. We do the things we promised we would do, and we hope people will say, \u8216\'20\'18Please, more of that.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This story has been co-published with\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tcf.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Century Foundation}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230607ej67001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
