\par\par \uc2 News\par \b \uc2 What US job will shrink the most in the next decade?\b0\par\par\uc2 Mona Chalabi \par \uc2 452 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2023\par 12:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cashiers are particularly vulnerable to automation, while things look better for healthcare workers\par \par \uc2 When a reader recently asked me to look at how many teachers were leaving the occupation, I assumed the numbers would be high. Between wage stagnation and the near impossible working conditions during the height of Covid, I guessed that the outlook for US teaching jobs would be bleak.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/emp/tables/occupational-separations-and-openings.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data}}}\uc2  tells a different story. Yes, a high number of people are exiting the occupation each year (148,000 on average) but the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  still expects that a huge number of people will enter the field, too. Over the period from 2021 to 2031, the bureau projects that there will be an additional 230,000 job openings in the US for teachers (including \u8220\'20\'1Cpreschool, elementary, middle, secondary, and special education teachers\u8221\'20\'1D). This is partly because of the number of teachers retiring but it\u8217\'20\'19s also because teaching, unlike other US jobs, can\u8217\'20\'19t be replaced with automation any time soon.\par \par \uc2 I found myself returning to the initial question, though, especially given concerns about artificial intelligence. I went back into the Excel spreadsheet wondering which jobs are expected to shrink the most. Cashiers are at the top of the list. By 2031, it\u8217\'20\'19s expected that 335,000 fewer jobs will be available to people working as cashiers. That\u8217\'20\'19s because those roles are especially vulnerable to erasure. According to a 2017 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://weinberg.udel.edu/retail-automation-stranded-workers-opportunities-and-risks-for-labor-automation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 University of Delaware\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Ccashiers are considered one of the most easily automatable jobs in the economy\u8221\'20\'1D. But it\u8217\'20\'19s also because a huge number of people in the US work in cashier roles \u8211\'20\'13 over three million of us work a cash register. Turnover is also super high. Each year, about 33,000 of these jobs will disappear but so many people enter and leave this profession, or change jobs within it, that it can be hard to spot the changes taking place in any given year.\par \par \uc2 Other jobs that will disappear quickly include \u8220\'20\'1Cassemblers and fabricators\u8221\'20\'1D (115,000 fewer jobs), telemarketers (21,000 fewer), farmers and ranchers (down 24,000) and postal service workers (down by 29,000).\par \par \uc2 The jobs with the highest growth numbers are, perhaps unsurprisingly, in healthcare support. As with teaching, we\u8217\'20\'19re a long way away from a robot being able to perform these skills. It takes human attributes to reliably dress a wound or help someone down the stairs. And as the US population ages, there will be high demand for that kind of help. It\u8217\'20\'19s expected that an extra 1.2 million people will work in these jobs by 2031.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230531ej5v00137\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
