\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 The food court rising above San Francisco\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18doom loop\u8217\'20\'19: \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re breaking down stereotypes\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Erin McCormick in San Francisco \par \uc2 2,349 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2023\par 20:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 While stores like \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  and \uc2 Nordstrom\uc2  are closing, La Cocina\u8217\'20\'19s entrepreneurs are building community in a neighborhood facing homelessness and addiction\par \par \uc2 From her coffee counter in La Cocina Municipal Marketplace, Santana Tapia has a clear line of sight into every gloomy prediction that has hit San Francisco since the rise of the Covid pandemic.\par \par \uc2 From the marketplace\u8217\'20\'19s windows in the heart of the city\u8217\'20\'19s troubled Tenderloin District, she notices the decline of foot traffic that has come from employees choosing to work at home rather than travel to their downtown offices, spawning some dire predictions of an economic \u8220\'20\'1Cdoom loop\u8221\'20\'1D. And she can see the ravages of the fentanyl epidemic happening all around her, with its record numbers of overdose deaths and scenes of homeless desperation.\par \par \uc2 Yet Tapia \u8211\'20\'13 and other small business operators in La Cocina Marketplace \u8211\'20\'13 are mounting a community-minded effort to battle the dire predictions, one cup of coffee and one lunch plate at a time.\par \par \uc2 Tapia\u8217\'20\'19s coffee house, the Fluid Coop Cafe, which she started last year with two other trans activists, serves up drinks with names like \u8220\'20\'1CExpresso Yo Self\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CLove You A-Latte\u8221\'20\'1D and hosts open mic nights celebrating the Tenderloin community.\par \par \uc2 The independent coffee business sits alongside seven restaurant counters that are part of La Cocina\u8217\'20\'19s non-profit incubator for small, women-owned food businesses, featuring dishes like Nepalese dumplings, pupusas from El Salvador and fluffy North African couscous.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  abruptly closed the store it had opened just a year ago in a fancy new apartment complex up the street, citing concerns for employee safety, La Cocina\u8217\'20\'19s entrepreneurs are taking the opposite tack.\par \par \uc2 With the brightly muraled food space serving up a $5 community meal and offering a free lending library and computers for public use, the food sellers are pushing against disinvestment and calling on city supporters to lean into the sense of diverse community that has long made San Francisco what it is.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe reason we have been gaining momentum is we are really focusing on the community and engaging the people who may look like they don\u8217\'20\'19t have a lot of money and those who are unhoused,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tapia. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are breaking down the stereotypes that are keeping people from coming into businesses.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Still, La Cocina marketplace is battling the same overlapping set of post-pandemic challenges hitting many of the businesses in San Francisco\u8217\'20\'19s central commercial corridor, which centers on a long strip of the city\u8217\'20\'19s Market Street.\par \par \uc2 In the area around Tenderloin and the South of Market District, scattered tech office buildings including those of \uc2 Nextdoor\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  are sitting mostly empty. The problems of homelessness and drug use that have always haunted the district have been grossly magnified by the pandemic and the rise of the deadly and hyper-addictive drug fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 In the neighboring Union Square shopping district, top department stores have pulled out one after another. Just last week, \uc2 Nordstrom\uc2 , which long anchored the downtown\u8217\'20\'19s glitzy Westfield Center shopping mall, announced it would close.\par \par \uc2 And in the downtown financial district, with its towering skyscrapers, high vacancy rates in the office buildings have infected the small businesses at street level, leaving vacant storefronts everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Central to many of these problems is the now unreliable attendance of the 130,000 or so commuters, who used to swell the city\u8217\'20\'19s population during the day as they flocked to their office jobs. \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s parent company, alone is clearing out of 435,000 sq ft of office space in one downtown high-rise. This, in turn, is killing the small businesses that served those office workers lunches, coffees and various other sundries.\par \par \uc2 The phenomenon of offices clearing out and affecting other businesses was first described as a potential \u8220\'20\'1Cdoom loop\u8221\'20\'1D by the \uc2 New York University\uc2  professor Arpit Gupta and his co-authors in a paper titled {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nber.org/papers/w30526"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Work From Home and the Office Real Estate Apocalypse}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Studies have shown that San Francisco\u8217\'20\'19s downtown, with its reliance on tech, is taking one of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s heaviest tolls. Office worker visits in the downtown district are down 46% in 2023 from 2019 levels, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://downtownsf.org/doing-business/pedestrian-activity-dashboard"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  a dashboard from a local business promotion group.}}}\uc2  Meanwhile the phrase \u8220\'20\'1Cdoom loop\u8221\'20\'1D has become a mantra in news articles and discussions about the city.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are still customers, just not enough customers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Leticia Landa, La Cocina\u8217\'20\'19s deputy director. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not just La Cocina; it\u8217\'20\'19s many, many businesses. These used to be areas with thousands and thousands and thousands of office workers. And now that is no longer the case.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 City leaders have acknowledged the change: \u8220\'20\'1CSan Francisco downtown, as we knew it, is not coming back,\u8221\'20\'1D Mayor \uc2 London Breed\uc2  said in her state of the city address on 9 February.\par \par \uc2 But, she argued: \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s OK. Empty office buildings have fueled dire predictions about economic doom and screaming headlines about the death of downtown. Let\u8217\'20\'19s keep some perspective. In 1907 [after the great 1906 earthquake], downtown was mostly rubble and ash. That\u8217\'20\'19s considerably worse than today\u8217\'20\'19s shift in how people work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Still, the pain the pandemic wrought is real, especially in the Tenderloin, where La Cocina is located.\par \par \uc2 At their restaurant, Kayma, inside the marketplace, the chef Wafa Bahloul and her husband, Mounir, serve up a taste of their Algerian heritage with a couscous chicken bowl and a hearty vegan stew along with eggplant marinated in olive oil and Algerian stuffed pastries.\par \par \uc2 But Bahloul, who immigrated to the US seven years ago, admitted that, when she had to go outside late at night or arrive at the building early in the morning, she was afraid of what she saw.\par \par \uc2 In the hours before La Cocina\u8217\'20\'19s two-man security team arrives and conducts a daily cleaning, the sidewalks in front of the marketplace are often packed with people sleeping on cardboard and littered with discarded drug paraphernalia. Bahloul said there had been at least one drug overdose in La Cocina\u8217\'20\'19s bathroom and her husband had once witnessed a shooting across the street.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHopefully the city can make it more safe,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to make it as a business. What\u8217\'20\'19s going on outside affects everybody.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The gritty, working-class neighborhood where La Cocina sits just three blocks from the now closed \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  at 8th and Market Streets has long been a pressure point in the economic forces at play in the city\u8217\'20\'19s development.\par \par \uc2 This edge of the Tenderloin has historically been home to a mix of newly arrived immigrants and working-class families with young children, scattered with vibrant ethnic restaurants and office buildings next to services for unhoused people and sex shops. It has long struggled with prostitution and drug use on the streets.\par \par \uc2 In the years before the pandemic, this area had become the latest flashpoint in the war over gentrification that has raged in San Francisco during the tech boom, with rising tension over the construction of giant luxury apartment developments steps away from the century-old single-room-occupancy hotels that housed some of the city\u8217\'20\'19s poorest residents.\par \par \uc2 Parts of the area got re-branded as Mid-Market and publicized as a new destination for tech workers by tony real estate firms.\par \par \uc2 The building that housed the \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  is part of \u8220\'20\'1CTrinity Place\u8221\'20\'1D, a vast new housing complex with gleaming metallic sculptures, roof decks and ultra-modern lobbies that sits just steps from where unhoused city residents line up for public restrooms and drug users can be seen nodding out on curbsides.\par \par \uc2 The Market Street Whole Foods, owned by \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , opened as a vast, basement-level flagship for the organic foods chain in March of 2022. But it was shuttered this April, with the company posting a small white sign directing customers to its eight outlets in other San Francisco neighborhoods, just a few meters from where the store\u8217\'20\'19s giant \u8220\'20\'1CNow Open\u8221\'20\'1D banners were still hanging.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we feel we can ensure the safety of our team members in the store, we will evaluate a reopening of our Trinity location,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sfstandard.com/business/downtown-san-francisco-whole-foods-market-closing/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in a brief statement}}}\uc2 . While the company didn\u8217\'20\'19t elaborate on its safety concerns, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sfchronicle.com/bayarea/article/sf-whole-foods-closure-overdose-17893301.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 San Francisco Chronicle reported}}}\uc2  a person had died of a fentanyl overdose in the store\u8217\'20\'19s bathroom and the store had reckoned with people urinating on the floor and arguing with security guards. Later reports suggested that the store had also been struggling financially.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I had to guess, I\u8217\'20\'19d say that no one from [\uc2 Whole Foods\uc2 ] global or regional took the time to walk around the neighborhood\u8221\'20\'1D before opening the store, one former employee {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sfist.com/2023/04/25/former-civic-center-whole-foods-employees-say-store-didnt-close-just-because-of-crime/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Chronicle.}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1CSales weren\u8217\'20\'19t too great for the supposed flagship store of the region.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The closure became the latest talking point in a debate over how homelessness and open-air drug use should be handled in the city. The mayor and others say crime has gone out of control and have called for more policing and arrests of drug dealers. More progressive city voices have pushed for a more empathetic approach: giving unhoused people more housing and treatment and offering safe spaces for drug users to use under supervised conditions.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/27/overdose-deaths-in-san-francisco-hit-200-in-three-months-a-crying-shame"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The fentanyl emergency has intensified}}}\uc2  in San Francisco this year, with a person dying of an overdose every 10 hours during the first quarter \u8211\'20\'13 a 41% increase over last year. More than 7,750 people are homeless in the city on any given night, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hsh.sfgov.org/about/research-and-reports/pit-hic/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2022 homeless count}}}\uc2 , a single-day estimate that is considered an undercount.\par \par \uc2 Yet Magnus Lofstrom, an expert in criminal justice with the \uc2 Public Policy Institute of California\uc2 , said that the current rhetoric on crime wasn\u8217\'20\'19t borne out by statistics. The most recent statewide statistics, which are for 2021, show that violent crime was down for San Francisco compared with just before the pandemic. And while retail theft was up somewhat, property crime as a whole was down compared with 2019 as well.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe perception of crime might have an impact on the foot traffic businesses are seeing,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m stressing the perception, because the perception is opposed to what we actually see in the violent crime data.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Matt Long, a tech worker who came down from his apartment in the Trinity Place complex to pick up some chicken for dinner one chilly evening last month, was shocked to find \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  closed.\par \par \uc2 He said he didn\u8217\'20\'19t support more drug arrests, but something needed to be done to solve the fentanyl crisis unfolding at all hours of the day and night around his neighborhood. He\u8217\'20\'19d like to see more housing for those experiencing homelessness and more safe spaces off the streets for drug users.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhatever the city has been doing isn\u8217\'20\'19t working,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need a more holistic solution.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Landa, of La Cocina, agrees that the Tenderloin\u8217\'20\'19s problems of homelessness and drug use have become a \u8220\'20\'1Cscapegoat\u8221\'20\'1D, sometimes taking the blame for the overall economic problems of the city.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo much of it is just about the perception more than the reality,\u8221\'20\'1D said Landa. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s such a vibrant, beautiful neighborhood. Yes, there is also drug dealing and there is also homelessness. And those are frustrating things. But instead of just focusing on those pieces, wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t it be so much better for everybody if we focused on the wonderful things and celebrated them and supported them?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Some are calling for an approach to rebuilding San Francisco\u8217\'20\'19s commercial future that is more like La Cocina\u8217\'20\'19s, emphasizing the diverse communities that were there long before the tech boom started transforming San Francisco a quarter-century ago.\par \par \uc2 From downtown business boosters to leftwing supervisors, there\u8217\'20\'19s a growing chorus of voices arguing that the city\u8217\'20\'19s business districts need to move away from the intense focus on tech development and lean into including the city\u8217\'20\'19s diverse communities again.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t believe in the doom loop,\u8221\'20\'1D said Robbie Silver, the director of Downtown SF Partnership, which concentrates on improving a 43-block area around the Financial District. \u8220\'20\'1CBut for downtown San Francisco to really thrive as an inclusive neighborhood again, it needs to have a more diverse portfolio.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not giving up on office space, but we need to mix it up with residential, hospitality, with arts and culture, education, and with life sciences. That will make it much more resilient against, God forbid, any future pandemic or economic crash.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The group has been organizing community-spirited events to draw people from other parts of the Bay Area in, including a planned \u8220\'20\'1CDrag Me Downtown\u8221\'20\'1D event, during San Francisco\u8217\'20\'19s Pride celebration in June that will feature pop-up performances by drag queens around various downtown bars and restaurants.\par \par \uc2 The city supervisor Dean Preston said business was booming in San Francisco\u8217\'20\'19s many quirky, residential neighborhoods that spread out in other parts of the city, such as Haight-Ashbury, which still draws visitors based on its history as the counterculture capital of the 60s Summer of Love; the Castro, long a beacon of gay communities; and Japantown, with its five-tiered pagoda and shabu-shabu restaurants.\par \par \uc2 He thinks that instead of relying on dreams of a big money tech transformation, the city\u8217\'20\'19s commercial core needs to cultivate some of the same types of community spirit that make other SF neighborhoods, like Noe Valley, North Beach and the Haight, thrive.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe model of this kind of booming tech downtown driving the economy is just not the future,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in La Cocina\u8217\'20\'19s kitchen, Bahloul is hoping that, as spring weather sets in, more people will come out and try some of her family\u8217\'20\'19s Algerian hospitality.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m asking for support from the city and the people of the city,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re small businesses. Come out and support us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230513ej5d001ba\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\cl